text_mt:
For the guise [of sacrifice] I take both of you;
for the rite I take both of you;
for the good deed I take both of you.
O divine waters, moving at the front, leading at the front, go forth at the front of this sacrifice!
Lead the sacrifice to the front, the lord of sacrifice to the front!
You did Indra choose in the contest against Vṛtra;
you chose Indra in the contest against Vṛtra.
You are sprinkled.
May the divine folk settle down together!
You belong to the tree;
you are grown by rain.
Go through the wide atmosphere!
Burnt up is the rakṣas!
Burnt up is the enemy!
You are the yoke-pin;
harm the one who harms, whoever harms us;
whom we harm, harm him!

Burned down is the rakṣas!
Burned down is the enemy!
Do not burn the ocean!
You are fixed;
firm the earth!
O Agni, strike away the flesh-devouring fire;
drive out the feeder on raw flesh!
O Agni, bear the place of sacrifice to the gods!
You are a support;
firm the atmosphere!
You are a supporter;
firm the sky!
You are order;
firm all things!
You are an arrangement;
you are an encircling arrangement;
sit in all the quarters!
Make the kin attend upon this sacrificer;
may the kin attend upon this sacrificer!
Through the heat of the cauldron of the Vasus, Rudras, Ādityas, Bhṛgus, and Aṅgirases, be heated!
The potsherds that the priests arrange at the cauldron,
those, according to Pūṣan's law, let Indra and Vāyu unyoke.

Scorched is the rakṣas!
Scorched is the enemy!
Do not wipe away life [and] breath!
Do not wipe away eye [and] ear!
Do not wipe away voice [and] cattle!
Do not wipe away sacrifice [and] offspring!
You are splendor;
you are brightness;
you are light;
you are nectar;
you belong to all the gods.
You are an offering to Vaiśvānara, of uplifted strength, possessing true power;
you are force;
overcome the enemy, overcome the assailants!
You are thousand-strengthened;
quicken that in me!
You are the clarified butter of clarified butter, the oblation of the oblation, the truth of truth;
besprinkled with truth,
with truth I sprinkle you.
With an undeceived eye I look upon you, for increase of wealth, for good offspring.
You are the dear abode of the gods, the unassailable place of sacrifice;
for the feast of the gods I receive you.
O divine waters, clean, unite with the gods!
Clean, well-attended, may we become your attendants!
You are a black [antelope], abiding in the ditch;
become clarified butter for Agni!
You are the vedi;
acceptable to the barhis I sprinkle you.
You are the barhis;
acceptable to the vedi I sprinkle you.
Svāhā to the Ancestors who drink the cauldron!

May Pūṣan untie your knot!
You are the topknot of Viṣṇu.
Spread wide, soft as wool, a good seat for the gods!
You are the gandharva Viśvāvasu, fleeing from everyone;
the encircling stick of the sacrificer, praised with the milk-offering.
You are Indra's right arm;
the encircling stick of the sacrificer, praised with the milk-offering.
May Mitra and Varuṇa enclose you from the north;
you are the encircling stick of the sacrificer, praised with the milk-offering.
As our constant hotṛ, O wise poet, may we, shining, kindle you!
On the highest summit of the earth, may Sūrya protect you with his rays in front against any curse!
You two are the supports of all people.
You are the seat of the Vasus, Rudras, and Ādityas, the womb of the ladles.
You are heaven by birth, Juhū by name, dear to the gods by a dear name;
sit in the firm seat!
You are atmosphere by birth, Upabhṛt by name, dear to the gods by a dear name;
sit in the firm seat!
You are earth by birth, Dhruvā by name, dear to the gods by a dear name;
sit in the firm seat!
You are the bull, mighty in chant;
I set you in the measure of the vaṣaṭ-call.
They have sat firm in the womb of cosmic order, in the world of good deeds;
guard them, O Viṣṇu!
Guard the sacrifice!
Guard the lord of sacrifice!
Guard me, the leader of the sacrifice!
You are Viṣṇu's;
you are abodes belonging to Viṣṇu, belonging to Prajāpati.

O divine waters, purifying, sweet-laden, cleanse me for the sacrifice to the gods!
O plant, protect this one! O razor, do not harm him!
With that body of yours which is most auspicious, touch him!
I shave these [hairs], listening to the god;
may I attain well-being beyond!
Golden-hued, clean, purifying, the waters have flowed, leaving behind defilement;
a hundred strainers are spread out in them; with these may god Savitṛ purify me!
May the waters, the mothers, refresh me; may the strainers of clarified butter purify me with clarified butter;
for may the divine [waters] carry off all stain; pure, cleansed, I emerge from them!
You are the shelter of Viṣṇu;
grant me shelter!
For vigor [I take] you!
You are the milk of the great ones, the sap of the waters and plants;
you are the bestower of luster;
bestow luster on me!
You are the pupil of Vṛtra's [eye], the bestower of vital strength on my sight;
through you I place between myself and hatred, between myself and enemies, a great mountain;
you are the guardian of sight;
protect my sight!
May the lord of thought purify you!
May the lord of speech purify you!
May the divine Savitṛ purify you with an unbroken strainer, with the rays of the good Sūrya!
O lord of the strainer, with that strainer of yours for whatever purpose I purify myself,
may I accomplish that!

To intention, to the yoker, to Agni, svāhā!
To insight, to the mind, to Agni, svāhā!
To consecration, to ascetic toil, to Agni, svāhā!
To Sarasvatī, to Pūṣan, to Agni, svāhā!
O divine waters, lofty, all-beneficent, heaven and earth, wide atmosphere,
may Bṛhaspati increase us with the oblation, svāhā!
Every mortal would choose the friendship of the divine guide;
everyone begs for wealth; he would choose splendor to flourish, svāhā!
You two are the works of art of the Ṛgveda and Sāmaveda;
I take hold of you both;
protect me until the end!
You are the shelter of Viṣṇu;
grant me shelter;
homage be to you;
do not harm me!
Sharpen this vision of the one striving, O god, [sharpen his] determination and skill, O Varuṇa;
by which may we cross over all evils; may I embark upon the boat that brings safe crossing!
O Sūrya and Agni, heaven and earth, wide atmosphere, waters, plants,
O lords of consecration, summon me to consecration!
O divine Savitṛ, you are the lord of consecration of consecration;
protect me as I am thus!
You are vigor, of the Aṅgirases, soft as wool;
place vigor in me!
You are the womb of Indra;
protect me;
homage be to you;
do not harm me!
I plow up agriculture yielding good grain; make the plants rich in fruit!
O horn, untie this knot which is tangled in his heart,
the mind of his which is tangled!
You are great, of the trees;
rich in splendor, bestow splendor upon the sacrificer;
protect me from the sight of the constellations!
We ask of you, O gods, what is desirable as the sacrificial rite proceeds;
for in your invocation, O gods worthy of sacrifice, we call upon you.
Svāhā, from the mind the sacrifice,
svāhā, from heaven,
svāhā, from the earth,
svāhā, from the wide atmosphere,
svāhā, from the wind I seize [it], svāhā!

May I not be parted from the presence of the constellations!
Perform the vow!
We contemplate the divine vision, very gracious for our assistance,
bestowing luster, conveying the sacrifice; may it be easily approachable under our control!
Those gods born of Manu, yoked by the mind, of good skill, whose father is Dakṣa, may they help us, may they protect us!
To them, svāhā!
Auspicious, when drunk, be you, O waters, very friendly in our womb, in our belly;
providing refreshment, free from disease, guiltless, auspicious, be you for our life!
O desire of the oblations, most gladdening, O Agni, do you keep good watch; may we rejoice well!
Protect us for welfare; awaken us again!
Again mind, again life has come to us; again breath, again intention has come;
may Vaiśvānara, undeceived, protector of the body, ward off all evils!
You, O Agni, are the protector of vows, a god among mortals;
you are to be praised at sacrifices; maintain the vow in us!
Pūṣan [is the lord] of acquisitions;
Soma [is the lord] of bounties;
may the one who fulfills not be deprived of fulfillment,
nor we of life and of luster!
Give so much, O Soma;
bring yet more!
God Savitṛ is the giver of wealth from the good;
Vāyu is the guardian;
Tvaṣṭṛ is the overlord;
Pūṣan is the receiver.
O divine waters, O child of the waters, that wave of yours which is fit for oblation, full of strength, most intoxicating, may I not step upon it!
Those voices which are in the bull of the cattle—Sūrya was foremost, Śukra was foremost—those I send forth according to your portion here;
may the voices return auspicious to us!
For Vāyu, you!
For Varuṇa, you!
For Rudra, you!
For Nirṛti, you!
For Indra, you!
For the Maruts, you!

This is your bright body, this is your luster; unite with it;
attain brilliance!
You are impetuous, held by the mind, pleasing to Viṣṇu;
in the impulsion of you whose power is true, may I attain the harness of the body, svāhā!
You are bright;
you are shining;
you are nectar;
you belong to all the gods;
you are thought;
you are zeal;
you are vision;
you are the sacrificial fee;
you are worthy of sacrifice;
you belong to the ruling power;
you are Aditi, facing both ways;
as such, be well-facing-forward, well-facing-backward to us!
May Mitra bind you by the foot!
May Pūṣan protect the paths!
For Indra, the overseer!
May your mother consent to you, may your father [consent],
may your brother of the same womb [consent],
may your companion of the same herd [consent]!
As such, O goddess, go to the god;
to Indra, [bring] Soma!
May Rudra turn you back;
safely, with Soma as your companion, come again!
You are a Vasu;
you are a Rudra;
you are Aditi;
you are an Āditya;
you are shining;
you are a Rudra;
may Bṛhaspati delight you in favor!
Rudra has taken notice [of you] together with the Vasus.
Upon the head of the earth I sprinkle you, worthy of sacrifice, in the footprint of Iḍā, full of clarified butter, svāhā!
Delight among us!
In us be your riches;
yours be the riches;
yours, yours be the riches;
may we not be separated from the flourishing of wealth!
Accompanied by Tvaṣṭṛ, may we attend upon you!

You are self-born;
you are self-become;
[you are] born for our ritual action;
by truth I grasp you;
by truth protect us!
Come to us, O Mitra, bestower of good friends, together with increase of wealth!
Enter the right thigh of Indra, willing, into willing, gracious into gracious!
Upward with life, with good life, upward with the sap of the plants,
upward from the realm of Parjanya, I have arisen following immortality!
Traverse the wide atmosphere!
Sit on the seat of Aditi!
The bull propped heaven; he measured out the wide space of the atmosphere, the expanse of the earth;
he sat down as universal ruler over all beings; all these are the vows of Varuṇa.
He spread the atmosphere out among the trees, swift vigor in racers, milk in inviolable [cows],
wisdom in hearts, Varuṇa [placed] fire in the directions, the sun in heaven; he placed Soma on the rock.
You are the yoke-pin;
injure the injurer who would injure us!
Whom we would injure, injure him!
You belong to Varuṇa;
may Varuṇa prop you up!
You are the prop of Varuṇa!
Move forward, O lord of the world, toward all domains!
May no thieves, may no waylayers, may no evil wolves find you!
Becoming a falcon, fly away
to the house of our sacrificer, consecrated
for the pressing for the gods!
Homage to the eye of Mitra and Varuṇa! Serve that truth for the great god!
Praise the sun, far-seeing, born of the gods, the banner, the son of heaven!
You belong to Varuṇa;
may Varuṇa prop you up!
You are the releasing of Varuṇa’s prop!
Untied is the fetter of Varuṇa;
flung away is the fetter of Varuṇa;
homage to the fetter of Varuṇa!
You are the body of Agni;
for Viṣṇu [I take] you!
You are the body of Soma;
for Viṣṇu [I take] you!
You are the hospitality of the guest;
for Viṣṇu [I take] you!
For Agni the giver of increase of wealth,
for Viṣṇu [I take] you!
For the falcon who brought Soma,
for Viṣṇu [I take] you!
You belong to Varuṇa;
you are Varuṇa whose vow is firm;
sit on the seat of truth of Varuṇa;
for Varuṇa [I take] you!

You are the birthplace of Agni;
you two are the two bulls;
you are Urvaśī;
you are Āyu;
you are Purūravas;
you are the Gāyatrī;
you are the Triṣṭubh;
you are the Jagatī!
Be of one mind, of the same dwelling, like-minded, spotless toward us!
Do not harm the sacrifice, do not harm the lord of the sacrifice, O Jātavedases;
be auspicious to us today!
Fire enters, moving within fire, the son of the ṛṣis, this sovereign ruler.
Let us serve him with an oblation; may we not disturb the share of the gods!
For the lord of falling upon, I take you;
for the lord of flying around, I take you;
for the son of the body, capable, mighty, most powerful through ability!
Unassailable are you, unassailable might of the gods, protecting against accusation, not to be accused!
May the lord of vows approve this vow of mine; may the lord of consecration approve the consecration!
I have straightway reached the truth;
set me upon prosperity!
O Agni, lord of vows, whatever body is mine, that is in you;
O Agni, lord of vows, whatever body is yours, this is in me;
together, O lord of vows, are the vows of us two vow-holders!
May stalk after stalk swell for you, O divine Soma, for Indra who wins the single prize!
Swell for Indra!
May Indra swell for you!
Make your comrades swell with acquisition, with wisdom!
Safely, O divine Soma, may I reach your pressing, safely the conclusion!
Sought is wealth, sought are lovely things, forth for vigor, for Bhaga;
truth to those who speak truth!
Homage to heaven,
homage to earth!
O Agni, that body of yours which lies in metal, the highest, dwelling in the depth,
you have struck down vehement speech, you have struck down fierce speech, svāhā!
That [body] of yours, O Agni, which lies in dust;
that [body] of yours, O Agni, which lies in heat;
that body of yours, O Agni, which belongs to Rudra—protect us with it; to that of yours, svāhā!

You are my course of austerity;
you are my course of finding [wealth].
May it protect me, the supplicant;
may it protect [me], the afflicted.
O Agni Aṅgiras, who are upon this earth, come with the name of life!
May the Vasus bring you with the Gāyatrī metre;
find for the lord of the sacrifice!
With that unassailable, unyielding domain of yours, I establish you.
O Agni Aṅgiras, who are upon the second earth, come with the name of life!
May the Rudras bring you with the Traiṣṭubha metre;
find for the lord of the sacrifice!
With that unassailable, unyielding domain of yours, I establish you.
O Agni Aṅgiras, who are upon the third earth, come with the name of life!
May the Ādityas bring you with the Jāgata metre;
find for the lord of the sacrifice!
With that unassailable, unyielding domain of yours, I establish you.
May you find, O Agni, that cloud-name which is yours.
You are a lioness;
you are a buffalo cow;
purify yourself for the gods;
adorn yourself for the gods!
May the Indraghoṣas protect you from the east with the Vasus;
may the swift-minded fathers protect you from the south;
may the wise Rudras protect you from the west;
may Viśvakarman protect you from the north with the Ādityas!
You are a lioness, overcoming rivals, svāhā!
You are a lioness, winning increase of wealth, svāhā!
You are a lioness, winning good offspring, svāhā!
You are a lioness, winning the Ādityas, winning kinfolk, svāhā!
You are a lioness!
Bring hither the gods for the sacrificer who serves the gods, svāhā!
To the beings, [I offer] you!
You possess all life;
make the earth firm!
You are a firm dwelling;
make the atmosphere firm!
You are an unshakeable dweller;
make the heaven firm!
You are the ashes of Agni;
you are the rubble of Agni;
you are the nest of Agni!
May the gleaming, high one drink the sweet honey of Soma, bestowing unswerving life upon the lord of the sacrifice;
he who, urged by the wind, guards by his own nature, delivers the offspring, and shines forth in manifold ways.

You are taken with a support.
You are bay, harnesser of the bays, possessor of bays, standing on the two bays;
of you whose praise is sung, O divine Soma, whose chant is recited, whose Yajus is offered,
of the possessor of bays, of the harnesser of the bays, may I obtain the cup with bays!
Roasted barley of the two bays, rich in bays, accompanied by Soma, for Indra;
for wealth, you;
for prosperity, you!

May Agni from the morning pressing protect us, Vaiśvānara, sharing all splendor, bringing well-being to all;
may he, the purifier, bestow wealth upon us; possessing a full life-span, may we share the feast!
For Agni I pluck you out with the Gāyatrī metre;
for Indra I pluck you out with the Triṣṭubh metre;
for the Ādityas I pluck you out with the Jagatī metre;
in the flight of the roaring ones I shake you;
in the flight of the exhilarated ones I shake you;
in the flight of the rejoicing ones I shake you;
in the flight of the purified ones I shake you;
in the flight of the homestead-dwellers I shake you;
in the flight of the honeyed ones I shake you;
in the flight of the sweet-flowing ones I shake you;
in the flight of the god-traversed ones I shake you.
You are taken with a support.
You, bright, O Śukra, for Śukra I take, in the form of day, in the rays of the sun.
They have shaken from the ocean; the streams of heaven have not ceased.
The lofty form of the bull shines on high,
in the thought of the Vasus, in the work of the Rudras, in the mind of the Ādityas;
for Indra possessing extensive wealth I offer you.
Desiring, O divine Soma, with the Gāyatrī metre approach the domain of Agni;
controlling, O divine Soma, with the Triṣṭubh metre approach the domain of Indra;
as our friend, O divine Soma, with the Jagatī metre approach the dear path of the All-Gods!

Upward carry the banners that god who knows all beings,
Sūrya, for all to see.
The bright face of the gods has arisen, the eye of Mitra, of Varuṇa, of Agni;
he has filled heaven and earth and the atmosphere, Sūrya, the self of the moving and the standing.
Go to heaven!
Go to the sun!
I approach your form with form, [your] vigor with vigor;
may Tutha, all-knowing, distribute you on the highest firmament of the earth!
This gift of yours, O Agni, goes forth, dropped from Soma;
lead it along the path of Mitra!
May this Agni make broad space for us; may he go forward, cleaving the assailants;
may he conquer the enemies, exulting; may he win the prize in the contest for prizes!
Go forth along the path of sacred order, having shining sacrificial gifts;
today may I gratify a Brahmin who has ancestors, of ancestral lineage, a seer descended from seers, having goodly sacrificial gifts!
Look at the sun! Look at the atmosphere!
Strive together with those sitting in the Sadas!
Given by us, full of sweetness, go to the gods!
Enter the giver!
Without forsaking us, by the path traversed by gods, sit in the world of the righteous;
that has been prepared for us!

'You are real; may I become real!'—
these are indeed blessings;
them he secures.
'You are full; may I become full!'—
full with limbs, indeed, he comes into being yonder.
'You are complete; may I become complete!'—
complete with limbs, indeed, he comes into being yonder.
'You are imperishable; may I become imperishable!'—
imperishable, indeed, he comes into being yonder.
'May the divine officiating priests cleanse [the sacrifice] through the eastern quarter!'—
these, indeed, are the cleansing rites of the sacrifice;
these are the propitiations.
The Baijavāpas knew them;
their sacrifice became cleansed, propitiated, [and] Paśupati did not slay their cattle.
Therefore, he who knows thus—his sacrifice becomes cleansed, propitiated, and Paśupati does not slay his cattle.
'Viṣṇu strode across the earth with the gāyatrī metre,' [he says].
With Viṣṇu at their head, indeed, the gods, having driven the asuras from these worlds, went to the heavenly world.
So the sacrificer, with Viṣṇu at his head, having driven his rival from these worlds, goes to the heavenly world.
'We have gone to the light,' [he says];
he goes to the heavenly world itself.
'We have become united with the light,' [he says];
for the heavenly world is light.
'Here do I wrap up the breath of so-and-so,' [he says];
he wraps up his breath itself.
He turns around in this manner;
for in this manner does the sacrifice turn around.
Moreover, he turns around following the course of that sun.
'You are splendour,' he says;
for Agni is splendour.
He, indeed, having left behind both offspring and cattle, goes to heaven.
When he says:
'May I be united with offspring, offspring with me; may I be united with cattle, cattle with me,'
he establishes himself firmly in offspring and in cattle.
'O Agni, lord of the house, may I, through you as lord of the house, be a good householder;
may you, through me as lord of the house, be a good householder,' [he says];
they grow old together without mutual seizure;
they attain the full life-span;
they do not incur distress.
He speaks the name of his son;
he continues the line of offspring itself.
He turns toward the human;
for he speaks the name of a human.
When he says:
'Unbroken is the divine thread; let not the human thread be broken,'
he joins together both the divine and the human.
'May I not be cut off from the divine domain, nor from the human,' [he says];
both conquer this world;
they are together in the heavenly world.

[Saying] thus,
having crossed over those gods who are sacrifice-slayers and sacrifice-stealers on the earth, he ascended to the intermediate space;
having crossed over those gods who are sacrifice-slayers and sacrifice-stealers in the intermediate space, he went to heaven;
having crossed over those gods who are sacrifice-slayers and sacrifice-stealers in heaven, he established him in preeminence among his kin.
The sacrificial cake is indeed the head of the sacrifice, the veda [grass-bundle] is the hair.
When he sweeps the sacrificial cake with the veda,
[it is] for the completeness of the sacrifice,
and also for its sacrificial fitness.
The sacrifice, indeed, concealed itself from the gods;
the gods found it by means of the veda.
That is the veda-nature of the veda.
When he sits on the altar with the veda,
he finds the sacrifice for him.
He gives the veda to the wife;
hard to find, indeed, is the sacrifice yonder;
he finds the sacrifice for him.
He gives it three times;
for the gods have a threefold truth.
It is placed in her lap;
she becomes prone to bear a male.
The veda is cattle;
for it is plants.
This space between the two fires is indeed the world of cattle;
in his own world the sacrificer deprives his rival of cattle.
Half-month by half-month the sacrifice is interrupted.
He goes strewing continuously from the Āhavanīya fire,
for the continuity of the sacrifice.
To it, thus made continuous, he sacrifices in the following half-month.
'Possessing cows, O Agni, possessing sheep, possessing horses is the sacrifice,' [he says];
he has made the sacrifice rich in cattle, rich in sheep, rich in horses.
'Rich in food is this, O Asura, rich in offspring, long-lasting wealth, broad-based, possessing an assembly,' [he says];
he asks for a blessing.
'May the wife go together with the husband among the well-doers,' [he says];
this is the wife's holding on to the sacrifice;
they are together in the heavenly world.
She who is his wife sits behind facing directly toward him;
if she were to sit behind facing directly toward [the fire], it would burn up her offspring.
When he says:
'O wife, this is your place,'
he makes a place for her;
it does not burn up her offspring.
'Which Sarasvatī is the house-gatherer,' [he says];
[she is] the house-gatherer;
he grants him settlements thereby.
Moreover, his kin, subsisting [on him], wait upon him, [saying]:
'Let us wait upon the voice of this brāhmaṇa or rājanya.'
They should not cook meat on that fire;
if they were to cook, they would make it a corpse-eater;
for they do not cook meat on that fire in which they offer oblations.
'You are unassailable, O Agni, and blameless,' [he says];
'unassailable' is indeed the dear body of Agni.
Indeed, they say: 'by the steadfastness of the custom-guardian.'
Both what is done here in the sacrifice and what is not,
and what expiation of the sacrifice we know here and what we do not,
of both this is the expiation.

The theologians say:
'They sprinkle the kindling; they sprinkle the oblation.
The kindling is the first of the oblations.
Why are there offering and invitatory verses for the other oblations,
[and] why not for the kindling?'
'Recite for Agni as he is being kindled!'—
[this is] the invitatory verse; the sāmidhenī verses are the offering verse; the invitation is the vaṣaṭ-call.
Where too much is done at the sacrifice, there too much is produced in the sacrificer's self;
if [it is] not attained, the sacrifice is cut off, [and] the sacrificer becomes hungry.
With the concluding verse of the sāmidhenī verses he completes the last [log] of the kindling;
the sacrificer does not become hungry;
too much is not produced in his self.
He who knows Prajāpati as seventeenfold, connected with the sacrifice, his sacrifice does not falter; he is established in Prajāpati through the sacrifice.
'Make [him] hear!'—
four syllables;
'Let it be heard!'—
four syllables;
'We who sacrifice'—
five syllables;
the vaṣaṭ-call has two syllables;
this indeed is Prajāpati, the seventeenfold, connected with the sacrifice.
Whether he exceeds the invitatory verse or the offering verse, if he does not depart from this, his sacrifice does not falter; he is established in Prajāpati through the sacrifice.
'Indeed we do not know whether we are Brahmins or non-Brahmins, whether we are of this ṛṣi or of another, whose [name] we pronounce;
but whoever pronouncing [the ancestry] sacrifices, to him that sacrifice comes;
it does not fall to another.'
Therefore, when the pravara is being chosen, he should say:
'O divine fathers, O fatherly gods,
whoever I am, being he, I sacrifice;
whoever I am, being he, I act;
may my sacrifice be prosperous, prosperous [and] appeased, prosperous [and] accomplished!'
Then whoever he is, being he, he sacrifices; to him that sacrifice comes;
it does not fall to another.
'With the measure, the counter-measure, the perimeter-measure, the upward-measure of the sacrifice I encompass you'—
the Gāyatrī is indeed the measure of the sacrifice,
the Triṣṭubh the counter-measure,
the Jagatī the perimeter-measure,
the Anuṣṭubh the upward-measure;
these metres indeed carry the sacrifice;
with them he encompasses it.

The Gandharvas and Apsarases asked Keśin Dārbhya:
'How is it that you, a sacrificer, are equal to a sacrificer who is your rival?'
'I know,' he said.
They said:
'Come, explain the sacrifice to us!'
He explained the sacrifice to them.
They said:
'Even by this the sacrificer would be worse off than his rival sacrificer.'
They said:
'We will arrange the sacrifice for you in such a way that you, a sacrificer, will overcome the rival sacrificer.'
They arranged the oblations, the sacrifice, for him.
Then Keśin overcame Śaṇḍika Audbhāri.
He overcomes his rival through the sacrifice who knows thus.
The āghāra [libation] is the breath;
it is to be offered in the eastern half;
he places breath in his mouth.
The āghāra is the breath;
it is to be offered in the middle;
he places breath in his middle.
He should offer the āghāra as the most abundant of the oblations;
the āghāra is the breath;
he makes his breath the most abundant.
He should pour the āghāra continuously;
the āghāra is the breath;
for the continuity of breath.
He should pour the āghāra upward for one desiring heaven;
the āghāra is the sacrificer;
he makes the sacrificer go to the heavenly world.
For one whom he hates, he should pour it downward;
he becomes worse off.
One oblation steps forward;
one steps away;
one is established.
That which he offers stepping forward, that is stepping forward;
that which he offers stepping away, that is stepping away;
that which he offers standing in the same place, that is established.
For him whom he may wish, 'May he become further wealthier, better,' he should offer stepping forward;
thereby he becomes further wealthier, better.
Then for him whom he may wish, 'May he become further worse off,' he should offer stepping away;
thereby he becomes further worse off.
Then for him whom he may wish, 'May he be neither wealthier nor worse off,' he should offer standing in the same place;
thereby he becomes neither wealthier nor worse off.
The fore-offerings are the seasons;
they are to be offered in the same place;
for the firm establishment of the seasons.
That [portion] which is of Agni's ghee-portion is to be offered in the northern half;
that which is further north than it belongs to the Rakṣases;
that which is of Soma's ghee-portion is to be offered in the southern half;
that which is further south than it belongs to the fathers.
This is the space between the oblations;
arranged are his oblations offered in proper sequence, who knows thus.
He who offers in the smoke offers it in darkness;
then the sacrificer becomes unpleasing to look at.
That which he offers on the coals is a blind oblation;
then the eye of the sacrificer is prone to perish.
Both are to be offered in what possesses light;
the sacrificer becomes pleasing to look at;
his eye does not perish.
That which a non-Brahmin eats is a spilled oblation;
of that Vasiṣṭha alone knew the expiation.
'Protect, O bright one!'—with this he should touch the sacrificial cake;
'Let the shareholder have his share!
May the shareless not have a share!
This is the oblation of the Brahmins, Soma-worthy, Soma-drinkers;
there is no part here for a non-Brahmin.
For me, acting, may it not diminish;
for me, giving, may it not fail!'
He establishes the sacrifice among those worthy of sacrificial gifts, unspilled, undisturbed;
and even that which a non-Brahmin eats becomes offered for him.

One intending to undertake the new- and full-moon sacrifices should pour out [a cake] on eleven potsherds for Agni and Viṣṇu.
Agni is all the deities;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice.
Having taken hold of the deities and the sacrifice, he undertakes the new- and full-moon sacrifices.
Some also offer the Jaya [oblations].
The gods and the Asuras contended.
Prajāpati saw these Jaya [oblations];
he gave them to Indra.
With them he conquered the Asuras firm step by firm step.
Firm step by firm step he conquers his rival for whom these are offered.
'Intention and impulse,' [he says];
intention is the sacrifice;
impulse is the sacrificial gift.
'Thought and thinking,' [he says];
mind is thought;
speech is thinking.
'Contemplation and study,' [he says];
offspring is contemplation;
cattle is study.
'Known and knowledge,' [he says];
the ṛc is the known;
the sāman is knowledge.
'Bhaga and insight,' [he says];
Prajāpati is Bhaga;
the sacrifice is insight.
'New moon and full moon,' [he says];
[they are] the new- and full-moon [sacrifices] themselves.
That makes twelve;
twelve months are the year;
having reached the year, he secures it.
'Prajāpati gave the Jaya [oblations] to mighty Indra, fierce, victorious in battles.
Through them, racing for the prize, may we win the prize; through them may we overcome all armies'—[with this verse]
he should offer the thirteenth oblation.
There is a thirteenth month;
having reached it with this [oblation], he secures it.
'O Agni, giver of strength, give force and vigor to me as I stride forward, who work curses [against enemies] and am free from curse;
for pre-eminence in this community, svāhā!'—with this he should sacrifice when he desires, 'May I be distinguished in this community.'
Distinguished indeed he becomes in that community, but a variegated mark is born on his body.

You, O Agni, have the splendor of the sun;
unite me with lifespan, with splendor.
You, O Agni, have united with the light of the sun,
with the praise of the ṛṣis, with the dear abode.
May I unite with lifespan, with splendor, with offspring, with the prosperity of wealth.
Kindling you for a hundred winters, radiant, may we kindle you,
possessed of vitality, [you] who grant vitality, possessed of power, [you] who grant power,
O Agni, crusher of rivals, having good heroes, [you] the unassailable.
You are the kindling stick of Agni;
protect me from slander.
You are the kindling stick of Soma;
be a supreme protector to me.
You are the kindling stick of Yama;
protect me from death.
You are the placer of lifespan, O Agni;
place lifespan in me.
You are the placer of splendor, O Agni;
place splendor in me.
You are the protector of sight, O Agni;
protect my sight.
You are the protector of hearing, O Agni;
protect my hearing.
You are the protector of the body, O Agni;
protect my body.
Whatever of my body is deficient, O Agni, fill that up for me.
O Agni, with whatever heat you have, burn against him who hates us and whom we hate.
O Agni, with whatever glowing flame you have, glow against him who hates us and whom we hate.
O Agni, with whatever radiant beam you have, beam against him who hates us and whom we hate.
O Agni, with whatever fiery force you have, strike against him who hates us and whom we hate.
O Agni, with whatever brilliance you have, sharpen against him who hates us and whom we hate.
O Agni, lord of radiant lusters, homage to your radiant luster;
place radiant luster in me.
O you whose wealth is wondrous, may I attain your far shore in safety.
O you whose wealth is hither, may I attain your far shore in safety.
You are waters;
may I enjoy your waters.
You are greatness;
may I enjoy your greatness.
You are vigor;
may I enjoy your vigor.
You are prosperity of wealth;
may I enjoy your prosperity of wealth.
O wealthy ones, rest in this womb, in this cattle-pen;
this is your relative;
do not go away from here;
become many;
do not abandon me.
You are united, all-shaped;
enter me with vigor, with lordship over cattle, with offspring, with prosperity of wealth.
May your riches rest in me;
may I attain a thousandfold prosperity of you.

Great be the celestial aid of the three, Mitra and Aryaman, / unassailable [that] of Varuṇa.
For when they are at home, or on travels, or in perils, / no deceitful, malicious slanderer has power over them.
For they, the sons of Aditi, bestow shelter continuously, / excellent things to the worshipper.
Make, O Brahmaṇaspati, the Soma-presser famed, / [like] Kakṣīvant, who is the son of Uśij.
He who is wealthy, who slays disease, finds wealth, increases nourishment, / may he, who is auspicious, attend us.
Of Mitra, the sustainer of peoples, the god, the fame is winning,
splendor most wondrous in renown.
You are never barren;
you never become negligent.
May your unassailable chariot encompass us from every side, / with which you protect the worshippers.
You are the crusher;
may I crush down him who hates us and whom we hate.
You are the overcomer;
may I overcome him who hates us and whom we hate.
You are the preeminent;
may I excel and surpass him who hates us and whom we hate.
May Pūṣan, protector of the path, protect me;
may Pūṣan, protector of cattle, protect me;
may Pūṣan, overlord, protect me.
The eastern quarter has Agni as deity;
whoever attacks me from this quarter, may he incur Agni.
The southern quarter has Indra as deity;
whoever attacks me from this quarter, may he incur Indra.
The western quarter has the Maruts as deity;
whoever attacks me from this quarter, may he incur the Maruts.
The northern quarter has Mitrā-Varuṇā as deities;
whoever attacks me from this quarter, may he incur Mitrā-Varuṇā.
The upward quarter has Soma as deity;
whoever attacks me from this quarter, may he incur Soma.
May order protect me from unrighteousness;
may dispensation protect me from violation.
Lifespan and extended life, and sight and broad sight, and forward-breath and outward-breath.
O Uruka, by the speech of you, Uruka, may we attain shared food, O Agni, lord of the house.

For whomsoever in the Agnihotra the stoma is yoked, heaven becomes his.
It is indeed a non-sacrifice where the stoma is not yoked.
[With] "Approaching the sacrifice":
this [earth] indeed is approaching (úpotis);
from here indeed he yokes the Soma;
moreover, he joins this [earth] to the stoma;
moreover, whatever creatures have come to be having names, them indeed he joins to the stoma.
[With] "Following his ancient gleam":
yonder world indeed is the ancient;
from yonder indeed he yokes the stoma;
moreover, the gods indeed are the ancient;
them indeed he joins to the stoma.
From both sides indeed he yokes the stoma, both from here and from yonder.
Having yoked the gods, he goes to the heavenly world.
When he says:
"Near, near, now, O generous one, more, now, those":
this [earth] indeed is approaching;
upon this [earth] indeed he becomes established.
Then, when he utters a verse containing *upa*:
whatever creatures are about to come to be, them indeed he joins to the stoma.
[With] "May your unassailable chariot":
from both sides indeed with this [verse] he encompasses the stoma yoked, both from here and from yonder.
[With] "Agni is the head":
heavenly thereby [it becomes];
[with] "The summit of heaven":
heavenly thereby [it becomes];
[with] "This lord of the earth":
a couple thereby [it becomes];
[with] "He quickens the seed of the waters":
it is full of seed, bringing cattle, completely prosperous.
He stands by with a gāyatrī [verse];
for Agni belongs to the Gāyatrī, having the gāyatrī metre;
with his own metre indeed he stands by him.
[With] "Both of you, Indra and Agni, are to be invoked":
for both of these [go] together;
for this [Agni] enters yonder [sun] when it has become day;
therefore yonder one shines by day;
yonder one enters this [earth/Agni] by night;
therefore this one [shines] by night.
When he says, "Both of you":
he stands by both of them without interruption;
he shines in both worlds, in this one and in yonder.
He stands by with a triṣṭubh [verse].
[With] "He was established here first by the creators":
for Agni was established first upon this [earth];
[with] "The Hotṛ, most worthy of sacrifice, to be praised at the rites":
for this one is the Hotṛ, most worthy of sacrifice, to be praised at the rites;
[with] "Whom Apnavāna and the Bhṛgus made shine forth":
for Apnavāna and the Bhṛgus made him shine forth;
[with] "In the woods, wondrous, pervasive for every clan":
for he pervades all this.
He stands by with a jagatī [verse].
[With] "Following his ancient gleam":
the heavenly world indeed is the ancient;
he becomes established in the heavenly world.
[With] "This is your seasonal womb":
for this is his seasonal womb,
Agni [is the womb] of the sun.
He stands by with an anuṣṭubh [verse].

[With] "Approaching the sacrifice",
he sows [seed] indeed with this [verse].
[With] "Agni is the head",
in that which has been sown he places the seed with this [verse].
[With] "Both of you, Indra and Agni, are to be invoked":
Indra and Agni indeed are the in-breath and out-breath;
the in-breath and out-breath indeed are thus placed at the head of the sacrifice.
[With] "He was established here first by the creators",
he placed the embryo indeed.
[With] "Following his ancient gleam",
he made the udder indeed.
[With] "This is your seasonal womb",
he caused [it] to be born and he made [it] grow.
He stands by with six [verses];
six indeed are the pṛṣṭhas;
he arranges the pṛṣṭhas indeed.
[With] "I have praised Dadhikrāvan", he stands by with [a verse] addressed to Dadhikrā;
this indeed is Agni's dear body addressed to Dadhikrā, bringing cattle, completely prosperous.
Unto Agni's dear abode indeed he approaches with this [verse];
moreover, he becomes rich in cattle;
moreover, the sacrificer purifies himself with this [verse].
He stands by with seven [verses];
seven-footed is the Śakvarī;
cattle are of the Śakvarī;
he secures cattle indeed.
When established, this one ages indeed;
for Agni is a beast;
therefore one should offer these oblations of the fire-establishment year by year;
thereby he does not age;
thereby he makes him renewed.
That is not to be heeded.
He is to be stood by with these very [verses] addressed to Agni Pavamāna, which are the offering verse and invitatory verse of the fire-establishment;
thereby he does not age;
thereby he makes him renewed.
He stands by with twelve [verses];
twelve months are a year;
having attained the year, he secures it.
With [a verse] addressed to Agni and Soma he is to be stood by as the thirteenth;
there is a thirteenth month;
having attained it with this [verse], he secures it.

The theologians say:
"Why do they attend upon the fire in the evening, [and] why not in the morning?"
Yonder sun indeed sets in motion in the evening;
therefore they attend upon [it] in the evening.
This one sets in motion in the morning;
therefore they do not attend upon [it] in the morning.
Therefore in the evening, when the Agnihotra is unoffered, the Agnihotrin must not eat;
and therefore in the morning, when [it is] unoffered, he must not eat.
Therefore in the evening [people are] hostile to a guest;
but on account of merit they give in the morning.
With the morning hand-washing [the fire] is to be attended upon in the morning.
While [the milk] is put on [the fire] or while it is being drawn up, he should wash his hands;
there he should speak four verses of the Vihavya [hymn], [and] four at the morning hand-washing.
By the morning hand-washing, truly, he reaches what has not been reached, he secures what has not been secured;
what has not been reached he indeed reaches by this, what has not been secured he secures.
This [fire], truly, was afraid when brought forth;
the gods surrounded it with the metres.
When he attends upon [it],
he surrounds it with the metres indeed.
He has done both for him.
With a verse addressed to Agni and Soma [the fire] is to be attended upon during the first half-month;
the first half-month, truly, belongs to Agni and Soma;
he hands it over to the second half-month indeed.
With a verse addressed to Indra and Agni it is to be attended upon during the second half-month;
the second half-month, truly, belongs to Indra and Agni;
he hands it over to the first half-month indeed.
All the deities indeed, having passed [him] along, protect him without looking away, who, knowing thus, attends upon the fire.

[With] "You, O Agni, are sun-splendored", he prays for a blessing for greater excellence, for greater welfare.
[With] "Unite me with life, with splendor", he prays for a blessing for himself.
[With] "You, O Agni, have joined with the light of the sun":
for together at that time do these two lights become.
[With] "With the praise of the ṛṣis":
the metres indeed are the praise of the ṛṣis;
he prospers it with the metres indeed.
[With] "With [your] dear abode":
the oblations indeed are the dear abode of Agni;
he prospers it with the oblations indeed.
[With] "May I be joined with life, with splendor, with offspring, with increase of wealth":
he prays for a blessing indeed.
[With] "Kindling you for a hundred winters":
for the invocation is an army-conquering one;
with it a noble should attend upon [the fire];
for when a noble conquers armies, then he prospers;
and one who is not a noble may attend upon [it];
for everyone wishes to conquer armies, everyone wishes to thrive.
Manu truly had ten wives:
having ten sons, having nine sons, having eight sons, having seven sons, having six sons, having five sons, having four sons, having three sons, having two sons, having one son.
Those who were nine, one went over to them;
those who were eight, two [went over] to them;
those who were seven, three [went over] to them;
those who were six, four [went over] to them;
then indeed five were just five.
These five sets of ten excluded these five
from whatever was Manu's own.
Therefore they ran up to Manu himself;
they sought refuge with Manu.
To them he gave these kindling-sticks;
with them they burned them out;
with them they defeated them.
He defeats his evil rival, who, knowing thus, puts on these kindling-sticks.
[With] "You are Agni's kindling-stick;
protect me from curse":
from curse he protects him.
[With] "You are Soma's kindling-stick;
be a supreme protector for me":
he becomes a supreme protector for him.
[With] "You are Yama's kindling-stick;
protect me from death":
from death he protects him.
This, indeed, Nārada used to say:
"This indeed was the attending upon the fire."
From curse he protects him;
he becomes a supreme protector for him;
from death he protects him.

[With] "You, O Agni, are the giver of life;
give life to me":
life indeed he places in him.
[With] "You, O Agni, are the giver of splendor;
give splendor to me":
splendor indeed he places in him.
[With] "You, O Agni, are the protector of sight;
protect my sight":
sight indeed he protects for him.
[With] "You, O Agni, are the protector of hearing;
protect my hearing":
hearing indeed he protects for him.
[With] "You, O Agni, are the protector of the body;
protect my body":
the body indeed he protects for him.
[With] "Whatever of my body is deficient, O Agni, fill that up for me":
whatever indeed of himself is deficient, whatever of offspring, whatever of cattle, that he fills up by this, that he causes to swell.
[With] "O Agni, what heat is yours":
these indeed are Agni's luminous bodies.
This, indeed, Aruṇa Aupaveśi used to say:
"Whichever rivals of myself who are of greater excellence, of greater welfare, I perceive, them let us defeat with these luminous bodies of Agni."
He defeats his evil rival, who, knowing thus, attends upon the fire.
[With] "O Agni, lord of lights, homage to your light;
place light in me":
light [already] pacified he places in himself; he becomes full of fiery energy, endowed with Brahmanical splendor.
[With] "O you whose wealth is wondrous, safely may I reach your other side":
night indeed is Citrāvasu (whose wealth is wondrous);
day is Arvāgvasu (whose wealth is hitherward);
Agni indeed is the night;
yonder sun is the day;
these two indeed are the two halves;
to them he prays.
He speaks thrice;
for the gods have threefold truth.
His sacrifice or the sacred word shines forth, who knows thus.
[With] "You are water;
may I partake of your water":
for they are water.
[With] "You are greatness;
may I partake of your greatness":
for they are greatness.
[With] "You are nourishment;
may I partake of your nourishment":
for they are nourishment.
[With] "You are increase of wealth;
may I partake of your increase of wealth":
for they are increase of wealth.
[With] "O wealthy ones, rest in this womb, in this cowpen":
in their own womb, in their own cowpen he settles them.
[With] "This is your kin;
do not go away from here;
become many;
do not abandon me":
he prays for a blessing.
He touches the calf;
for cattle are fond of the calf;
this one indeed rules over thousandfold increase;
he thrives by a thousand, he does not fall away from a thousand, who knows thus.
[With] "You are united, all-formed":
for with form after form she is united;
with forms indeed he prospers her.
He took their name;
he made friendship with them;
and indeed, when he takes the name of a friend, he makes him a friend thereby.

[With] "Great may the aid of the three be", he attends [upon the fire] with a triplet addressed to Prajāpati.
Belonging to Prajāpati indeed are these offspring;
them indeed he secures;
them he makes edible.
Then he approaches Prajāpati himself;
in Prajāpati alone among the deities does he firmly establish himself.
[With] "The soma-presser, the sounding one", he attends [upon the fire] with [a verse] addressed to Bṛhaspati;
he firmly establishes himself in the Brahman;
then he secures Brahmanical splendor.
Whoever contends with one established in the Brahman falls before him.
Both kinds of deities indeed expect [oblations] from the Agnihotrin: those to whom he offers, and those to whom he does not.
[With] "Of Mitra who supports the peoples", he attends [upon the fire] with [a verse] addressed to Mitra;
from both sides indeed, from here and from yonder, he makes Mitra with this [verse].
[With] "Never are you barren", he attends [upon the fire] with two bṛhatī verses addressed to Indra.
Belonging to Indra indeed are cattle;
them indeed he secures;
them he makes edible.
[With] "Around you the invulnerable chariot":
yonder sun indeed is the invulnerable chariot;
this one indeed traverses both these worlds;
he encompasses all indeed with this [verse].
[With] "You are the crusher;
may I crush down him who hates us and whom we hate":
he should press down with his heel if he contends with an inferior.
[With] "You are the overcomer;
may I overcome him who hates us and whom we hate":
he should press down with the right side of the foot if he contends with an equal.
[With] "You are the prevailing one;
may I prevail over him who hates us and whom we hate":
he should press down with the forepart of the foot if he contends with a superior.
All of them indeed he overcomes;
all of them he surpasses;
all of them he steps beyond.
[With] "May Pūṣan, path-protector, protect me":
[this is] this [earth] indeed.
[With] "May Pūṣan, cattle-protector, protect me":
[this is] the intermediate space indeed.
[With] "May Pūṣan, overlord, protect me":
[this is] yonder [sky] indeed.
Unto these worlds indeed he has approached;
for these worlds he protects himself,
for non-injury.
[With] "The eastern quarter, Agni the deity":
he deposits body-protections in the quarters.
Then he with whom he contends or who acts wrongfully toward him, encountering these very deities, perishes first.
Seven indeed are the greatnesses in man;
they indeed are to be praised by him;
they indeed are the seven ṛṣis;
the life-breaths indeed are the seven ṛṣis;
the life-breaths indeed he praises with this.
He praises his own life-breaths;
he strips off the life-breaths of his rival.
No one assailing him lays him low, who knows thus.
[With] "May law protect me from unrighteousness;
may dispersion protect me from dispersion;
life and forward-life, sight and distinct sight, onward and downward, and Uruka":
this [breath] indeed is Uruka;
he distributes;
what this one distributes, that he allots back to this one.
"O Agni, lord of the house, the sacrificer kindles the fire":
this indeed is the sacrificer's own, namely the fire;
this is Agni's, namely the sacrificer;
this is made like a proper dwelling.
"To light, to the thread, to you": having sat down between the two fires, he should say [this].
Whatever blessing he prays for before, whatever after, that he places in himself.

The gods indeed could not divide the fire.
When they carried it to the east, it was entirely in front;
when they carried it to the west, it was entirely behind.
They carried it away by means of a horse drawing first.
That is the first-drawing horse's nature of drawing first.
For the division of the fire indeed a horse is given at the fire-establishment.
Undivided indeed is his fire, unestablished, who does not give a horse at the fire-establishment.
Now he who gives a horse at the fire-establishment,
[it is] for division;
having divided it, he establishes it.
With the stoma leading, indeed, the gods conquered the Asuras.
This horse, truly, is the stoma.
When they lead a horse in front,
[it is] for conquest;
having conquered, he establishes it.
Prajāpati's eye indeed swelled;
what swelling he had, that became the horse.
When they lead a horse in front,
he bestows the eye upon the sacrificer.
It is not to be released facing away.
If he were to release it facing away, the eye would leave the sacrificer;
he would be blind.
Having held it facing back, [the fire] is to be established;
he has held back the eye for the sacrificer.
It is not to be established in [the horse's] footprint;
he would make it belong to the homestead;
Rudra would be prone to harm his cattle.
It is to be established from the side, either from this [side] or from that;
he does not make it belong to the homestead;
Paśupati does not become harmful to his cattle.
The gods indeed sent the Gāyatrī to the sacrifice;
she went empty.
Agni gave fiery splendor to her;
that became a goat.
When he gives a goat at the fire-establishment,
he secures fiery splendor.
It is to be given to the Agnīdh;
for the Agnīdh is the mouth of the sacrifice;
by means of the mouth of the sacrifice he brings success to the mouth of the sacrifice.
He gives a milch cow and a draft ox;
thereby he secures all life-span.
All life-span indeed is this: both a milch cow and a draft ox.
These two indeed are the mother and father of the sacrifice: both a milch cow and a draft ox.
Ghee and milk belong to the cow, and the cake and mess of boiled rice belong to the ox.
Regarding that they say:
"He indeed secures a wish-yielding cow who gives a milch cow and a draft ox at the fire-establishment."
Therefore, of whatever cattle he may most desire thriving, of those he should give a two-year-old in age, and an uncastrated two-year-old draft bull.
That is a pair for the thriving, for the procreation of cattle.
Speech indeed is the cow for purchasing Soma;
a two-year-old in age is the cow for purchasing Soma.
When he gives a two-year-old in age,
he secures speech.
He speaks beautifully, who knows thus.
A bolster made of all kinds of thread is to be given.
This bolster made of all kinds of thread is indeed a manifestation of the meters.
When he gives a bolster made of all kinds of thread,
he secures the meters.
Cattle indeed are the meters;
he secures cattle.
Unfit for sacrifice indeed, impure, is he born from wantonness; he sucks human milk.
He gives gold;
by that he purifies himself.
It is of a hundred measures;
a man indeed has a life-span of a hundred, having a hundredfold vigor;
he attains life-span, vigor.
For the former two oblations two [pieces] of thirty measures are to be given,
and for the latter one [a piece] of forty measures.
Thereby he has raised him up;
thereby he becomes progressively more prosperous, more distinguished.
Unborn indeed is a man as long as he does not establish the fire;
he is born only then, when he establishes the fire.
Wearing linen garments, the two [sacrificer and wife] should establish the fire.
Those are to be given to the Adhvaryu.
This linen is indeed a manifestation of the caul;
they strip away the caul.
Having thrown in gold, the fire is to be established.
Gold indeed is the fiery splendor of Agni;
with fiery splendor he establishes it.
It is not to be thrown out;
as if one were to throw out what has been deposited, such is that.
If he were to throw it out, he would be full of longing, hungry.
It is not to be thrown out;
he becomes free from longing, not hungry.

For whomever he desires, "May he be rich in cattle," from the house of one who is very prosperous they should fetch the fire.
Just as cattle were created along with this one being created, so cattle follow him when he is being fetched;
for Agni is Rudra.
Then, for whomever he desires, "May he be an eater of food," from his roasting-pan they should fetch the southern fire.
This one among the fires is the eater of food;
the roasting-pan is the maker of food;
he secures food for him.
Concerning this they say:
"Just as a low-caste man, a native Puklaka, would practice [healing], so is he."
It should only be churned;
it becomes peaceful to the sacrificer, peaceful to the cattle.
He who, not intending to sacrifice with soma, establishes the fire, must not dispense the oblations before a year.
Rudra would be prone to harm his cattle.
These indeed are cattle, namely rice and barley.
Having cooked a four-cup portion of boiled rice of these, he should present it to the Brahmins as if with live grains.
Then, regarding the deities for whom he establishes the fire, if he were not to sacrifice to them, he would be cut off from them;
he would become brooding and hungry.
He must offer ghee to them;
thereby he is not cut off from them;
he becomes unbrooding, not hungry.
In the year he dispenses the oblations;
then Rudra, separated from him by a year, does not harm the cattle.
He has offered the agnihotra for a year;
thereby he found fervor;
he climbed up to the heavenly world together with everything.
Three times indeed did this Virāj stride out: the Gārhapatya, the Āhavanīya, and the Sabhya.
Thereby he attained the Virāj;
the Virāj is food;
food indeed he thus attained.
In the middle of the gaming-ground he should offer for a nobleman with a verse addressed to Varuṇa.
Varuṇa is the king of the gods;
he secures kingship for him.
Having laid down gold, he offers;
he offers only in [a place] possessing fire, possessing a proper seat.
The Adhvaryu would become blind if he were to offer in a place without a seat.
He should hand him a hundred dice;
he should sort them out.
A man has a hundred-year lifespan, a hundredfold vigor;
he secures lifespan and vigor.
They should gamble for his cow in the hall on that day;
they must not harm its joints;
he should present it to the members of the assembly.
What he wins with it is to be given to the Brahmins;
thereby he secures food suited for the hall.

The re-establishment [of the fire] is indeed the portion of Agni.
Seeking to obtain this portion, one indeed fails.
If, while establishing [the fire], he should think, "May this [sacrifice] prosper for me," he should, having raised [the fire], establish it again.
The portion that he misses while seeking to obtain it, having reached it, he truly prospers.
Everything is made relating to Agni.
Having given whatever [is made] as a portion to Agni alone, he prospers with all prosperity.
"Materials are not to be gathered;
no yajus is to be performed," they say,
"for he has already gathered materials and performed the yajus."
Regarding this they say:
"The materials are indeed to be gathered;
the yajus is to be performed."
A re-sewn garment is to be given, a renewed chariot, a re-released ox;
these are indeed the forms of the re-establishment;
having attained its forms, he secures it.
Agni, when being raised, rises up toward the waters and the plants.
The darbha grasses are indeed waters and plants.
Because the darbha grasses are upolapas,
having attained him from the waters and the plants, he secures him.
The gods, going to victory against the Asuras, deposited their dear bodies in Agni:
"If they should conquer us, we will flee to these;
and if we should conquer, we will return to these."
Having conquered, the gods sought them:
"All of us together!"
That Agni said:
"Whoever establishes me having me as his deity, he shall unite with these bodies."
The gods established him.
He united with these bodies.
Cattle indeed are the dear bodies of the gods;
they united with cattle.
Therefore, he who, knowing thus, establishes the fire again, unites with these bodies of Agni.
Cattle indeed are the dear bodies of the gods;
he unites with cattle.
Manu, desirous of nourishment, established [it];
he nourished these nourishments;
through that, he prospered.
Tvaṣṭṛ, desirous of cattle, established [it];
these cattle of Tvaṣṭṛ were generated;
through that, he prospered.
Prajāpati, desirous of offspring, established [it];
these offspring of Prajāpati were generated;
through that, he prospered.
He who established him at first, he united with that wealth (vasu);
that is the Punarvasu-nature of Punarvasu.
Therefore [the fire] is to be established under Punarvasu;
for he united again with that wealth.
He who established him prospered;
therefore he is to be established under Anurādhā;
for prosperity;
he truly prospers;
also for pairing.

"Return again with food, again, O Agni, with nourishment and life; / protect us again from distress!" — / he should sacrifice before the fore-offerings.
"Return with wealth, O Agni; swell with a stream, / all-nourishing, from all sides!" — / he should sacrifice after the after-offerings.
With food, with cattle, he, when being raised, rises up together.
[Saying,] "Return again with food," / he secures food and cattle again;
also he makes them portion-holders;
also he prospers the blessings of this sacrifice on both sides.
"Nothing not belonging to Agni is done; why then are the two ghee-portions sacrificed?"
These two ghee-portions are the two eyes of the sacrifice.
If he were to omit the two ghee-portions, he would omit the two eyes of the sacrifice.
He should perform [the verse,] "O Agni, you purify lives" in the place of Soma.
Because it relates to Agni,
thereby it relates to Agni;
because it relates to Pavamāna,
thereby it relates to Soma;
nothing not belonging to Agni is done; they do not omit Soma.
"Agni is the head, the peak of heaven" — he desirous of offspring or desirous of cattle should perform in the place of Soma.
Since it is paired, thereby it possesses procreation;
since it possesses seed, thereby it relates to Soma.
He who raises the fire fails of the sacrifice.
The sacrifice is fivefold.
Because it is on five potsherds,
he grasps the sacrifice again.
It should be on eight potsherds;
for Agni belongs to the Gāyatrī, has the Gāyatrī meter;
relating to Agni indeed is this made, namely the re-establishment;
therefore [it should be] on eight potsherds.
That is not to be heeded.
It should indeed be on five potsherds.
From the fifth season, the sixth season arose;
this is equal: the fifth season and the sixth.
Because it is on five potsherds,
from the year he attains and secures him.
The seasons are procreation;
Agni is the procreator;
because it is on five potsherds,
from procreation the procreator procreates him.

They milk into a cooking-pot.
With this indeed they approach;
for from here it does not spill, nor from there,
for unspiltness.
It is made by an Ārya, with upright potsherd,
for being with the gods,
for it is with the gods.
Belonging to the Asuras indeed is that vessel which is made by a potter, turned on a wheel.
Where it may spill, there he should pour water.
Water indeed is appeasement;
water is expiation;
water is medicine.
Where indeed these [waters] reach of this [earth], there most excellent, most dense plants grow.
If it should break while being milked, having rinsed another cooking-pot, [milk] is to be milked [into it];
for sacrifice is the expiation of sacrifice.
If it should spill when put on [the fire], or when being taken off, or when taken off, or when being scooped up, or when scooped up, or if brought forward for the offering, having poured it down again, another is to be milked into it;
for sacrifice is the expiation of sacrifice.
Having recited a verse to Varuṇa, one should offer with [a verse] to Varuṇa.
Varuṇa indeed seizes of the sacrifice that which suffers injury.
This is an expiation, an atonement.
There should be no pouring upon for one desiring brilliance, desiring Vedic luster,
and for one wishing to overthrow [a rival],
and who should desire, "May a hero be born to me!"
The unpoured cauldron indeed is brilliance, Vedic luster.
He becomes possessed of brilliance, possessed of Vedic luster;
he overthrows whom he wishes to overthrow;
a hero is born to him.
One should offer with ghee for whom there is no pouring upon;
this indeed is unpoured upon.
But there is no expiation for the spilt [milk];
also he does not become, as it were, possessed of cattle.
This milk of cattle is cut off;
he places grief in the cattle;
unappeased brilliance burns the cattle down.
Therefore it should indeed be poured upon.
If he should pour water upon, he would remove the heat.
It is to be poured upon with the water used for washing the cow during milking;
for that is neither water nor milk.
Regarding this they say:
"This indeed spills;
when this is separated from the milking, then it is spilt,
for the trees of this [earth] are concealed [from it]."
So [they say].
It is to be poured upon with water alone;
by a single drop he does not remove the heat;
he appeases the grief of the cattle.
Cattle indeed are brilliance, Vedic luster.
A pairing indeed are water and milk;
from a pairing indeed creatures and cattle are born;
that is a pairing.
From that very pairing the sacrificer is born with offspring and cattle.
One should offer with boiled rice for whom it is to be poured upon;
for this, poured upon, is appeased, fit for sacrifice, a pairing, productive.

Belonging to Rudra while in the cow;
belonging to Vāyu when let loose [to the calf];
belonging to the Aśvins while being milked;
belonging to Soma when milked;
belonging to Varuṇa when set on [the fire];
belonging to Mitra when covered with skin;
belonging to the Maruts when forming drops;
belonging to Pūṣan when boiling up;
belonging to Sarasvatī when overflowing;
belonging to Viṣṇu when brought back;
belonging to heaven when taken off [the fire];
belonging to Tvaṣṭṛ when being drawn up;
belonging to Dhātṛ when drawn up;
belonging to Viśvakarman when raised;
belonging to Savitṛ when being brought forward;
belonging to Heaven and Earth when being borne forth;
belonging to Indra and Agni when set down;
belonging to Prajāpati on the kindling-stick;
belonging to the Viśvedevas when offered.

Thought is the ladle;
thinking is the ghee;
speech is the vedi;
reflection is the sacrificial grass;
intent is Agni;
the known is the agnīdh;
the Lord of Speech is the hotṛ;
the mind is the upavaktṛ;
the out-breath is the oblation;
the sāman is the adhvaryu.
"Go to Indra, svāhā!"
The earth is the hotṛ;
heaven is the adhvaryu;
Tvaṣṭṛ is the agnīdh;
Mitra is the upavaktṛ.
"O Lord of Speech, through the fullest gathered power of speech, to the healthy
master of sacrifice grant desirable things!"
The Lord of Speech drank the soma;
he begat Indra for virile power.
Let soma drink of soma;
let the bright drink of the bright!
Boiled are your somas, O Indra, wind-swelling, hearing our invocation.
Agni is the hotṛ;
the two Aśvins are the two adhvaryus;
Rudra is the agnīdh;
Bṛhaspati is the upavaktṛ.
"O Lord of Speech, Hinvividhe by name,
may we worship your name;
may you worship our name!"
The Lord of Speech drank the soma;
may he place manly courage in us!
Let soma drink of soma;
let the bright drink of the bright!
Boiled are your somas, O Indra, wind-swelling, hearing our invocation.
Mahāhavis is the hotṛ;
Satyahavis is the adhvaryu;
Acittapājas is the agnīdh;
Acittamanas is the upavaktṛ;
Anādhṛṣya and Apratidhṛṣya are the two abhigaras;
Ayāsya is the udgātṛ.
"O Vidhe by name,
may we worship your name;
may you worship our name!
May the lineage of the gods not be cut off, nor that of men!
Homage to Mother Earth!"

Agni with the sacrificial formulas;
Savitṛ with the chants;
Indra with the recitation-delights;
Bṛhaspati with the meters;
Aditi and the waters and the sacrificial grass;
the Ādityas with the ghee offerings;
the Maruts with the sadas and the havirdhāna;
Viṣṇu with consecration and ascetic heat;
Mitra and Varuṇa with the fire-hearths;
the Aśvins with the milk-mixture;
Tvaṣṭṛ with the kindling-stick;
Pūṣan with the utterances of "svāhā".
Speech is the wife of Vāyu;
Pathyā of Pūṣan;
the Earth of Agni;
Senā of Indra;
Dhēnā of Bṛhaspati.
The gāyatrī of the Vasus;
the triṣṭubh of the Rudras;
the jagatī of the Ādityas;
the anuṣṭubh of Mitra;
the virāj of Varuṇa;
the paṅkti of Viṣṇu;
the consecration of Soma.

Prajāpati, indeed, was alone.
He desired:
"Becoming the sacrifice, may I generate creatures."
He arranged himself as the ten-hotṛ sacrifice.
He made thought the ladle, thinking the ghee, speech the vedi, reflection the sacrificial grass.
Having divided himself tenfold and having formed a pair, he sought a support.
He perceived the three-fold vital breaths as the support;
with them he created creatures.
The waxing fifteen-day fortnight broke forth upwards;
with it he created the gods;
those gods swelled upwards.
He who knows thus swells upwards.
The waning fifteen-day fortnight broke forth downwards;
with it he created the asuras;
those asuras collapsed downwards.
His rival collapses who knows thus.
By day he created the gods, by night the asuras;
those gods became white, the asuras black.
By truth he created the gods, by untruth the asuras;
those gods became truth,
the asuras untruth.
With the right hand he created the gods, with the left the asuras;
those gods became vigorous, the asuras powerless.
Then the gods prevailed, the asuras were defeated.
So he who knows thus prevails himself, and his rival is defeated.
He thought: "Where shall I offer?"
Prajāpati found no place where he might offer;
nor did he have anything else to offer from other than breath.
He perceived Indra within his own self as the support;
with "Go to Indra, svāhā!" he breathed out.
Breath is indeed virility; Indra is virility.
Into virility, verily, he offered virility.

Going to heaven, they said:
"From here, give us the sacrificial post!"
"By what support will you find [it] right here?" they said.
They, as the seven-hotṛs, sat down with Aryaman as householder.
Mahāhavis was the hotṛ,
Satyahavis the adhvaryu,
Acittapājas the agnīdh,
Acittamanas the upavaktṛ,
Anādhṛṣya and Apratidhṛṣya the abhigaras,
Ayāsya the udgātṛ.
They drew this cup-offering:
"O Vidhe, by name! May we worship thy name;
mayest thou worship our name!"
They perceived the thirty-three-fold support;
with it they extended this [all].
Indeed, by the seven-hotṛ and by the thirty-three-fold this [world] is connected, since here gods and men bestow upon one another.
The ten-hotṛ is the out-breath;
the four-hotṛ is the eye;
the five-hotṛ is the ear;
the seven-hotṛ is speech and the self.
The ten-hotṛ is the agnihotra;
the four-hotṛ is the new- and full-moon [sacrifices];
the five-hotṛ is the four-month sacrifices;
the seven-hotṛ is the soma rite.
The agnihotra is offered for life;
he reaches a full life who knows thus.
The new- and full-moon [sacrifices] are offered for the eye;
[he is] not [deprived] of his eye at home who knows thus.
The four-month sacrifices are offered for the ear;
[he is] not [deprived] of his ear at home who knows thus.
The soma rite is offered for speech and the self;
[he is] not [deprived] of speech nor of the self at home who knows thus.
Prajāpati, indeed, is the ten-hotṛ;
being one, he has the greatest share of the sacred utterances;
being one, he becomes the greatest who knows thus.
The full-moon [sacrifice] is offered for splendor.
The brahmin belongs to Soma by deity, has the gāyatrī meter;
Agni has the gāyatrī meter;
he has placed him on his own two deities with equal width;
no other rules over his acquisition and preservation.
The new-moon [sacrifice] is offered for life;
for he is brought together, as it were;
he reaches a full life who knows thus.
The soma rite is offered for the heavenly world;
he goes to the heavenly world who knows thus.
He should touch the drawn agnihotra with the ten-hotṛ;
with the ten-hotṛ, indeed, the gods created the agnihotra;
he touches it when born, when created.
He should touch the new- and full-moon [sacrifices] with the four-hotṛ;
with the four-hotṛ, indeed, the gods created the new- and full-moon [sacrifices];
he touches both when born, when created.
He should touch the four-month sacrifices with the five-hotṛ;
with the five-hotṛ, indeed, the gods created the four-month sacrifices;
he touches them when born, when created.
He should touch the soma rite with the seven-hotṛ;
with the seven-hotṛ, indeed, the gods created the soma rite;
he touches it when born, when created.
He should recite the ten-hotṛ before the kindling verses;
Prajāpati, indeed, is the ten-hotṛ;
the kindling verses are the opening of the sacrifice;
he places a pair at the opening of the sacrifice for him.
He should recite the four-hotṛ before the fore-offerings;
with the four-hotṛ, indeed, the gods generated Indra;
he generates Indra in the sacrifice for him;
he sacrifices with a sacrifice accompanied by Indra.
He should recite the five-hotṛ before the oblations;
with the five-hotṛ, indeed, the gods created cattle;
they defeated the asuras;
he creates cattle, he defeats the rival.
He should recite the seven-hotṛ before the after-offerings;
with the seven-hotṛ, indeed, the gods went to heaven;
he goes to heaven.
He should recite the ten-hotṛ before the bahiṣpavamāna;
Prajāpati, indeed, is the ten-hotṛ;
the bahiṣpavamāna is the opening of the sacrifice;
he places a pair at the opening of the sacrifice for him.
He should recite the four-hotṛ before the ājya [chants];
he should recite the five-hotṛ before the midday pavamāna;
he should recite the seven-hotṛ before the ārbhava pavamāna;
with the seven-hotṛ, indeed, the gods went to heaven;
he goes to heaven.
Whoever knows the caturhotṛ-formulas that are to be gratified at each pressing, is gratified with offspring and with cattle.
These four-hotṛs to be gratified at each pressing are the brahmins who know much;
he gratifies them;
they, being gratified, gratify him with offspring and with cattle.

"A rat I make thy victim, O Rudra;
this is thy portion, O Rudra;
enjoy it together with thy sister Ambikā, svāhā!
We have appeased Rudra, O mother, appeased the three-eyed god,
so that he may make us more prosperous, so that he may make us better,
so that he may make us more abundant, so that he may promote us further, so that he may cause us to decide.
Healing for cow, for horse, for man healing,
also healing for us, that there may be good healing, good passage for ram and ewe!
We worship the three-eyed god, fragrant, increaser of nourishment;
as a cucumber from its stem, from death may I be released, not from immortality!
O Rudra, this is thy portion;
with it as provision go beyond the Mūjavants!
With pināka in hand, clad in a skin, with unstrung bow! //"

Sour milk and boiled milk are a couple.
Now, what is mixed, appearing like an egg, like whey, that is an embryo.
Creatures were propagated from heat;
in that it attains boiledness, it attains heat;
therefrom he is propagated.
Now, this curd-mixture is for the All-gods.
Prajāpati created creatures;
he created them through the Vaiśvadeva itself;
therefore these creatures belong to the All-gods.
He sacrifices to the All-gods;
the All-gods are drops;
those indeed he sacrifices to.
Also, from the giving from yonder, men live upon the sacrifice.
This is the womb of creatures.
The Maruts desired it;
thence they were created seized by distress.
In that it is for the Svātavats,
it is for selfhood, for atonement.
This is the procreator of cattle.
In that the [oblation] to the Maruts is on seven potsherds,
seven are the Maruts;
the Maruts are the clan;
also, the [oblation] to the Maruts is for atonement;
also, by this he secures village-born food.

Having created creatures, having expiated distress, he desired: "May I slay Vṛtra."
Having united with these deities, with the Maruts as the people, with Agni as the vanguard, he swooped down.
Going up to Vṛtra, seeing Vṛtra, seized by paralysis of the thighs, not daring, he stopped.
The Maruts went after him;
they went past [him].
When they reached his vital parts, then he stirred;
they heated him thoroughly then.
Therefore they are the Sāṃtapanas.
With Agni as the vanguard Indra slew Vṛtra;
therefore this [oblation] is for having a vanguard.
Moreover, Agni is the general of the gods;
therefore this is for raising an army.
Indra could not lift the thunderbolt against Vṛtra;
he poured out this share for the Maruts;
they heated him thoroughly for heroic power;
with that heroic power he raised it.
They heated him thoroughly then;
therefore they are the Sāṃtapanas.
At midday the pot of boiled rice is to be offered;
then he heats both ends.
It should be a pot of boiled rice;
for that heats him all around.
The gods did not find Vṛtra's vital part;
the Maruts pierced him with razor-sharp wheel-rims;
they heated him thoroughly then;
therefore they are the Sāṃtapanas.

On the morrow, being about to slay Vṛtra, they dwelt in preparation.
They said: "Whose indeed shall this be tomorrow? Or cook for whom?"
They cooked this boiled rice;
with it they noticed the cattle.
They knew: "Whichever of the two these [cattle] will approach, they shall become this [master of all]."
For them they stood ready with this [food];
them they drew near with this.
That is indeed the course of the cattle.
Having victory on the morrow, they dwelt.
They said: "Whose indeed shall this be tomorrow? Or cook for whom?"
They cooked this boiled rice.
They said: "Let us not eat what has not been offered."
They offered it to the Maruts, the Gṛhamedhas.
The Maruts, the Gṛhamedhas, are cattle;
indeed to the cattle they offered it.
They were arrayed for battle.
The Asuras sent hunger against the gods.
The gods, hearing it, cooked this boiled rice.
Finding no place among the gods, it entered the Asuras again.
Then the gods flourished,
the Asuras perished.
Therefore, he who, knowing thus, cooks this boiled rice, becomes [prosperous] himself; his rival perishes.
Even with a neighbor he may cook;
he sends hunger against his rival alone.
What of the four-month sacrifices [is this], that is their domestic sacrifice, relating to cattle;
relating to cattle is the Gṛhamedha [rite].
He does not offer the fore-offerings, nor the after-offerings;
he does not recite the kindling verses.
He offers the two butter-portions,
for sacrifice-hood.
He cuts off [a share] together for Agni;
for Agni is the finished offering;
Agni is a firm standing;
for the firm standing of the finished offering.
They invoke the iḍā;
the iḍā is cattle;
relating to cattle is the Gṛhamedha [rite];
therefore they invoke the iḍā.
He deposits the scrapings,
for continuity.

"Desiring, we summon thee; desiring, we would kindle thee; / desiring, bring the desiring fathers to eat the oblation." / Thus he recites.
For the fathers are desiring.
He recites an anuṣṭubh;
the anuṣṭubh is an end;
the fathers are an end;
therefore he recites an anuṣṭubh.
He recites one;
for the fathers have one world.
He recites three times;
for the fathers are in the third world.
In that he recites one,
by that it belongs to the fathers.
In that [he recites] three times,
by that it belongs to the gods;
both are sacrificed to.
He chooses neither the Hotṛ nor the Ṛṣi-ancestor;
he releases them from death itself.
He offers the fore-offerings without sacrificial grass;
the sacrificial grass is offspring;
he releases offspring from death itself.
One puts on his upper garment [regularly] when about to act for men;
he wears it over the right shoulder for the gods.
Now here he should have his sacred thread over the right shoulder [prācīnāvīta],
for distinction.
Having sliced off portions from the south, stepping across toward the north, standing facing the south he offers,
for the south belongs to the fathers.
First he sacrifices to Soma;
Soma is indeed the deity of the fathers;
Soma has the fathers as deity.
In that he sacrifices to Soma with the fathers,
he thereby sacrifices to the Soma-drinking fathers.
In that [he sacrifices to] those sitting on the grass,
[it is to] the sacrificers.
In that [he sacrifices to] the Agniṣvāttas,
[it is to] the householders.
In that [he sacrifices to] Agni, the conveyer of kavyas—
Agni indeed has two bodies:
with the one conveying havyas he conveys the oblation to the gods, with the one conveying kavyas to the fathers—
[it is] for the full completion, for stability.
Two indeed are the yājyā and the anuvākyā for the gods;
with the one he holds the breath, with the other he causes it to go.
Now here three are to be made;
for the fathers are further beyond than the gods;
so by that which is the third, he has indeed given beyond [them].
He cuts off portions five times;
the sacrifice is fivefold;
as great as the sacrifice is, he takes hold of it.
He pronounces the vaṣaṭ with: "Svadhā! Obeisance!"
Svadhā-call is for the fathers, obeisance for the gods;
both are sacrificed to.
He places [them] at the corners;
men indeed drink after the divine fathers;
he has sacrificed to the divine fathers.
He places [them] three times;
for we are turned toward three persons here;
he names three beyond [them],
for three are beyond: father, son, grandson,
for unbroken continuation.
He places [them] in all the corners;
for the fathers are in all directions;
he causes him to reach all directions.
He does not place [it] in that [direction];
if he should place it in that [direction], he would surround them with death;
they follow after that [direction].
Now in that he wipes [his hand] on it,
he thereby pleases it.

Now the year became desired for him, the seasons [became] desired.
Then the Rudras were not desired for him.
In that there are these Tryambaka [offerings],
by that the Rudras become desired and pleased for him.
They are [cakes] on one potsherd;
a person is not to be obtained by [multiple] potsherds;
he reaches him singly.
Moreover, this [earth] is one;
he firmly establishes himself on this [earth] itself.
They deliberate: "Is [the offering] to be anointed with butter, or not to be anointed with butter?"
If he should anoint it with butter, he would deliver his cattle to Rudra.
That is not to be heeded;
it is to be anointed with butter indeed,
for there is no oblation that is not anointed with butter.
They carry a single firebrand;
for a single firebrand belongs to the Rudras.
They carry it smoking;
for what is smoking belongs to the Rudras.
They carry it in that direction [the north-east];
for that is the direction of the Rudras.
They carry it backwards;
they carry Rudra away backwards indeed.
Saying, "I make the mole thy beast, O Rudra," he scatters one [cake] on a mole-heap;
by that he buys off [his] cattle [from Rudra];
therefore the Lord of Cattle is destructive to them.
He should sacrifice at a crossroad;
for at a crossroad are the dwellings of the Rudras;
he buys Rudra off in his own dwellings.
"This is thy portion, O Rudra; enjoy it together with thy sister Ambikā, svāhā!"
Autumn indeed is the womb of Rudra, [namely] his sister Ambikā;
he indeed approaches [creatures] in pursuit of her;
therefore he slays most in autumn.
Together with her he buys him off.
He offers with a middle [palāśa] leaf;
for that is least harmed by the Rakṣases.
He offers with [a leaf] from the wilderness;
he buys Rudra off in the wilderness itself.
If he were to offer with a vessel, he would bring Rudra down upon creatures;
therefore he offers with [a leaf] from the wilderness.
"We have paid homage to Ambā [and] to Rudra,"
[saying which] they have become free from debt.
"Medicine for cow, for horse, medicine for man,"
[saying which] having become free from debt, they made medicine.
Saying, "We sacrifice to Tryambaka," they walk around.
There the woman desiring a husband also walks around;
that is finding a husband for her.
Then, tossing those [cakes] upward, they catch them;
they catch good fortune itself.
They pour them together for the sacrificer;
they pour good fortune together for him.
If there is one who desires a husband, they should pour [them] together for her;
they pour good fortune together for her.
Having placed them in a net-bag, he hangs them on a tree.
"O Rudra, this is thy portion; / with it as provisions, go beyond Mūjavant, / with pināka in hand, clad in a skin, with unstrung bow!"
The mountain indeed is the womb of Rudra;
from there indeed, coming down, he harms creatures;
by his own portion he causes him to go to his own womb.
They return without looking back,
so that Rudra will not follow.
They cleanse themselves outside the cowpen;
outside the cowpen indeed they buy Rudra off.
Ambī is a woman named Bhaga;
therefore they are the Tryambakas.
He whose oblation is unestablished, he is unestablished;
his Tryambaka [cakes] are unestablished.
Having returned, he should offer a caru to Aditi in ghee in the house.
Aditi is this [earth];
this is the firm foundation;
in that it is for Aditi,
he firmly establishes himself on this [earth] itself.

One whose kinsmen have dispersed should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra and Agni.
Indra and Agni are indeed strength and virility;
with strength and virility he brings them back near.
One who has a rival should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra and Agni.
Indra and Agni are indeed strength and virility;
with strength and virility he overcomes them.
One desiring offspring, who though capable of offspring does not obtain offspring, should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra and Agni.
Indra and Agni hid Prajāpati's offspring.
He ran to them with this share;
they two gave the offspring back to him.
Indeed Indra and Agni hide the offspring of him who, though capable of offspring, does not obtain offspring.
He has approached them with a share;
they two give offspring back to him.
The offering- and invitatory verses contain [the word] "find" [or "obtain"],
for obtaining [offspring].
Setting out for battle, he should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra and Agni.
Indra and Agni are indeed strength and virility;
with strength and virility he advances against it.
Having joined battle, he should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra and Agni.
Indra and Agni are indeed strength and virility;
with strength and virility he conquers it.
When he has won the battle, he should then offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra and Agni.
He who wins a battle is deprived of strength and virility.
Indra and Agni are strength and virility;
with strength and virility they two replenish him.
Setting out toward people, he should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra and Agni, and a caru to Pūṣan.
Indra and Agni are indeed strength and virility;
with strength and virility he advances toward them.
Pūṣan is the bestower of virility;
he bestows virility upon him.
Taking possession of land, he should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra and Agni, a caru to Pūṣan, and a caru to the Lord of the Field.
Indra and Agni are indeed strength and virility;
with strength and virility they two obtain space for him.
Pūṣan is the bestower of virility;
he bestows virility upon him.
This [earth] is the mistress of the field;
in her he establishes himself.

For a desire, he should offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara.
The year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
in a year desire is obtained;
he obtains the year;
he obtains the desire for which he has a desire.
Intending to plot against a neighbour, he should offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara.
The year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
[an oath/alliance] is contracted for a year;
having attained the year, he harms his desire [without] Varuṇa [punishing him].
Going to seek a gift, he should offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara.
The year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
[a gift] is received for the year;
having attained the year, he wins the gained gift.
When he has won it, he should then offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara.
The year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
he harnesses the year;
the year wins for him;
he makes him a sharer;
he unyokes him.
He should give the sacrificial fee to one whom he hates.
He wanders about with a noose;
he fastens it upon him.
A one-year-old bull is the sacrificial fee,
for it is the counterpart of the year.
About to eat improper food, he should offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara.
The year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
he conceals it for the year.
When he eats improper food, he should then offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara.
Whatever improper food he eats there, the year makes that palatable for him;
he eats it as palatable.
Lead or a black cloth is the sacrificial fee.
Lead is improper food; a black [cloth] is improper food;
warding off improper food with improper food, he places food-sustenance in himself.
Setting out for battle, he should offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara.
The year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
with the year he advances against it.
Having joined battle, he should offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara.
Whichever of two combatants has the firmer footing, he conquers.
This [earth] is Agni Vaiśvānara;
he makes this his footing;
on this he strides boldly forward;
he wins the battle.
When he has won the battle, he should then offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara.
The year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
he harnesses the year;
the year conquers for him;
he makes him a sharer;
he unyokes him.
He who desires: "May I through this king deposit these barleys or rices," should offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara.
The year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
the year is the giver of food-sustenance;
he has approached him with a share;
he grants him food-sustenance.
He should offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara and a barley caru to Varuṇa;
he should sacrifice for one who is diseased.
Seized by Varuṇa is he who is diseased;
from Varuṇa he frees him by that.
Yonder sun is Agni Vaiśvānara.

Varuṇa seizes him who is held fast by yonder [sun];
having freed him from that, as great as his self is, so much he frees from Varuṇa.

He should offer a barley caru to Varuṇa of this size, and [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara;
he should sacrifice for one desiring prosperity.
Seized by Varuṇa is he who desires prosperity;
from Varuṇa he frees him by that.
The caru is of this size;
so great is the self;
as great as his self is, having freed him from Varuṇa,
yonder sun is Agni Vaiśvānara;
he makes him take hold of yonder one;
he makes him attain the measure of yonder sun.

He should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Jātavedas, [one] on eleven potsherds to Dadhikrāvan, [and one] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara, who, having given all his possessions, undertakes the first offering.
Agni indeed knows of this [man] where his sacrifice is, where his good deed is;
Agni indeed finds that for him.
Impure indeed is he who gives everything;
so Dadhikrāvan makes him pure.
The year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
the year indeed knows of this [man] where his sacrifice is, where his good deed is;
the year indeed finds that for him.
He should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Surabhimat;
he should sacrifice for one who is falsely accused.
Rathaprota Dārbhya was falsely accused;
to him the two Kaulakāvatīs said:
‘We two will sacrifice for you in such a way that they will eat your food.
Go where you do not hear of domestic cattle;
whoever approaches you, sit quite silent.’
So [they said].
Tigers, having smelled him, went away quite silently.
On the next day, having followed with the sacrificial implements, having churned fire there, the two offered [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Surabhimat.
Then they did not bypass him.
For him whom they falsely accuse, he should sacrifice with this.
Foul-smelling, indeed, do they falsely accuse him of an offense;
this is the healing form of Agni, namely Surabhi [the fragrant];
he has provided a remedy for him;
he has made him fragrant;
he pacifies [it].
He should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Pavamāna, [one] on eleven potsherds to Dadhikrāvan, [and one] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara, having returned home.
Pavamāna purifies him, Agni burns out [the taint].
Unpurified indeed is he whom they falsely accuse;
indeed they say: ‘Not even Dadhikrāvan could purify him’;
so Dadhikrāvan purifies him.
The year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
the year indeed sweetens him.
He should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni, [one] on eleven potsherds to Agni and Soma, [and one] on two potsherds to Heaven and Earth, who wishes to win a battle or wishes to overpower a rival.
The [cake] to Agni is for prosperity.
By Agni and Soma as heroic power, Indra slew Vṛtra;
he indeed slays Vṛtra who wins a battle or overpowers a rival;
therefore this is Vṛtra-slaying.
Indra raised the thunderbolt against Vṛtra;
Heaven and Earth did not assent to him;
they assented to him by this share.
As for the one to Heaven and Earth,
[it is] for the assent of the thunderbolt,
and so that he may have an assented thunderbolt.
When he wins a battle or overpowers a rival, then he should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni, [one] on eleven potsherds to Indra and Agni, [and one] on two potsherds to Heaven and Earth.
The [cake] to Agni is for prosperity.
By Agni and Soma as heroic power, Indra slew Vṛtra;
he was deprived of strength, of heroic power;
he saw this [cake] to Indra and Agni;
by it he placed strength and heroic power in himself.
He indeed is deprived of strength, of heroic power, who wins a battle or overpowers a rival;
Indra and Agni are strength and heroic power;
with strength, with heroic power, they two cause him to prosper.
Now, as for the one to Heaven and Earth:
to the two who assented to the thunderbolt for him, this share is made.

A Brahmin should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Agni-Soma for a desire.
A Brahmin belongs indeed to Agni-Soma by deity;
he approaches his own deity with a share for his desire;
they accomplish his desire for him, whatever his desire is.
He should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Agni-Soma;
he should sacrifice for a Brahmin desiring prosperity.
The gods indeed sat at a sacrificial session in Kurukṣetra: Agni, Soma, Indra.
They said:
'Whichever of us fame shall reach first, he shall overcome with us.'
Fame reached Soma among them.
They went together toward him;
therefore people go together toward Soma,
for he is the most famous.
Soma indeed desired that [place down below];
he went to the mountain;
Agni went after him;
the two, Agni and Soma, became united on the mountain.
Therefore Agni continuously burns on the mountain, and Soma is on the mountain.
Indra indeed considered himself as if slackened;
he went after Agni and Soma;
he said to the two:
'Sacrifice for me!'
Agni and Soma indeed sacrificed for him with this [offering].
In him Agni placed fiery brilliance, Soma virility;
then Indra became [mighty].
He who should desire prosperity, for him he should sacrifice with this;
Agni places fiery brilliance in him, Soma virility;
he indeed becomes [prosperous].
One desiring spiritual luster should offer [a cake] of millet on eleven potsherds to Agni-Soma in spring.
Agni and Soma are indeed the givers of spiritual luster;
he approaches them with their share;
they grant him spiritual luster.
He should sacrifice in spring;
spring is indeed the season of the Brahmin;
in his own season they grant him spiritual luster.
In that it is a cake, thereby it belongs to Agni;
in that it is of millet, thereby it belongs to Soma;
he has delivered the entire sacrificial offering to Agni-Soma;
they grant him all spiritual luster.
The two samyājyā verses should be addressed to Soma and Agni.
Agni is fiery brilliance;
Soma is virility;
with fiery brilliance and with virility he grasps spiritual luster on both sides for him.
One desiring cattle should offer a caru of half-ground [grain] to Soma-Pūṣan.
Soma is the depositor of semen;
Pūṣan is the progenitor of cattle;
Soma deposits semen for him;
Pūṣan brings forth cattle.
One desiring the office of purohita should offer a caru to Soma-Indra.
A Brahmin belongs to Soma by deity;
a nobleman belongs to Indra;
Soma indeed grants what cannot be taken away;
he approaches him with his share;
he grants him a realm that cannot be taken away.
One who has harmed a fellow, or one who is diseased, should offer a caru to Agni-Varuṇa.
He who harms a fellow indeed commits an untruth;
he who commits an untruth offends against the deity.
Agni is all deities;
here indeed is that deity also against whom he offended; from that he frees him.
In that it belongs to Varuṇa,
from Varuṇa he frees him thereby.
This ... is performed: an expiation of everything, an atonement of everything.

One desiring spiritual luster should offer a caru of white rice in ghee to Soma-Rudra.
Svarbhānu the Asura pierced the sun with darkness;
Soma and Rudra healed him;
they drove away his blemish with this very [offering];
with this they placed fiery brilliance in him.
He who should desire spiritual luster, for him he should sacrifice with this;
he drives away his blemish;
he places fiery brilliance in him.
The caru is of this size;
so great is the self;
as great as his self is, so much fiery brilliance he places in him.
The rice grains are white; they milk white cows for ghee;
fiery brilliance is collected thereby.
Ghee is the sprinkling;
they wipe themselves with ghee;
it is in ghee;
he places further fiery brilliance in him.
On whatever night he is about to sacrifice in the morning, they should not carry water into his house for that night;
water is pacification;
they would appease [the fire].
They sacrifice in an enclosed space,
for the securing of fiery brilliance.
They proceed along with the rays [of the sun];
along with the rays they strike away his blemish.
He should sacrifice at the full moon with Tiṣya;
Soma is the moon;
Rudra is Tiṣya;
he has approached them directly.
The plants belonging to Soma face eastward;
those belonging to Rudra face westward;
for they do not dry up toward the east; they dry up toward the west.
The verses are of Manu;
whatever Manu spoke, that he spoke as medicine;
that is for the sake of medicine.
These are Śakvarī verses,
for power;
they are Nārāśaṃsī verses,
for peace.
From leprosy indeed is fear for him;
for they drive it far away.
One desiring cattle should offer a caru of half-ground [grain] to Soma-Pūṣan.
A Brahmin belongs to Soma by deity;
cattle are Pūṣan;
he strengthens his own deity with cattle;
he has indeed made a skin.

Practicing sorcery, he should offer a caru of black rice to Soma-Rudra.
The plants belong to Soma;
having begged him away from that, he shuts up his cattle for Rudra.
The rice grains are black;
black is darkness;
darkness is death;
he causes him to be seized by death alone.
The barhis is made of śara-reed; the firewood is of vibhīdaka wood.
Thinking, 'May he crush [him]',
the barhis is of śara-reed; for division the firewood is of vibhīdaka wood.
He who should desire, 'May I produce a second one in his realm', should offer a caru of unselected rice in butter-milk to Soma-Rudra.
These creatures belong to Soma;
he produces a second one in his realm.
Half the barhis is of śara-reed, half not of śara-reed;
half the firewood is of vibhīdaka wood, half not of vibhīdaka wood;
he produces a second one in his realm.
He should offer a curd-mixture to Soma-Rudra;
he should sacrifice for one who is diseased.
Dead, he belongs to Agni;
living, to Soma;
from both sides he buys him off.
Man is milk;
milk ails in him;
by milk he buys off his milk.
The Hotṛ should have his eyes bandaged;
having led him away to the wilderness, he should make him open his eyes;
he should give an ox to him;
he should slaughter it;
he should eat of it;
if he should not eat of it, he would die.

One practicing sorcery, or one against whom sorcery is practiced, should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Agni-Viṣṇu;
he should also sacrifice with ghee for Sarasvatī.
Agni is all deities;
with the deities he counteracts his deities;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
with the sacrifice, the sacrifice;
Sarasvatī is speech;
with speech, speech.
Thereby he has employed [it] after counteracting [the attack];
and further, having counteracted, he has employed [it] over [him].
One desiring sight should offer a caru in ghee to Agni-Viṣṇu.
Agni is the giver of sight of men, Viṣṇu of the gods;
these two are the givers of sight;
he approaches them with a share;
they grant him sight.
Ghee is the milk of the cow, rice grains are of the draft ox;
that is a pair;
sight is a pair;
with a pair they produce a pair of sight for him.
Ghee is milk;
sight is milk;
with milk they produce milk-like sight for him.
Ghee is fiery brilliance;
sight is fiery brilliance;
with fiery brilliance they produce fiery-brilliant sight for him.
He gives gold;
gold is lifespan;
sight is lifespan;
with lifespan he places lifespan and sight in him.
It is of a hundred measures;
man has a lifespan of a hundred, a strength of a hundred;
he obtains lifespan and strength.
One against whom sorcery is practiced should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Agni-Viṣṇu.
Agni is all deities;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
he enters between the deities and the sacrifice in order to overcome [the rival].
In battle he should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Agni-Viṣṇu;
he should also sacrifice with ghee for Sarasvatī.
Agni is all deities;
with the deities he repels his deities;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
with the sacrifice, the sacrifice;
Sarasvatī is speech;
with speech, speech.
If he should think, 'They are attacking from the front', he should make two invitatory verses and one offering verse.
With two [the balance] is made equal; with one he prevails.
He whose rival should sacrifice with soma should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni-Viṣṇu in the morning, a caru to Sarasvatī, a caru to Bṛhaspati; [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Agni-Viṣṇu at midday, a caru to Sarasvatī, a caru to Bṛhaspati; [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni-Viṣṇu in the afternoon, a caru to Sarasvatī, a caru to Bṛhaspati.
Agni is all deities;
with the deities he obtains his deities;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
with the sacrifice, the sacrifice;
Sarasvatī is speech;
with speech, speech;
Bṛhaspati is the sacred word;
with the sacred word he obtains his sacred word;
with the potsherds, the meters;
with the cakes, the soma-pressings.
He should offer [a cake] on one potsherd to Mitra-Varuṇa, or a curd offering;
with this he obtains the anūbandhyā cow.
This is the sacrifice-modeling offering;
with it he obtains the sacrifice.

One desiring rain should offer a caru to Agni-Maruts.
They should make the caru and the pot from the same clay.
In the very fire where they cook the caru, they should fumigate the pot.
Smoke goes from here to yonder, not the flame;
therefore they fumigate it, they do not cook it.
When they set down the oblations, then, having placed the pot on the southern hip [of the altar], they should fill it with water.
If it should break before the completion [of the rite], he should say: 'It will rain today.'
If at the completion, he should say: 'It will rain tomorrow.'
If it should break after a long time, he should say: 'We do not know for sure.'
Agni seeks rain from here; the Maruts send it down from yonder.
These are the givers of rain;
he approaches them with a share;
they grant him rain.
One desiring a village or desiring cattle should offer a caru of priyaṅgu-millet in milk to the Maruts.
In the milk of speckled cows, there should be ghee of speckled cows;
into that they should also sprinkle cow-urine.
When the speckled one milked, that became priyaṅgu-millet;
this [earth] or speech is the speckled one;
this priyaṅgu-millet is her milk;
with her own milk he approaches her.
The invitatory and offering verses contain the word 'dear' (priya);
he makes him dear to his kinsmen.
They are two-footed and four-footed;
he wins for him both two-footed and four-footed cattle.
Just as the calf stretches toward the udder, or the cow toward the calf, so the kinsmen stretch toward him.
He whose two twin sons or twin calves should be born should offer [a cake] on thirteen potsherds to the Maruts.
A twin person being born bodes childlessness; a cow, cattleness.
That which is thirteenth;
Prajāpati is the thirteenth;
he obtains Prajāpati.
That which is twelfth, is by means of the year;
that the invitatory verse is a gāyatrī, is by means of the year;
that the offering verse is a jagatī, is for cattle;
that there is an offering to the Maruts, is for cattle.
Where the common people should attempt to overpower the king, he should offer [a cake] on seven potsherds to the Maruts.
The Kayāśubhīya of Agastya should be the kindling verses and the invitatory and offering verses.
Agastya sprinkled oxen for the Maruts;
he slaughtered them for Indra.
They flew at him, taking the thunderbolt;
with this [hymn] he pacified them;
with this he pacifies them.
It is on seven potsherds;
for the Maruts are seven;
the Maruts are the common people;
with their own share he pacifies them.

He whose well-known sacrifice is omitted should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Pathikṛt.
He goes off the path whose well-known sacrifice is omitted.
Agni is the path-maker of the gods;
he approached him with his share;
he leads him back to the path.
An ox is the sacrificial fee,
for it draws along the path.
He who, being one who has established his sacred fires, should go abroad should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Vratapati.
He violates many a vow who, being one who has established his sacred fires, goes abroad,
for on a day of vow he either approaches a woman or eats meat.
Agni is the lord of vows of the gods;
he approached him with his share;
he causes him to take hold of the vow.
He who, being one who has established his sacred fires, should shed tears should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Vratabhṛt.
He who has established his sacred fires is brought to the gods;
therefore he must not shed tears,
for the gods do not shed tears.
Agni is the bearer of vows of the gods;
his vow went to Agni;
from him he takes hold of the further vow.
One against whom sorcery is practiced should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Yaviṣṭha.
With whatever employments the other one employs [sorcery] against him, the youngest [Agni] keeps them away from him.
In battle he should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Vājasṛt.
He desires to win the prize who wishes to win a battle;
Agni is the prize-runner of the gods;
he approached him with his share;
he runs for the prize for him.
In battle he should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Anīkavant.
Whithersoever the army-face of Agni goes, that is not to be resisted;
Agni conquers the armies for him.
He should also sacrifice with ghee for Viṣṇu.
From this Viṣṇu conquered these worlds;
following the conquest of Viṣṇu, he conquers these worlds;
he thrusts the rival away from these worlds.
He who should desire, 'May I shut up his cattle for Rudra', should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Rudravant.
Agni is Rudra;
for Rudra he shuts up his cattle.
If he should desire, 'May it be appeased', he should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Surabhimat.
This is Agni's healing body, namely the fragrant one;
he made medicine for him;
he made him fragrant;
he indeed appeases [it].
He who should desire, 'May I be possessed of food, an eater of food, and a lord of food', should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Annavant, Annāda, and Annapati.
Agni is possessed of food, an eater of food, and a lord of food among the gods;
he approached him with his share;
he makes him possessed of food, an eater of food, and a lord of food.

'O Ādityas, I shall make a portion for you; bring back so-and-so, son of so-and-so,' he should say when about to offer the oblation.
He should offer this caru to the Ādityas in ghee.
These creatures belong to the Ādityas;
he has approached them;
they bring him back.
Seven pegs of aśvattha-wood should lie within the altar.
At the completion [of the rite], he should drive them through into the middle pole of the chariot-stand:
'Here I bind the Ādityas until the return of so-and-so.'
Thus he binds the Ādityas;
they, seeking release, bring him back.
The virility of the common people departed;
it entered the aśvattha-tree;
by that virility it became quivering.
Thus by this he secures the virility of the common people.
If he should not return in seven [days], having placed them in the kindling-wood, he should offer this very oblation.
These creatures belong to the Ādityas;
he has approached them;
they bring him back.
If in seven [days],
twenty-one are the sevenfold Ādityas;
he has approached them;
they bring him back.
If he should return in one [group of seven] or in two, they would be liable to expel him.
Now, since he returns in all, he returns as one who cannot be expelled.
When he has returned, then he should offer a caru in ghee to the sustaining Ādityas.
The Ādityas are the expellers;
the Ādityas are the returners;
they sustain him.
'O Aditi, assent, true wish, here the mind!' with this [formula] he should take up the footprint of the expelled king.
Having gone around with the wind to the assembly-hall of him who would be a village-spokesman in the east, they should scatter it:
'Go forth, O self-strong Maruts, with this lord of the clan to yonder king!' with this [formula] they should bring rice from his house.
He should separate it threefold.
Those [grains] which are black, he should tie them up in a black antelope skin and put them down.
Those which are white, he should offer as this caru to the Ādityas in ghee.
These creatures belong to Aditi;
he has approached them;
they bring him back.
Then those [grains] from which he separated them, having gone north, having broken up a mound of earth from an anthill, he should sacrifice:
'What in your terrible mouth today I sacrifice, for the release of these bonds, / you whom people foolishly rejoice in as earth, / I know you completely as Nirṛti.'
He who is expelled is seized by Nirṛti;
thereby he releases him from Nirṛti.
In this way he has returned to the common people;
now his kingship has not returned.
Therefore, those grains of rice which are black, he should offer them as a caru to Varuṇa in ghee.
Varuṇa is the king among the gods;
he is the returner of kingship;
he has approached him with his share;
he brings him back to kingship.
This indeed belongs to diverse clans;
by this he returns to both kinds of clans, the divine and the human.

One who desires spiritual luster should offer a caru of white rice in ghee to Sūrya.
There should be a gold ornament of a hundred measures below;
there should be a golden ornament of a hundred measures above.
Yonder sun indeed was here;
having grasped him with these two, they placed him above these creatures.
Sitting above, yonder sun eats these creatures.
This [earth] is the silver one; that [sky] is the golden one.
Grasping him with these two, he places him above these creatures.
Sitting above, he eats these creatures.
He should also offer five kṛṣṇalas at the fore-offerings.
By these [five], yonder sun is endowed with splendor along these five seasons;
he makes him endowed with splendor along these five quarters.
One who desires long life should offer a caru of a hundred kṛṣṇalas in ghee to Prajāpati.
The gods, having slain the Asuras, feared death;
those gods ran to Prajāpati.
Prajāpati caused them to sacrifice with this [offering];
then the gods went to immortality.
Gold indeed is immortality;
long life is immortality;
with immortality having attained [it], he placed immortal life in them.
One who desires long life should cause him to sacrifice with this [offering].
This indeed is the immortality of a mortal, that he attains his full lifespan.
Gold indeed is immortality;
long life is immortality;
with immortality having attained [it], he places immortal life in him;
by that he attains his full lifespan;
he does not die before his full lifespan.
It consists of a hundred kṛṣṇalas;
man has a hundred years of life and a hundredfold virility;
he attains life and virility.
He cuts off four kṛṣṇalas each time
for success.
He says that everything is to be carried around to the Brahman priest;
he establishes the whole sacrifice in the Brahman priest;
the Brahman priest places all virility in him.
Moreover, the Brahman priest is the Brahman;
through the Brahman the Brahman priest places long life in him.

One who desires the office of purohita should offer a caru to Bṛhaspati.
Its yājyā and anuvākyā to Bṛhaspati should be luminous.
Bṛhaspati is indeed the Brahman;
the Brahmin is of Bṛhaspati by deity.
He has approached his own deity for the office of purohita;
his own deity causes him to attain the office of purohita.
That which wavers and shines out, that is light;
therefore [the verses] are luminous.
If he should not quite attain the office of purohita, he should offer an oblation to Indra and Bṛhaspati.
He has made the Brahman and the Kṣatra yoked together;
they immediately appoint him as purohita.
One who desires a village or desires cattle should offer a caru to Bṛhaspati in milk.
Its yājyā and anuvākyā to Bṛhaspati should contain [the word] 'group' (gaṇa).
They should bring milk from the house of him who is prosperous in many men.
It should be the milk of their own cows;
it should be water;
it is prosperity that is thus gathered.
Bṛhaspati is indeed the Brahman;
Bṛhaspati is the meters;
through the meters Bṛhaspati is accompanied by a group;
he thus presents his own deity with the most [abundant];
he makes him accompanied by a group of kinsmen.
He should offer a caru to Brahmaṇaspati in battle.
Its yājyā and anuvākyā to Brahmaṇaspati should contain [the words] 'hero' (vīra) and 'vital power' (vayas).
He should cause him to sacrifice where the roof [of the enemy's dwelling] is seen.
The prastara should have its barhis pointing upward;
the enclosing-sticks should have arrows;
birds enter the other village—thus it is to be understood: 'We shall conquer.'
Brahmaṇaspati is indeed the Brahman;
he has approached the Brahman itself.
He should offer a caru to Brahmaṇaspati where he desires: 'May there be the power of the Brahman.'
Its yājyā and anuvākyā to Brahmaṇaspati should be accompanied by the Maruts.
Brahmaṇaspati is indeed the Brahman;
the common people are the Maruts;
he thus establishes the common people upon the Brahman,
for firmness, for non-slackness.

One who desires cattle should offer a caru of garmut-grass to Bṛhaspati.
Prajāpati created cattle;
created from him, they went away.
Bṛhaspati followed them;
where they settled, there garmut-grass arose from the dung.
He said to him:
'Cause me to sacrifice with this.'
He caused him to sacrifice with it;
cattle turned toward him.
One who desires cattle should cause him to sacrifice with this [offering];
cattle indeed turn toward him;
for when he caused him to sacrifice with this, then cattle turned toward him.
One who desires cattle should offer a caru of garmut-grass to Soma and Pūṣan.
Prajāpati created cattle;
created from him, they went away.
He made Pūṣan their overlord;
where they settled, there garmut-grass arose from the dung.
He brought it:
'By this [is] my support.'
Soma said:
'What ripens without tilling is mine.'
He offered it to Soma and Pūṣan;
cattle turned toward him.
One who desires cattle should cause him to sacrifice with this [offering];
cattle indeed turn toward him;
for when he caused him to sacrifice with this, then cattle turned toward him.
Soma is indeed the placer of semen;
Pūṣan is the procreator of cattle;
Soma places semen in him;
Pūṣan procreates cattle.
One who desires cattle should offer a caru of garmut-grass to Prajāpati.
It should be in the milk of speckled cows; the ghee should be of speckled cows.
They should also sprinkle cow's urine into it.
When they milked the speckled [cow], that became garmut-grass.
This [earth] or speech is the speckled one;
its head is the garmut-grass;
therefore it is like an egg, like biestings.
One who desires cattle should offer that caru to Prajāpati.
Cattle belong to Prajāpati;
Prajāpati is the procreator of cattle;
he has approached him with his own share;
he procreates cattle for him.
He should offer a caru made of vāstva-grain to Rudra where Rudra afflicts creatures.
Vāstva is born of the site (vāstu);
what is made of vāstva belongs indeed to Rudra;
with his own share he appeases him.
He should cause a Niṣāda chieftain to sacrifice with it;
for that is his iṣṭi;
a hornless or a lop-eared donkey is the dakṣiṇā.

One who desires, 'May I be the highest (parameṣṭhin),' should offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to Parameṣṭhin.
Parameṣṭhin indeed is he among the gods who is highest;
the Rājanya is the highest among men.
He approaches him with his own share;
he makes him the highest.
He should hand him a bow and three [arrows, saying]: 'May Agni bestow splendor upon you; may Indra [bestow] indriyá-power, ancestral kinship.'
Agni indeed bestows splendor upon him;
Indra [bestows] indriyá-power, ancestral kinship.
One who has a rival should offer [a cake] on twelve potsherds to the All-gods.
Having placed it upon the barhis, he should divide it in the middle with the wooden sword, [saying]: 'Here do I divide myself and such-and-such,' [naming] the one whom he hates.
What is crushed down and what adheres to the wooden sword, he should cut off for Viṣṇu of Wide Strides.
Viṣṇu indeed strides between them.
He should sweep it back together, [saying]: 'Here do I sweep together myself and such-and-such,' [naming] the one who is dear to him.
These creatures are indeed belonging to the All-gods;
with them he divides off the other;
with them he unites himself.
When an army is setting out, he should offer a caru to Indrāṇī.
Indrāṇī indeed is the army;
by means of the Brahman he sharpens it from the front, from the mouth, for victory.
There should also be balbaja-grass in the kindling-wood;
these [blades of grass] have indeed arisen from dung;
he leads it forth in the proper manner.
Whatever he may obtain in that army, that is the sacrificial gift.
One who desires prosperity should offer a caru to Vācaspati.
He who is the overseer of speech, he is Vācaspati;
that is indeed a means of producing prosperity.

One whose gold is lost, or who finds gold, should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni, a caru to Savitṛ, gruel to Vāyu—or fresh milk—[and a cake] on one potsherd to Bhūmi.
Agni indeed found gold in the beginning;
he consulted with Savitṛ;
there is indeed nothing that is unapproached by Vāyu;
upon this [earth] he found it.
The [offering] to Agni is for acquisition;
the one to Savitṛ is for impulsion;
the one to Vāyu is for bringing [it] forth.
Then, as to the one to Bhūmi:
upon her indeed he found it.
These indeed are the givers;
he approaches them with their share;
they bestow [it] upon him.
When he finds [it], then he should offer to these very ones;
he gives a share to those very ones by whom he found it,
for harmlessness.
Prajāpati gave his daughters, the lunar mansions (nakṣatras), to King Soma.
He dwelled only with Rohiṇī, not with the others.
Not being approached, they returned [to their father].
He seized him with consumption (rājayakṣma);
he wasted away.
Therefore, one seized by consumption wastes away.
He ran up to Prajāpati;
Prajāpati said to him:
'Speak the truth.'
He spoke the truth:
'So that I may dwell equally among all.'
Therefore he dwells equally among all.
Prajāpati sought an expiation for him;
he offered a caru to the All-gods for him on the new-moon day;
thereby he found an expiation for him.
He swelled along with that one [the moon] swelling;
he became better off.
For one who is seized by consumption, he should offer a caru to the All-gods on the new-moon day.
By this [offering] he indeed found an expiation for him;
by it he finds an expiation for him.
He swells along with that one swelling;
he becomes better off.

Prajāpati distributed the shares to the gods.
Indra said:
'Whatever is left over, that is mine.'
What was left over was indriyá-power.
It rose upward along these worlds.
He did not reach it with one [cake];
nor with two;
having reached it with the third, he obtained it.
In that there are three cakes,
having reached the indriyá-power and virility from these worlds, he obtains [them].
Each succeeding cake is larger;
for each succeeding world is greater.
The first is for Indra the King;
the middle one is for Indra the Self-ruler;
the last is for Indra the Overlord.
Indra became all these: kingship, self-rule, overlordship.
He who sacrifices with these becomes all these;
he who knows thus becomes all this.
Having recited the invitatory verse of the first, he should sacrifice with the offering verse of the middle one;
having recited that of the middle one, he should sacrifice with that of the last;
having recited that of the last, he should sacrifice with that of the first.
In this way, all [verses] become invitatory verses for him, all become offering verses,
for the recognition of these worlds,
and also for continuous connection.
One desiring spiritual luster should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra accompanied by the Gharma-vessel, accompanied by the Sun.
The Gharma-vessel is splendor;
the Sun is splendor;
he obtains splendor itself.
One desiring cattle should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra accompanied by indriyá-power.
Cattle are indriyá-power;
Indra is the giver of indriyá-power;
he approaches him with his share;
he bestows indriyá-power and cattle upon him.

One should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra accompanied by the Arka, accompanied by the horse sacrifice (Aśvamedha);
he should make an expelled [chieftain] sacrifice.
He who is expelled has indeed reached the end;
the Arka is the end;
the horse sacrifice is the end;
by means of the end, he provides for him at the end.
A barren cow is the sacrificial fee,
[thinking:] 'May he bring [them] into subjection to me.'
One who desires, 'May I prosper among people,' should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra accompanied by the Arka, accompanied by the horse sacrifice.
People are indeed the end of prosperities;
the Arka is the end;
the horse sacrifice is the end;
by means of the end, he provides for him at the end.
A barren cow is the sacrificial fee,
[thinking:] 'May he bring [them] into subjection to me.'
One who has attained fortune should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra accompanied by the Arka, accompanied by the horse sacrifice.
When one reaches the end of fortune, he then becomes worse off;
the Arka is the end;
the horse sacrifice is the end;
he takes hold of the end itself;
he does not become worse off.
One who desires, 'May a great sacrifice fall to my lot,' should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra accompanied by the Arka, accompanied by the horse sacrifice.
These indeed are the two sacrificial bodies of Indra: the Arka and the horse sacrifice;
he takes hold of those two;
by means of them, he takes hold of the great sacrifice.
He should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra accompanied by the Gharma-vessel, accompanied by the Sun;
[a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra accompanied by wrath, accompanied by mind;
[a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra accompanied by indriyá-power;
[a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra accompanied by the Arka, accompanied by the horse sacrifice;
he should make one desiring prosperity sacrifice.
This [earth] of Indra is the Gharma-vessel and the Sun;
this is his wrath and mind;
this is his Indra and indriyá-power;
this is his Arka and horse sacrifice.
Indra became all these;
he who sacrifices with these becomes all these;
he who knows thus becomes all this.

One should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra, the Deliverer from Distress (Aṃhomuc);
he should make a diseased person sacrifice.
This indeed is Indra's healing body: the Deliverer from Distress;
he approaches him with his share;
he releases him from distress.
One who fears losing his possessions, death, or expulsion should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra the Protector (Trātṛ).
Indra is the protector;
Indra is the expeller;
he approaches him with his share;
he protects him.
A senior kinsman should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra who keeps [men] upright (Anvṛju).
The senior kinsman is indriyá-power;
by means of indriyá-power, he makes them upright.
In battle, one should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra the Roaring (Prababhra).
Indra, roaring, hurled the thunderbolt at Vṛtra;
he hurls the thunderbolt roaring against them.
In battle, one should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra the Dispeller of Foes (Vaimṛdha).
He who wins a battle smites away the foes;
he smites away the foes.
In battle, one should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra the Slayer of Assailants (Abhimātihan).
He who wins a battle slays assailants;
he slays assailants.
One who desires, 'May I overpower [them]; may I have safety,' should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra the Overpowerer of Assailants (Abhimātiṣāh).
Indra raised the thunderbolt against Vṛtra;
the Dānavas did not yield to him;
they yielded to him with this share;
then he slew the assailants;
he slew Vṛtra.
He who wins a battle indeed slays Vṛtra;
therefore, this is Vṛtra-slaying.

One should offer [a cake] on one potsherd to Indra;
he should make an expelled [chieftain] sacrifice.
'Come forward from the furthest distance'—these should be the invitiation and offering verses.
He who is expelled has indeed gone to the distance;
having reached him from the distance, he brings him back.
One should offer [a cake] on thirteen potsherds to Indra;
he should make an expelled [chieftain] sacrifice.
The thirteenth is redundant;
the expelled one is redundant;
from what is redundant, having reached him who is redundant, he brings him back.
One should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra the Thunderbolt-bearer;
[a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra the Vṛtra-slayer;
[a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra the Overcomer of Vṛtra, [for one] whose rival sacrifices with Soma.
He who sacrifices with Soma raises a thunderbolt against his rival.
In that it is for the Thunderbolt-bearer,
by means of the thunderbolt he lays low his thunderbolt.
In that it is for the Vṛtra-slayer,
his rival is indeed Vṛtra;
he slays him.
In that it is for the Overcomer of Vṛtra,
his rival is indeed Vṛtra;
he overcomes him.
One who disputes over land or cattle should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra the Conqueror of the Field (Kṣetrañjaya).
Indra is the conqueror of the field among the gods;
he approaches him with his share;
he conquers the field and cattle for him.
Where kings are nearly equal, one should offer [a cake] on eleven potsherds to Indra the Overlord (Adhirāja).
Indra is the overlord among the gods;
he approaches him with his share;
he brings him to paramount rule.

For him over whose sānnāyya the moon rises [early], he should divide the [grains] fit for the sacrificial cake into three parts.
Those that are medium-sized, he should offer [as a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni the Giver (Dātṛ).
Those that are largest, [as] a caru in sour milk to Indra the Bestower (Pradātṛ).
Those that are smallest, [as] a caru in boiled milk to Viṣṇu Śipiviṣṭa.
Agni is the giver of the medium;
Indra is the bestower of the greatest;
and that which is smallest, that is Śipiviṣṭa;
he attains that—
cattle indeed.
Soma is left over for him over whose sānnāyya the moon rises;
he is left over for cattle.
One who desires cattle, having sacrificed at the new moon, should drive away the calves.
He should divide the [grains] fit for the sacrificial cake into three parts.
Those that are smallest, he should offer [as a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni Possessed of Gifts (Sanimant).
Those that are medium-sized, [as] a caru in boiled milk to Viṣṇu Śipiviṣṭa.
Those that are largest, [as] a caru in sour milk to Indra the Bestower.
Agni finds for him that which is here;
Viṣṇu, that which is in the intermediate space;
Indra, that which is in heaven.
Warriors seek cattle;
in that these grains of rice are divided,
these warriors are the seekers,
they are the blockers.
One who has attained fortune should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni;
a milch cow is its sacrificial fee.
Having moved eastwards, [he offers a cake] on three potsherds to Viṣṇu;
a milch mare is its sacrificial fee.
Having moved eastwards, having gone to a mountain or waters, [he offers] a caru in ghee to Prajāpati;
a female slave is its sacrificial fee.
In that there is [a cake] to Agni, he strides onto this [earth] by it;
in that there is one to Viṣṇu, onto the intermediate space by it;
in that there is one to Prajāpati, onto that world by it.
In that he moves eastwards, he steps onto [them];
in that he goes to a mountain or waters, he reaches the end.
In that there are three milch animals as sacrificial fee—
these worlds are three;
he makes these worlds milch cows for him;
he makes these worlds yield to him.
These worlds, having yielded, milk for him who knows thus.
For one who vomits Soma, he should offer a caru of millet to Soma and Indra (Somā-Indra).
Indra drank the Soma of Tvaṣṭṛ uninvited;
his upward drink of Soma fell down;
those became millets.
He who vomits Soma is deprived of the drink of Soma.
In that it is to Soma,
he unites him with the drink of Soma.
He who vomits Soma is deprived of indriyá-power and virility.
In that it is to Indra,
he unites him with indriyá-power and virility.
For the drink of Soma in him has become loose, as it were;
then he vomits Soma.
In that he mixes it with millet grains,
[it is] for the retention of the drink of Soma.

One should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni, [and] a curds-mixture to Mitra and Varuṇa;
he should make a diseased person sacrifice.
The [cake] to Agni is for success;
also, having made him possessed of bones, he establishes him.
The curds-mixture, indeed, if unappeased without an animal [sacrifice], is liable to wipe [the sacrificer] away.
An animal also should be seized,
for appeasement, that there may be no wiping away.
These are animals, namely rice and barley;
in that the sacrificial cake is made of rice,
by that alone the animal is seized,
for appeasement, that there may be no wiping away.
For a brahmin, it should be to Mitra and Varuṇa;
for the brahmin is of the nature of Mitra and Varuṇa by divinity.
For a rājanya, it should be to Indra and Varuṇa;
for the rājanya is of the nature of Indra and Varuṇa by divinity.
For a vaiśya, it should be to Agni and Varuṇa;
Agni is all the deities;
here is the divinity of the vaiśya also.
He should make a diseased person sacrifice;
he has approached his own deity with an expiation.
Seized by Varuṇa indeed is he who is diseased;
from Varuṇa he thereby releases him.
Man is milk; it is the milk of this [man] that is sick;
by means of milk he buys back his milk.
When he pushes apart,
for cutting open, they extract the arrow-point from the center;
thereby it is the consumption that they extract from his center.
Then, when having swept [it] together again he cuts off portions together for Agni, just as having extracted the arrow-point they bind it with a turban, so is that. He should make one desiring prosperity sacrifice;
he has approached his own deity with an expiation.
Seized by Varuṇa indeed is he who desires prosperity;
from Varuṇa he thereby releases him.
Man is milk;
he who desires prosperity desires milk;
by means of milk he secures milk for him.
When he pushes apart,
[it is] for the protection of the sacrifice.
Then, when he sweeps together again,
with prosperity he sweeps him together.
He should make one desiring a village sacrifice;
he has approached his own deity with an expiation.
Seized by Varuṇa indeed is he who desires a village;
from Varuṇa he thereby releases him.
Man is milk;
he who desires a village desires milk;
by means of milk he secures milk for him.
When he pushes apart,
[it is] for the protection of the sacrifice.
Then, when he sweeps together again,
with a village he sweeps him together.
He offers [cakes] on one potsherd;
here and there indeed are Varuṇa's nooses;
from them he releases him.
If he were to offer separately, that would be a dismemberment of the sacrifice.
All are to be offered in the fire,
for success.
'That body of yours, O Mitra and Varuṇa, which is full of vigor, with that we would worship you;
with that do release this so-and-so from distress!
That body of yours, O Mitra and Varuṇa, which is full of strength, with that we would worship you;
with that do release this so-and-so from distress!
That body of yours, O Mitra and Varuṇa, which is sorcerous, with that we would worship you;
with that do release this so-and-so from distress!
That body of yours, O Mitra and Varuṇa, which is demonic, with that we would worship you;
with that do release this so-and-so from distress!
That body of yours, O Mitra and Varuṇa, which is full of vigor, full of strength, sorcerous, demonic, with that we have worshipped you;
with that have released this so-and-so from distress!
That noose of yours, O King Varuṇa, which is among the gods, that of yours I avert with this [offering];
to that [noose] of yours, svāhā!
That noose of yours, O King Varuṇa, which is in food, that of yours I avert with this [offering];
to that [noose] of yours, svāhā!
That noose of yours, O King Varuṇa, which is among two-footed [and] four-footed cattle, that of yours I avert with this [offering];
to that [noose] of yours, svāhā!
That noose of yours, O King Varuṇa, which is in plants, in trees, in the waters, on the earth, in the directions, that of yours I avert with this [offering];
to that [noose] of yours, svāhā!'
These indeed are Varuṇa's nooses;
from them he releases him.

One who has rivals should offer a caru to the Viśve Devas.
The gods and the Asuras were contending.
Those gods sacrificed with the Saṃgrahaṇa [rite].
Whatever property of the Asuras there was, they gathered it together.
Indeed, it was their minds that they gathered together.
Deprived of mind, they were defeated.
One who has rivals should sacrifice [with this].
This, truly, is the grasping of minds.
He thereby grasps the minds of his rivals;
deprived of mind, they are defeated.
One who desires a village should sacrifice [with this].
This, truly, is the grasping of minds.
He thereby grasps the minds of his kinsmen;
grasped in mind, they do not depart from him.
They should bring clarified butter from the house of all the kinsmen.
From the houses of however many they bring clarified butter, the minds of all of them he grasps;
grasped in mind, they do not depart from him.
He offers with the āmana-mantras.
He makes them well-disposed.
'O divine Āmana, whichever kinsmen are like-minded, those I desire in my heart;
may they desire me in their heart;
make them well-disposed to me, svāhā!
O divine Āmana, whichever sons are like-minded, those I desire in my heart;
may they desire me in their heart;
make them well-disposed to me, svāhā!
O divine Āmana, whichever women are like-minded, those I desire in my heart;
may they desire me in their heart;
make them well-disposed to me, svāhā!
O divine Āmana, whichever cattle are like-minded, those I desire in my heart;
may they desire me in their heart;
make them well-disposed to me, svāhā!'
These are indeed the kinsmen: like kinsmen, like sons, like women, like cattle.
He unites himself with them;
he becomes [prosperous] through them.
A speckled milch cow is the dakṣiṇā,
for she belongs to the Viśve Devas.
Then, as to its being an offering to the Viśve Devas:
these creatures belong to the Viśve Devas;
he secures them;
he makes them [his] food.
The fore-offerings and after-offerings are outside the self;
the deity is the self.
If he were to offer either before or after the fore-offerings and after-offerings, he would place his kinsmen outside himself.
Then, in that he offers in the middle,
he places the kinsmen right within himself.
If he should desire: 'May they come quickly, may they depart quickly,' he should offer with a wooden [ladle];
for trees are moving and unmoving.
If he should desire: 'May they be firm, may they depart only with difficulty,' he should offer with an earthen [vessel];
for this [earth] is firm.
'Firm you are;
be firm among the gods;
may I be firm among my kinsmen, dear to my kinsmen, fierce, observant, finder of wealth!
Fierce you are;
be fierce among the gods;
may I be fierce among my kinsmen, dear to my kinsmen, fierce, observant, finder of wealth!
Overcoming you are;
be overcoming among the gods;
may I be overcoming among my kinsmen, dear to my kinsmen, fierce, observant, finder of wealth!
Encompassing you are;
be encompassing among the gods;
may I be encompassing among my kinsmen, dear to my kinsmen, fierce, observant, finder of wealth!
Wise you are;
be wise among the gods;
may I be wise among my kinsmen, dear to my kinsmen, fierce, observant, finder of wealth!'
These are the kinsmen;
he establishes them in him;
he makes them non-departing from him.

Now this horse is accepted [as a gift].
When accepted, being toothed on both jaws, it consumes his indriya-power and his cattle.
Varuṇa indeed is the horse, belonging to the deity Varuṇa.
Whoever accepts a horse falls into Varuṇa's power.
Therefore an offering of a horse-oblation is to be performed,
for being free from Varuṇa.
They are on four potsherds;
four-footed, indeed, is the horse;
by the potsherds he obtains it.
As many horses as there are, so many are the sacrificial cakes;
he frees him entirely.
There is one extra;
for that which he does not remember, for that is it.
He who is going to accept [a horse] again should not sacrifice [yet];
for if he were to accept again, he would fall into Varuṇa's power again.
Now, if he is going to accept again, half of his [cakes] should be to Varuṇa, half to Sūrya and Varuṇa.
In that [one cake] is to Varuṇa,
by that he frees him from Varuṇa.
Then in that [the other] is to Sūrya,
he receives it close to his own deity,
for the non-injury of himself.
There are twenty-one sāmidhenī verses;
yonder sun is the twenty-first;
yonder sun is Prajāpati;
the horse belongs to Prajāpati;
as great as the horse is, him he obtains.
All other animals have a womb, have a human womb;
the horse is womb-less, born of the waters.
In that this caru to Apāṃ Napāt is [offered],
he has given him a womb;
he establishes him in his own womb.
'Whatever noose of yours, O King Varuṇa, of gāyatrī meter, is established in the brāhmaṇa, that [noose] of yours I avert with this offering; to that [noose] of yours, svāhā!
Whatever noose of yours, O King Varuṇa, of triṣṭubh meter, is established in the kṣatra, that [noose] of yours I avert with this offering; to that [noose] of yours, svāhā!
Whatever noose of yours, O King Varuṇa, of jagatī meter, is established in the common people, that [noose] of yours I avert with this offering; to that [noose] of yours, svāhā!
Whatever noose of yours, O King Varuṇa, of anuṣṭubh meter, is established in the quarters, that [noose] of yours I avert with this offering; to that [noose] of yours, svāhā!'
The meters are indeed the nooses of Varuṇa;
with them he seizes him;
with those by which he seizes him, with them he releases him.
He offers with two verses to Apāṃ Napāt;
from whatever Varuṇa is watery, from that he frees him.

You are the life of Agni;
by that give life to him, to so-and-so.
You are the breath of Indra;
give breath to so-and-so, whose breath you are, svāhā!
You are the breath of the Fathers;
give breath to so-and-so, whose breath you are, svāhā!
You are the breath of the All-gods;
give breath to so-and-so, whose breath you are, svāhā!
You are the breath of Bṛhaspati;
give breath to so-and-so, whose breath you are, svāhā!
You are the breath of Prajāpati;
give breath to so-and-so, whose breath you are, svāhā!
What came as fresh became fresh butter; what crept became clarified butter;
what was sprinkled became ghee.
The path of ghee, the navel of immortality, given by Indra, offered by the Maruts;
that Viṣṇu saw, that Iḍā gavyā stirred up.
By the chant of the Pāvamāna, on the course of the Gāyatrī, by the power of the Upāṃśu [cup] I release you;
by the Bṛhat [and] the Rathantara, on the course of the Triṣṭubh, by the power of the Śukra [cup] I draw you up;
by the measure of Agni, on the course of the Jagatī, may god Savitṛ raise you up for life, for living!
This luster given by Agni has come, great bounty, strength, vigor, power;
for long life, lasting a hundred autumns, I receive [it], for great indriya-power.
Make this man, O Agni, fit for life, for luster, for sharp vigor, O Varuṇa, O King Soma!
Grant him protection like a mother, O Aditi, O All-gods, so that he may reach old age!
Agni is life;
his life-makers are men;
by that life be possessed of life!
Brahman is life;
its life-makers are brāhmaṇas;
by that life be possessed of life!
The sacrifice is life;
its life-makers are the sacrificial gifts;
by that life be possessed of life!
Immortality is life;
its life-makers are the gods;
by that life be possessed of life!
You are the breath of the Aśvins;
may they two give you breath;
live by that!
You are the breath of Mitra and Varuṇa;
may they two give you breath;
live by that!
You are the breath of Bṛhaspati;
may he give you breath;
live by that!
You are the breath of Prajāpati the highest;
may he give you breath;
live by that!

He should offer a cake on eleven potsherds to Agni and Viṣṇu, a caru to Sarasvatī, [and] a caru to Bṛhaspati.
On the day before, he should make the diseased person sacrifice.
Agni, indeed, is all the deities;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice.
By the deities and by the sacrifice he places life in him.
Speech is Sarasvatī;
Brahman is Bṛhaspati.
By speech and by Brahman he places life in him.
Moreover, the deities are the breaths;
having seized the breaths on the day before, he fasts.
On the next day, he should offer a cake on eight potsherds to Agni, a caru in milk to Soma, a caru in ghee to Aditi, a caru made of barley to Varuṇa, [and] this [cake] on twelve potsherds to Agni Vaiśvānara;
he should make the diseased person sacrifice.
When someone dies, his body goes to Agni, [his] sap to Soma.
In that there is the [cake] to Agni,
he ransoms his body by that;
in that there is the [offering] to Soma,
[he ransoms his] sap by that;
having ransomed as much as he is—
Aditi, indeed, is this [earth];
upon her creatures are born;
upon her he brings him to birth.
There is a caru of this size;
such indeed is the self;
having freed as much as his self is from Varuṇa—
Agni Vaiśvānara, indeed, is the year;
he establishes him in the year;
he makes him possess the lifespan of a year.
[He says:] 'You are the life of Agni; by that give life to him, to so-and-so.'
Agni, indeed, is the giver of life to men;
he bestows life on him.
He offers with five [oblations];
fivefold is man;
as much as man is, he sets him in motion;
having set him in motion as much as man is—
This one indeed who purifies himself is breath;
he blows over from these quarters;
these quarters belong to these deities;
having set him in motion according to the deity from these quarters, he places the breaths in him.
'What came as fresh became fresh butter; what crept became clarified butter; what was sprinkled became ghee,' [he says];
he thereby proclaims the greatness of ghee;
also, he magnifies it.
'By the chant of the Pāvamāna, on the course of the Gāyatrī, by the power of the Upāṃśu [cup] I release you,' he says;
just as one releases, draws up, [and] raises one who is condemned to death, so is that.
As much as there are chants, cups, and meters,
by as much as there is, he places life in him;
by the sacrifice he places life in him.
All the priests recite around him;
all of them, indeed, heal this man;
all of them place life in him.
Taking hold of the Brahman priest's hand, they recite around him;
the Brahman priest is Brahman;
by Brahman alone he places Brahman-life in him.
They make him drink ghee off gold;
gold, indeed, is immortality;
ghee is life;
from immortality they make him drink life; he sucks it out, as it were;
he places life in himself.
He binds it upon him;
he makes him prosper with life.
With a verse to Agni he binds it on;
Agni, indeed, is all the deities;
by all the deities he places life in him.
'Agni is life; his life-makers are men; by that life be possessed of life,' [he says];
he who knows the gods as possessed of life and as life-makers reaches a full lifespan, [and] does not die before his full lifespan.
These deities, indeed, are possessed of life and life-makers, namely these breaths;
they place life in him.
'You are the breath of the Aśvins; may they two give you breath; live by that,' [he says];
these breaths, indeed, are those;
he places them in him;
he makes them non-departing from him.
Ten [cows] are to be given;
for ten are the breaths in the self;
he places the breaths in him.
A horse is to be given;
a garment is to be given;
gold is to be given;
an ox is to be given;
a boon is to be given;
much is to be given.

He should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni, [and a cake] on five potsherds to Indra.
Curd, honey, clarified butter, parched grain, [and] water: that is mixed together.
There is a caru for Aryaman.
The [cake] to Agni is for prosperity.
The [cake] to Indra is on five potsherds;
fivefold is the sacrifice;
fivefold are the offering-mantra and invitatory-mantra;
fivefold are cattle;
fivefold is man.
As great as man is, him he obtains;
having become complete, he obtains cattle.
For this mixture is like cattle;
moreover, as many cattle as there are, those he unites for him.
Regarding this they say:
"[The cake] to Indra should be made on eleven potsherds,
for cattle belong to Indra."
Moreover they say:
"It should be made for Prajāpati,
for cattle belong to Prajāpati;
Prajāpati is the progenitor of cattle;
him indeed he approaches with his share;
he propagates cattle for him."
Now this caru is for Aryaman.
He who gives is Aryaman;
giving is Aryaman;
subjects desiring to give come to be his.
He should offer [a cake] on eight potsherds to brilliant Agni, a caru to Sūrya, [and a cake] on eight potsherds to brilliant Agni.
He should make one desiring sight sacrifice [it].
By the sight of Agni people see by night, by [the sight] of Sūrya by day.
These two indeed are the givers of sight;
these two indeed he approaches with their share;
these two bestow sight upon him.
"May he bestow equal sight."
Because the same deity is on both sides of the caru,
he establishes distinct eyes for him.
As much as he sees with one eye, so much he would see with both, if the caru were not in between.
Now because the caru is in between,
therefore this [space] is between the two eyes,
for separation.
White rice grains are [used]; they milk white cows for the clarified butter.
For yonder Āditya is like this,
for success.
It is in milk;
milk indeed is clarified butter;
milk is sight;
by milk indeed he places milk [and] sight in him.
He places [them] with [verses] to Sūrya;
he places sight in him.

One desiring cattle should seize a brown, shaggy, tawny [animal] sacred to Soma.
Plants are sacred to Soma;
cattle are plants.
In that it is sacred to Soma,
he visibly seizes an animal for him.
It is shaggy;
this is indeed the form of prosperity;
he secures prosperity alone.
It is brown, tawny;
[it is] the form of Soma, for success.
One desiring cattle should seize one born last of triplets [sacred] to Soma and Pūṣan.
Soma indeed is the depositor of semen;
Pūṣan is the progenitor of cattle.
Soma alone deposits semen for him;
Pūṣan begets cattle.
Two of these are born to a teat, the third to vigor;
vigor is indeed cattle;
by vigor alone, having obtained vigor, cattle, he secures [them].
Thrice indeed in a year this one gives birth alongside other cattle;
this is indeed the form of prosperity;
he secures prosperity alone.
Some creatures have a share, others have no share;
in that the sacrificial post is of udumbara-wood,
he makes both kinds share-holding.
Month by month this [tree] ripens inside, [different] from other trees;
this is indeed the form of prosperity;
he secures prosperity alone.
One desiring cattle should seize a hornless [animal] sacred to Prajāpati.
Cattle indeed belong to Prajāpati;
Prajāpati is the progenitor of cattle.
Him alone he approached with his portion;
he begets cattle for him.
Prajāpati is indeed the womb;
from the womb he is born.
This [animal] corresponds to the forms of all cattle:
like a man's, its beard;
like a horse's, its head;
like an ass's, its ears;
like a dog's, its hairs;
like a cow's, its forefeet;
like a sheep's, its hind [feet];
it is indeed a goat;
having obtained all the forms of cattle, he secures [them];
all forms of cattle attend upon him.
The libation is accompanied by [the verse] 'Hiraṇyagarbha';
the sāmidhenī-verses are those of Prajāpati;
the āprī-verses are those of Prajāpati.
Gold is to be given,
for the possession of seed.
A tārpya-garment is to be given,
for having the same origin.
An outer garment is to be given;
by that he obtains and secures the forms of the sacrifice.
With this indeed Upakeru prospered;
he prospers who sacrifices with this.
Twelvefold indeed he enclosed the enclosure;
there he gave twelve boons each time.
That twelve are given is the copy of that.
He should seize a white [animal] for Vāyu;
he should make one desiring prosperity sacrifice.
Vāyu indeed is the most vigorous, the swiftest of the gods;
he leads him to prosperity.
They say about that:
'An unsettled deity is capable of wiping away, capable of leading him to distress.'
Beyond that, that very [deity] leads him to prosperity.
He should seize a white [animal] for Vāyu with teams;
he should make one desiring a village sacrifice.
Vāyu indeed leads these creatures about, tethered through the nose, thus and thus.
In that it is for Vāyu,
Vāyu alone leads to him the clan tethered through the nose.
The offering and invitatory verses are accompanied by teams;
he maintains the village in him.
It is white,
the form of sacred lore, for success.
He should seize a white [animal] for Vāyu with teams;
he should make a sick man sacrifice.
Breath indeed is Vāyu;
for breath has departed from him, and therefore he is sick.
In that it is for Vāyu,
Vāyu alone brings breath to him.
The offering and invitatory verses are accompanied by teams;
he maintains breath in him.
It is white,
the form of sacred lore, for success.
He should seize a white [animal] for Vāyu with teams;
he should make one desiring cattle sacrifice.
Breath indeed is Vāyu;
cattle run toward breath when they sniff the wind in the rains.
In that it is for Vāyu,
Vāyu alone brings cattle to him.
The offering and invitatory verses are accompanied by teams;
he maintains cattle in him.
It is white,
the form of sacred lore, for success.

Yonder sun was deprived of its splendors;
then all this became darkness.
Prajāpati saw these ten bulls;
moreover they say Indra saw them.
He seized them as sacred to Indra.
Having attained the powers and virilities, he placed [them] in him by means of them.
In that [bulls] with a white mark on the forehead are seized, by means of them he placed splendor in him from the front;
in that [bulls] are white-humped, from above by means of them;
in that [bulls] have white backs, from behind by means of them.
Then yonder sun became splendid on all sides.
One who desires splendor should seize these bulls sacred to Indra.
In that [bulls] with a white mark on the forehead are seized,
from the front by means of them he places splendor in him;
in that [bulls] are white-humped,
from above by means of them;
in that [bulls] have white backs,
from behind by means of them.
On all sides he makes him splendid;
he causes him to attain the measure of yonder sun.
He should seize a tenth [animal] sacred to Prajāpati in the twelfth month.
Twelve months are the year;
having attained the year, he wins it.
Nine are seized;
nine indeed are the vital breaths;
the vital breaths indeed are virility in man;
he places the vital breaths, virility, in him.
Ten are seized;
the Virāj has ten syllables;
having placed these powers and virilities in herself, the Virāj —
this [earth] is the Virāj;
in this very [earth] he is firmly established.
Homage to the greatness, to the eye, O father of the Maruts! Thus I sing to thee: / Offered, go by the paths traveled by the gods; be established among the plants with thy bodies!
This was the tie of the gods, the lord of waters, the bull of plants; / Pūṣan chose the drop of Soma; high was the rock when that was [so].
The drop fell down along the earth, along heaven, and along this womb and that which was prior; / pouring the drop that moves along the common womb, I follow the seven Hotṛs.
Father of the calves, lord of the cows not to be slain, and he is the father of great water-buckets; / calf, afterbirth, fresh milk, biestings, curd, whey, and clarified butter are his womb.
The cows chose thee for kingship; the Maruts of fine songs magnify thee; / seated upon the heights with the peaks of dominion, then, fierce one, distribute wealth to us!

An eight-pot-sherd [cake] sacred to Agni,
a barley mess sacred to Varuṇa,
a gavidhukā mess sacred to Rudra,
curd sacred to Indra.
A milch draft-cow is the sacrificial gift.
From the descent of the waters they fetch apāmārga plants.
Having ground them into groats, having gone away to the south, having placed a single firebrand on a natural cleft, he offers with a dipping spoon of palāśa wood:
'At the impulse of the god Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, with the might of Indra, you are the slayer of the Rakṣases, svāhā!
Slain is the Rakṣas;
we have killed the Rakṣas.'
A boon is the sacrificial gift.
'Which gods sit in the east, led by Agni, slayers of the Rakṣases, may they help us;
may they protect us;
to them, svāhā!
Which gods sit in the south, led by Yama, slayers of the Rakṣases, may they help us;
may they protect us;
to them, svāhā!
Which gods sit in the west, led by the Maruts, slayers of the Rakṣases, may they help us;
may they protect us;
to them, svāhā!
Which gods sit in the north, led by Mitra and Varuṇa, slayers of the Rakṣases, may they help us;
may they protect us;
to them, svāhā!
Which gods sit above, having food, led by Soma, slayers of the Rakṣases, may they help us;
may they protect us;
to them, svāhā!
Here I sweep together the Rakṣas.
O Agni, burn up the Rakṣas!
Burned up is the Rakṣas.
To Agni sitting in the east, svāhā!
To Yama sitting in the south, svāhā!
To the Maruts sitting in the west, svāhā!
To Mitra and Varuṇa sitting in the north, svāhā!
To Soma sitting above, having food, the slayer of Rakṣases, svāhā!'
A chariot drawn by five horses is the sacrificial gift.

A mess of boiled rice for Anumati;
a mess of boiled rice for Rākā;
a mess of boiled rice for Sinīvālī;
a mess of boiled rice for Kuhū;
a twelve-pot-sherd [cake] for Dhātṛ.
A four-year-old cow is the sacrificial gift.
An eleven-pot-sherd [cake] sacred to Agni and Viṣṇu;
a mess of boiled rice sacred to Indra and Viṣṇu;
a three-pot-sherd [cake] sacred to Viṣṇu.
A dwarf is the sacrificial gift.
An eleven-pot-sherd [cake] sacred to Soma and Pūṣan;
a mess of boiled rice sacred to Indra and Pūṣan;
a mess of boiled rice sacred to Pūṣan.
A black [animal] is the sacrificial gift.
A twelve-pot-sherd [cake] to Agni Vaiśvānara;
a barley mess sacred to Varuṇa.
Gold and a horse are the sacrificial gift.

A mess of boiled rice sacred to Bṛhaspati at the house of the Brahman;
a white-backed [animal] is the sacrificial gift.
An eleven-pot-sherd [cake] sacred to Indra at the house of the king;
a bull is the sacrificial gift.
A mess of boiled rice sacred to Aditi at the house of the chief queen;
a milch cow is the sacrificial gift.
A mess of boiled rice sacred to Nirṛti made of [grains] winnowed with nails at the house of the discarded wife;
a black-and-white hornless cow that kicks away is the sacrificial gift.
An eight-pot-sherd [cake] sacred to Agni at the house of the army commander;
gold is the sacrificial gift.
A two-pot-sherd [cake] sacred to the Aśvins at the house of the charioteer;
two twin calves are the sacrificial gift.
An eight-pot-sherd [cake] sacred to Savitṛ at the house of the distributor;
a white [animal] is the sacrificial gift.
A ten-pot-sherd barley [cake] sacred to Varuṇa at the house of the bard;
a brown, large-castrated [bull] is the sacrificial gift.
A seven-pot-sherd [cake] sacred to the Maruts at the house of the village-headman of the Vaiśya class;
a speckled four-year-old cow is the sacrificial gift.
A mess of boiled rice sacred to Pūṣan at the house of the tax collector;
a black [animal] is the sacrificial gift.
A three-pot-sherd [cake] sacred to Viṣṇu at the house of the carpenter and chariot-maker;
all [tools] of metal are the sacrificial gift.
A gavidhukā mess sacred to Rudra at the house of the dice-master and the hunter;
a sword tied with hair is the sacrificial gift, or a spotted three-year-old [bull], or a halter with a horsehair cord.

'You are the womb of dominion;
you are the caul of dominion;
you are the navel of dominion.'
'Agni the lord of the house has recognized [him];
Indra of ancient renown has recognized [him];
Mitra and Varuṇa of fixed vows have recognized [him];
Heaven and Earth, increasers of order, have recognized [him];
Pūṣan who knows all has recognized [him];
the goddess Aditi has recognized [him];
recognized is this so-and-so, descendant of so-and-so, son of so-and-so, in such-and-such a clan, for great dominion, for great lordship over the folk!'
'This is your king, O folk;
Soma is the king of us Brahmans!'
'You are Indra's bolt that slays Vṛtra;
with you may he slay Vṛtra!'
'You are repellers of enemies;
protect [him] in front;
protect [him] behind;
protect [him] across;
protect [him] along;
protect [him] above;
protect him from the quarters!'
'You are Mitra;
you are Varuṇa.'
'Golden-coloured at the dawning of the dawn, with iron pillars at the rising of the sun, / do you mount the chariot-seat, O Varuṇa and Mitra, and thence behold Aditi and Diti!'

The sacred formulation born first in the east, the eager one has unveiled from the boundary of brilliant light;
he has revealed its lowest stations, its highest, the womb of the existent and of the nonexistent.
Hiraṇyagarbha came into being in the beginning; born, he was the sole lord of what has come to be.
He supported this earth and the heaven. To what god should we pay homage with the oblation?
Gathered from the waters, and from the sap of the earth, he came into being out of Viśvakarman;
Tvaṣṭṛ comes shaping his form; that was the divine birth of the Puruṣa in the beginning.
The drop has fallen.
Homage be to the serpents, whichever are along the earth,
whichever are in the intermediate space, whichever are in heaven, to those serpents homage!
Those that are arrows of the sorcerers, those of the trees,
those that lie in pits, to those serpents homage!
Those that are in the realm of light of heaven, or those in the rays of the sun,
those that have made their seats in the waters, to those serpents homage!
Assume force as if a wide net; advance like a king with his elephant, terrible!
Following your swift net, rushing, you are a shooter: pierce the demons with your hottest [arrows]!
Your whirling [flames] fly swiftly; blazing boldly, touch them!
With heats, O Agni, with your tongue, unhindered, send forth the sparks, the meteors on every side!
Send forth your scouts, be most rapid; be an unfoiled protector of this folk!
He who speaks evil against us far off, he who speaks evil near at hand, O Agni, let none of your tormentors overpower you!
Rise up, O Agni, stretch out against [them]; burn down the adversary, O you of sharp missile!
He who has practiced hostility against us, O kindled one, burn him down low like dry brushwood!
Be upright, pierce [the foe] away from us; make manifest the divine powers, O Agni!
Slacken the taut [bows] of those impelled by sorcery; crush the kin and the non-kin, the enemies!
This Agni is the lord of thousandfold, of hundredfold prize,
the head, the poet of riches.
By the heat of Agni I set you down;
with that deity, in the manner of the Aṅgirases, sit firm!
You have become the leader of the sacrifice and of the realm of space, where you accompany with auspicious teams;
in heaven you have set your head winning the sun; your tongue, O Agni, you have made the conveyer of oblations.
By the vigor of Indra I set you down;
with that deity, in the manner of the Aṅgirases, sit firm!
Firm are you, supporting,
unthrown, well held by Viśvakarman.
May the ocean not strike you up; may the well-winged bird not [strike you]!
Unwavering, make the earth firm!
You are heat;
give me heat;
sustain the earth;
make the earth firm;
do not harm the earth;
protect me from the earth!
You are light;
give me light;
sustain the intermediate space;
make the intermediate space firm;
do not harm the intermediate space;
protect me from the intermediate space!
You are heaven;
give me heaven;
sustain the sky;
make the sky firm;
do not harm the sky;
protect me from the sky!
What moist wombs are yours, O Agni, what nesting-places, what drops, O Agni, what navels are yours,
in agreement with both of these, knowing, sit down here with your body!
Sprouting from every node, from every joint,
even so, O dūrvā-grass, stretch out for us with a thousand and with a hundred!
You who stretch out with a hundred, who sprout with a thousand,
to you, O divine brick, may we pay homage with the oblation!

Yoke, O Agni, those horses of yours that are good, god,
that, swift, bear sufficiently!
Yoke, O Agni, the horse that summons gods best, like a charioteer;
sit down as the ancient Hotṛ!
Flowing together like streams, the words of praise, purified in heart and mind;
I gaze upon the streams of ghee, the golden reed in the middle of Agni.
For the verse, thee!
For brilliance, thee!
For light, thee!
For brightness, thee!
This has become the steed of the whole world and of Agni Vaiśvānara.
Agni is full of fiery energy through fiery energy;
the gold plate is full of luster through luster.
A giver of thousands art thou;
for a thousand, thee!
Anoint the Āditya, the embryo, with milk, the all-formed counterpart of a thousand;
bypass [him] with heat, do not burn [him]; being piled, make him live a hundred years!
The rush of the wind, the navel of Varuṇa, the horse born in the middle of the flood,
the child of the rivers, the tawny one rooted in the rock—O Agni, do not harm him in the highest firmament!
The unceasing, ruddy, active drop, Agni, do I praise with homages at the early thought;
he, fitting together limb by limb according to the seasons—do not harm the cow, Aditi, the wide ruler!
The Varutrī of Tvaṣṭṛ, the navel of Varuṇa, the sheep born beyond the space,
the great, thousandfold wonder-working power of the Asura—O Agni, do not harm her in the highest firmament!
That fire which was born from Agni's heat, from his burning, over the earth or from heaven,
which Viśvakarman begot as these creatures—O Agni, let your wrath bypass him!
Do not harm this two-footed beast, thou thousand-eyed one, being piled for the offering!
The wild ape I assign unto thee;
with him piling [the altar], sit down in thy body!
Let thy burning pain reach the ape;
let thy burning pain reach him whom we hate!
Do not harm this single-hoofed beast, neighing, the courser among coursers!
The wild gaur I assign unto thee;
with him piling [the altar], sit down in thy body!
Let thy burning pain reach the gaur;
let thy burning pain reach him whom we hate!
This thousandfold, hundred-streamed fountain, opening out in the middle of the flood,
Aditi yielding ghee for the folk—O Agni, do not harm her in the highest firmament!
The wild gavaya I assign unto thee;
with him piling [the altar], sit down in thy body!
Let thy burning pain reach the gavaya;
let thy burning pain reach him whom we hate!
This woolly one, the navel of Varuṇa, the skin of beasts, two-footed and four-footed,
the first birthplace of Tvaṣṭṛ, of the gods—O Agni, do not harm him in the highest firmament!
The wild ram I assign unto thee;
with him piling [the altar], sit down in thy body!
Let thy burning pain reach the ram;
let thy burning pain reach him whom we hate!
For the he-goat was born from Agni's heat; he saw the begetter in the beginning;
by him the gods went to divinity, to the highest; by him, fit for sacrifice, they went to the heights.
The wild śarabha I assign unto thee;
with him piling [the altar], sit down in thy body!
Let thy burning pain reach the śarabha;
let thy burning pain reach him whom we hate!

This one in front is the earth;
his breath is derived from the earth;
spring is derived from breath;
the Gāyatrī is connected with spring;
from the Gāyatrī, the Gāyatra [sāman];
from the Gāyatra, the Upāṃśu [cup];
from the Upāṃśu, the Trivṛt [stoma];
from the Trivṛt, the Rathantara [sāman];
Vasiṣṭha is the ṛṣi.
With thee, taken by Prajāpati, I take breath for the creatures.
This one on the south is Viśvakarman;
his mind is derived from Viśvakarman;
summer is derived from mind;
the Triṣṭubh is connected with summer;
from the Triṣṭubh, the Svāra [sāman];
from the Svāra, the Antaryāma [cup];
from the Antaryāma, the Pañcadaśa [stoma];
from the Pañcadaśa, the Bṛhat [sāman];
Bharadvāja is the ṛṣi.
With thee, taken by Prajāpati, I take the mind for the creatures.
This one behind is of all-encompassing range;
his eye is derived from the all-encompassing range;
the rainy season is derived from the eye;
the Jagatī is connected with the rainy season;
from the Jagatī, the Ṛk-sama [sāman];
from the Ṛk-sama, the Śukra [cup];
from the Śukra, the Saptadaśa [stoma];
from the Saptadaśa, the Vairūpa [sāman];
Jamadagni is the ṛṣi.
With thee, taken by Prajāpati, I take the eye for the creatures.
This one on the north is heaven;
his hearing is derived from heaven;
autumn is derived from hearing;
the Anuṣṭubh is connected with autumn;
from the Anuṣṭubh, the Aiḍa [sāman];
from the Aiḍa, the Manthin [cup];
from the Manthin, the Ekaviṃśa [stoma];
from the Ekaviṃśa, the Vairāja [sāman];
Viśvāmitra is the ṛṣi.
With thee, taken by Prajāpati, I take hearing for the creatures.
This one above is thought;
her speech is derived from thought;
winter is derived from speech;
the Paṅkti is connected with winter;
from the Paṅkti, the Nidhanavat [sāman];
from the Nidhanavat, the Āgrayaṇa [cup];
from the Āgrayaṇa, the Triṇava and Trayastriṃśa [stomas];
from the Triṇava and Trayastriṃśa, the Śākvara and Raivata [sāmans];
Viśvakarman is the ṛṣi.
With thee, taken by Prajāpati, I take speech for the creatures.

The eastern direction;
the spring season;
Agni the deity;
the priesthood the wealth;
the Gāyatrī the meter;
the Rathantara the sāman;
the fifteenfold [stoma] the course;
Sānaga the ṛṣi;
the eighteen-month-old [calf] the age;
the Kṛta among throws of dice;
the east wind the wind.
The fathers, grandfathers, earlier [and] later,
may they aid us,
may they protect us in this priesthood, in this office of purohita, in this rite, in this wish, in this invocation of the gods!
The southern direction;
the summer season;
Indra the deity;
the princely power the wealth;
the Triṣṭubh the meter;
the Bṛhat the sāman;
the fifteenfold the stoma;
and it has the seventeenfold course;
Sanātana the ṛṣi;
the two-year-old [bull] the age;
the Tretā among throws of dice;
the south wind the wind.
The fathers, grandfathers, earlier [and] later,
may they aid us,
may they protect us in this priesthood, in this office of purohita, in this rite, in this wish, in this invocation of the gods!
The western direction;
the rainy season the season;
the All-Gods the deity;
the commonalty the wealth;
the Jagatī the meter;
the Vairūpa the sāman;
the seventeenfold the stoma;
and it has the twenty-onefold course;
Ahabhūna the ṛṣi;
the three-year-old [bull] the age;
the Dvāpara among throws of dice;
the west wind the wind.
The fathers, grandfathers, earlier [and] later,
may they aid us,
may they protect us in this priesthood, in this office of purohita, in this rite, in this wish, in this invocation of the gods!
The northern direction;
the autumn season;
Mitra and Varuṇa the deities;
prosperity the wealth;
the Anuṣṭubh the meter;
the Vairāja the sāman;
the twenty-onefold the stoma;
and it has the twenty-sevenfold course;
Purāṇa the ṛṣi;
the four-year-old [bull] the age;
the Abhibhava among throws of dice;
the north wind the wind.
The fathers, grandfathers, earlier [and] later,
may they aid us,
may they protect us in this priesthood, in this office of purohita, in this rite, in this wish, in this invocation of the gods!
The upward direction;
the winter and dewy seasons the two seasons;
Bṛhaspati the deity;
fruit the wealth;
the Paṅkti the meter;
the Śākvara and Raivata the two sāmans;
the twenty-sevenfold the stoma;
and it has the thirty-threefold course;
Suparṇa the ṛṣi;
the five-year-old [bull] the age;
the Āskanda among throws of dice;
the wind from above the wind.
The fathers, grandfathers, earlier [and] later,
may they aid us,
may they protect us in this priesthood, in this office of purohita, in this rite, in this wish, in this invocation of the gods!

Protect my exhalation!
Protect my inhalation!
Protect my circulating breath!
Illuminate widely my sight!
Cause my hearing to be praised!
Swell the waters!
Quicken the plants!
Protect the two-footed!
Guard the four-footed!
Send down rain from the sky!
Ruling power is the age;
Mayanta is the meter.
Support is the age;
Adhipati is the meter.
The head is the age;
Prajāpati is the meter.
Viśvakarman is the age;
Parameṣṭhin is the meter.
The eighteen-month-old [calf] is the age;
the Triṣṭubh is the meter.
The two-year-old [bull] is the age;
the Virāj is the meter.
The thirty-month-old [calf] is the age;
the Gāyatrī is the meter.
The three-year-old [bull] is the age;
the Uṣṇih is the meter.
The four-year-old [bull] is the age;
the Anuṣṭubh is the meter.
The five-year-old [bull] is the age;
the Bṛhatī is the meter.
The breeding bull is the age;
the Kakubh is the meter.
The bull is the age;
the Satobṛhatī is the meter.
The draft-ox is the age;
the Paṅkti is the meter.
The milch-cow is the age;
the Jagatī is the meter.
The he-goat is the age;
Yuvala is the meter.
The ram is the age;
Viśāla is the meter.
Man is the age;
Tandra is the meter.
The lion is the age;
Chadis is the meter.
The tiger is the age;
Anādhṛṣya is the meter.

This one in the east is Harikeśa, a ray of the sun;
his general and village-headman are Rathakṛtsna and Rathaujas;
Puñjikasthalā and Kṛtasthalā are his two apsarases;
sorcerers are his missile,
rākṣasas his counter-missile.
Homage be to them!
May they be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place into their jaws.
This one in the south is Viśvakarman;
his general and village-headman are Rathasvana and Rathecitra;
Menakā and Sahajanyā are his two apsarases;
biting beasts are his missile,
homicide is his counter-missile.
Homage be to them!
May they be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place into their jaws.
This one in the west is Vidadvasu;
his general and village-headman are Rathaprota and Asamaratha;
Āmlocantī and Pramlocantī are his two apsarases;
tigers are his missile,
serpents his counter-missile.
Homage be to them!
May they be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place into their jaws.
This one in the north is Saṃyadvasu;
his general and village-headman are Senajit and Suṣeṇa;
Viśvācī and Ghṛtācī are his two apsarases;
waters are his missile,
wind his counter-missile.
Homage be to them!
May they be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place into their jaws.
This one above is Arvāgvasu;
his general and village-headman are Tārkṣya and Ariṣṭanemi;
Urvaśī and Pūrvacitti are his two apsarases;
thundering from above is his missile,
lightning his counter-missile.
Homage be to them!
May they be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place into their jaws.

With the eastern direction I set thee down.
With Agni the god as deity, with the gāyatrī meter, I lay down the head of Agni;
with the head of Agni of the gāyatrī meter I lay down the head of Agni.
With the southern direction I set thee down.
With Indra the god as deity, with the triṣṭubh meter, I lay down the wing of Agni;
with the wing of Agni of the triṣṭubh meter I lay down the wing of Agni.
With the western direction I set thee down.
With the All-Gods as deity, with the jagatī meter, I lay down the tail of Agni;
with the tail of Agni of the jagatī meter I lay down the tail of Agni.
With the northern direction I set thee down.
With Mitra and Varuṇa, the two gods, as deity, with the anuṣṭubh meter, I lay down the wing of Agni;
with the wing of Agni of the anuṣṭubh meter I lay down the wing of Agni.
With the upward direction I set thee down.
With Bṛhaspati the god as deity, with the paṅkti meter, I lay down the back of Agni;
with the back of Agni of the paṅkti meter I lay down the back of Agni.

Madhu and Mādhava, the two spring seasons: / thou art the inner clamp of Agni.
May heaven and earth be shaped [for me]; may the waters and plants be shaped [for me]; / may the fires separately, sharing my vow, be shaped for my preeminence.
The fires that are like-minded between heaven and earth, / adapting themselves to the two spring seasons, may they assemble together around [me] as the gods around Indra.
Śukra and Śuci, the two summer seasons;
Nabhas and Nabhasya, the two rainy seasons;
Iṣ and Ūrj, the two autumn seasons;
Sahas and Sahasya, the two winter seasons;
Tapas and Tapasya, the two cool seasons: / thou art the inner clamp of Agni.
May heaven and earth be shaped [for me]; may the waters and plants be shaped [for me]; / may the fires separately, sharing my vow, be shaped for my preeminence.
The fires that are like-minded between heaven and earth, / adapting themselves to the two cool seasons, may they assemble together around [me] as the gods around Indra.

Thou art the winner of the east wind.
Thou art the winner of cloud.
Thou art the winner of lightning.
Thou art the winner of thunder.
Thou art the winner of rain.
Thou art the vehicle of Agni.
Thou art the vehicle to Agni of Agni.
Thou art the vehicle of Vāyu.
Thou art the vehicle to Vāyu of Vāyu.
Thou art the vehicle of the gods.
Thou art the vehicle to the gods of the gods.
Thou art the vehicle of the All-Gods.
Thou art the vehicle to the gods of the All-Gods.
Thou art the converging vehicle.
Thou art sitting in the atmosphere.
Sit in the atmosphere.
Ambā and Bulā and Nitatnī and Stanayantī and Abhrayantī and Meghayantī and Cupuṇīkā:
for the flood [I set] thee,
for grace thee,
for truth thee,
for intent thee,
for good intent thee,
for like intent thee,
for Vivasvat thee,
for the sky, for light thee,
for the Ādityas thee.

Water-fortress art thou by name, pervaded with food.
Unto thee I approach with myself, with men, with cows, with horses, with life, with splendor, with offspring, with wealth, with gain, with wisdom, with riches, with thriving.
Men are thy guardians;
Agni is the sovereign lord.
With that deity, like the Aṅgirases, sit firm.
Unconquered art thou by name, pervaded with the Brahman.
Unto thee I approach with myself, with men, with cows, with horses, with life, with splendor, with offspring, with wealth, with gain, with wisdom, with riches, with thriving.
The Maruts are thy guardians;
Vāyu is the sovereign lord.
With that deity, like the Aṅgirases, sit firm.
Over-heaven art thou by name, pervaded with immortality.
Unto thee I approach with myself, with men, with cows, with horses, with life, with splendor, with offspring, with wealth, with gain, with wisdom, with riches, with thriving.
The All-Gods are thy guardians;
Sūrya is the sovereign lord.
With that deity, like the Aṅgirases, sit firm.
May Prajāpati seat thee on the back of the earth.
Earth art thou;
ground art thou;
breadth art thou;
wide earth art thou.
Aditi art thou, all-sustaining, supporter of the whole world.
Support the earth;
make the earth firm;
injure not the earth.
Protect me from the earth for all out-breathing, in-breathing, inter-breathing, up-breathing, for standing firm, for moving about.
May Agni protect thee all around with great well-being, with most auspicious protection.
May Viśvakarman seat thee on the back of the atmosphere.
Support the atmosphere;
make the atmosphere firm;
injure not the atmosphere.
Protect me from the atmosphere for all out-breathing, in-breathing, inter-breathing, up-breathing, for standing firm, for moving about.
May Vāyu protect thee all around with great well-being, with most auspicious protection.
May Parameṣṭhin seat thee on the back of heaven, spacious, broad, shining, radiant,
who shinest unto heaven, unto earth, unto the wide atmosphere.
Support heaven;
make heaven firm;
injure not heaven.
Protect me from heaven for all out-breathing, in-breathing, inter-breathing, up-breathing, for standing firm, for moving about.
May Sūrya protect thee all around with great well-being, with most auspicious protection.
Snorting like a horse desiring grass, when he stood out from the great enclosure, / then the wind blows after his flame; then may thy path be black.
In the seat of Āyu I set thee down, in the surge of the ocean, in the shade of the well.
Homage to the ocean;
homage to the eye of the ocean.
The measure of a thousand art thou;
the fore-measure of a thousand art thou;
the counterpart of a thousand art thou;
the commensurate measure of a thousand art thou;
the up-measure of a thousand art thou;
belonging to a thousand art thou;
for a thousand thee.
May these bricks be milch-cows for me, O Agni:
one and a hundred,
a hundred and a thousand,
a thousand and ten thousand,
ten thousand and a hundred thousand,
a hundred thousand and ten thousand […],
ten million and a hundred million,
a billion and a ten-billion,
a hundred-billion and a trillion.
May these bricks be milch-cows for me, O Agni, sixty, a thousand, ten thousand, unexhausted,
standing in the seasons, thriving in ṛta, dropping ghee, dropping honey, rich in sap, rich in milk, self-nourishing, nesting.
May those bricks be milch-cows for me, O Agni, Virājs by name, yielding desires yonder in that world.

Homage to the mountain-wanderers, the hearts of the gods!
Homage to the discriminators!
Homage to the destroyers!
Homage to the crushers!
O prowler, lord of nourishment, impoverished one, blue-and-red one,
do not terrify these cattle, these offspring, do not injure them, and let none of ours be sick!
These thoughts we bring forward to the strong Rudra, the braided-haired one, who rules over heroes,
so that there may be welfare for our two-footed and four-footed, and that everything in this village may be thriving and free from illness.
That body of yours, O Rudra, which is auspicious, auspicious, having all-healing medicines,
auspicious, having medicines for affliction, with that make us live happily!
May the missile of Rudra bypass us, may the disfavor of the furious, malevolent one bypass [us];
loosen [your bowstrings] firm against our patrons; O bounteous one, have mercy on our children and offspring!
O most bounteous, most auspicious one, be auspicious to us, be well-disposed;
unstringing your bow, without anger, be well-disposed;
bearing the pināka, come hither; clothed in a hide, approach!
O diverter, bright-red one, homage be to you, O lord;
those missiles of yours that are a thousand, let them strike down others than us!
Thousands by thousands are the missiles in your arms;
as ruler of them, O generous one, turn their points away!
The innumerable thousands of Rudras who are upon the earth,
their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.
The Bhavas who are over this great flood, the atmosphere,
their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.
The Rudras, blue-necked, white-throated, who have resorted to heaven,
their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.
The Śarvas, blue-necked, white-throated, who move upon the earth below,
their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.
Those who in the trees are meadow-yellow, blue-necked, bright-red,
their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.
Those who are overlords of beings, crestless and braided-haired,
their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.
Those who are path-protectors of paths, bringing delight in food, your warriors,
their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.
Those who frequent the fords, bearing darts and quivers,
their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.
Those who pierce among the foods, [among] people drinking from vessels,
their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.
The Rudras who, so many or more, have settled in the quarters,
their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.
Homage be to the Rudras who are in heaven, whose arrows are rain;
to them ten [fingers] eastward, ten southward, ten westward, ten northward, ten upward;
to them be homage;
may they have mercy on us;
whomever we hate and whoever hates us, him we put into their jaws!
Homage be to the Rudras who are in the atmosphere, whose arrows are wind;
to them ten [fingers] eastward, ten southward, ten westward, ten northward, ten upward;
to them be homage;
may they have mercy on us;
whomever we hate and whoever hates us, him we put into their jaws!
Homage be to the Rudras who are on earth, whose arrows are food;
to them ten [fingers] eastward, ten southward, ten westward, ten northward, ten upward;
to them be homage;
may they have mercy on us;
whomever we hate and whoever hates us, him we put into their jaws!

Father of the eye, for he is wise in mind, generated these two bowing ones as ghee;
when their ancient ends were fixed firm, only then did heaven and earth spread out.
Viśvakarman measured out, he who is vast, the supporter, the distributor, and the highest vision;
their offerings rejoice together with food, where they call the one beyond the seven ṛṣis.
They sacrificed wealth together into this, the ancient ṛṣis, like singers in abundance,
seated in the unseen and seen realm, who formed these created beings.
Who is our father, the begetter, who the distributor, who produced existence beyond existence for us,
who alone is the name-giver of the gods, to him other worlds go asking questions.
Beyond heaven, beyond this earth, beyond the gods, what asura-like [entity] is there?
Which first embryo did the waters hold, wherein all the gods came together?
The waters held that very first embryo, wherein all the gods saw one another;
placed on the navel of the Unborn is the One, wherein all beings rested.
If Viśvakarman, the god, was born, then the Gandharva became the second;
the third, the father, the begetter of plants; they distributed the embryo of the waters in many places.
You do not know him who generated these; something else has become intermediate between you;
shrouded in mist and with chatter, breathing-to-satisfy, reciters of ukthas, they wander about.

Stride up to the firmament with the fire, bearing the cauldron in your hands;
having gone to the back of heaven, to the sun, sit mingled with the gods!
Along the eastern [direction] […].
Go forth, O Agni, foremost of those who seek the gods, the eye of gods and of mortals;
eager to sacrifice, along with the Bhṛgus, let the sacrificers go safely to the sun!
From the earth I have mounted up to the atmosphere; from the atmosphere I have mounted up to heaven;
from the back of the firmament of heaven I have gone to the light, to the sun.
Going to the sun, they do not look back; they mount up to heaven, to both worlds,
they who, well-knowing, have extended the sacrifice flowing in every direction.
Night and dawn […].
O Agni of a thousand eyes […].
You are the eagle, winged;
sit upon the back of the earth!
Fill the atmosphere with brightness;
prop up heaven with light;
firm the directions with splendor!
Invoked, fair-faced, in front, O Agni, sit upon your own womb, the Sādhya;
in this upper place of meeting, sit, all gods and the sacrificer!
That wondrous, all-peopled good favor of Savitṛ the desirable I choose,
which Kaṇva milked for him, the swelling, thousand-streaming, great cow with milk.
May we worship you at your highest birth, O Agni; may we worship with praises in the lower place of meeting;
from which womb you have arisen, sacrifice to that; forth into you, when kindled, have they offered the oblations.
Kindled forth, O Agni, shine before us with unceasing flame, O youngest one;
to him ever come the spoils.
"O Agni, him today" […].
"Seven are your [kindling-sticks], O Agni" […].
I offer the layer with the mind, so that the gods may come here,
the invited sacrificers, increasers of truth.
In the flight of your path, the ocean, I offer to Viśvakarman
an unassailable oblation forever.

Bright-light, and Clear-light,
and True-light, and Luminous,
and True, and Protector of truth, and Transcending distress;
and Winner of truth, and Winner of reality,
and Winner of armies, and Having good armies;
and Having friends near, and Having foes afar, a troop;
and Cosmic Order, and Truth,
and Firm, and Supporter,
and Sustainer, and Disposer, and Maintainer;
and Such, and Such-like,
and Equal, and Counter-equal,
and Measured, and Commensurate, all-bringing ones;
such ones, such-like ones, come well to us,
equal ones, counter-equal ones, come,
measured and commensurate, to our aid, all-bringing Maruts, to this sacrifice!
The Maruts, the divine clans, follow Indra;
just as the Maruts, the divine clans, followed Indra, so may both the divine and human clans follow this sacrificer!

And fire and the heat-cauldron;
and the sun-hymn and the sun;
and breath and the horse sacrifice;
and Aditi and the earth;
and Diti and the sky;
may the Śakvarī meters, the fingers, and the quarters be prepared for me through sacrifice!
And the season and the vow;
and the year and heat;
may day and night, the two thigh-bones, and the Bṛhat and Rathantara [chants] be prepared for me through sacrifice!
And one, and three, up to thirty-three;
and four, and eight, up to forty-eight;
and the eighteen-month-old calf, and the eighteen-month-old heifer;
and the two-year-old bull, and the two-year-old cow;
and the thirty-month-old bull, and the thirty-month-old cow;
and the three-year-old bull, and the three-year-old cow;
and the four-year-old bull, and the four-year-old cow;
and the five-year-old bull, and the five-year-old cow;
and the breeding bull, and the barren cow;
and the bull, and the miscarrying cow;
and the milch cow, and the draft ox;
and the stoma, and the yajus formula;
and the ṛk, and the sāman;
and the consecration, and heat;
and the Bṛhat, and the Rathantara;
may the herbs, the lords of the forest, and the quarters be prepared for me through sacrifice!
For food, you!
For prize-winning, you!
For winning the prize, you!
For refreshment, you!
For strength, you!
For wealth, you!
For nourishment, you!

May all the Maruts today with all their help, may all the fires be kindled for us;
may all the gods come hither with aid, may all wealth, [all] prize be with us.
Prize is my seven directions, or the four distant realms;
may prize with all the gods aid me here in the winning of wealth.
Prize in front and from the middle for us; prize shall adapt the gods through the seasons.
For in the impulse of the prize I bend down; may I become master of the prize in every quarter.
May prize impel a gift for me today; may prize increase the gods with oblation.
For prize has made me rich in all heroes; may I, as master of the prize, win all quarters.
I unite myself with the milk of the earth, I unite myself with waters, with herbs;
so may I win the prize, O Agni.
Put milk into the earth, milk into the herbs, milk into the sky, milk into the atmosphere;
may the directions be full of milk for me.

Conquering by truth, truth-aboded Agni is the Gandharva;
his Apsarases are the herbs, named Joys;
may he protect this our priesthood and nobility;
may they protect this our priesthood and nobility;
to him svāhā, vaṭ!
To them svāhā, vaṭ!
The gathered, all-harmonious Sun is the Gandharva;
his Apsarases are the rays, named Active;
Suṣumṇa, sun-beamed Soma the moon is the Gandharva;
his Apsarases are the lunar mansions, named Bekuris;
the vigorous, all-encompassing Wind is the Gandharva;
his Apsarases are the waters, named Strengths;
the nourishing eagle, the Sacrifice is the Gandharva;
his Apsarases are the sacrificial gifts, named Desires;
Bṛhaspati, all-working Indra is the Gandharva;
his Apsarases are the Maruts, named Might;
Prajāpati, the highest, the Mind is the Gandharva;
his Apsarases are the ṛks and sāmans, named Praises;
unpitying, distant-missiled Death is the Gandharva;
his Apsarases are the creatures, named Timid;
O Lord of the world, whose houses are above, O Lord of Virāj, grant great protection to this priesthood, to this nobility;
whose Apsarases are all the quarters, named Pleasing—may he protect this our priesthood and nobility;
may they protect this our priesthood and nobility;
to him svāhā, vaṭ!
To them svāhā, vaṭ!

You are the ocean, vaporous, wet with drops;
bringing peace, bringing pleasure, blow unto me, svāhā!
You are aid-seeking, reverent;
bringing peace, bringing pleasure, blow unto me, svāhā!
You are of the Maruts, the host of the Maruts;
bringing peace, bringing pleasure, blow unto me, svāhā!
In union with the year through the second halves of the months,
in union with the Dawn through her ruddy ones,
in full union with the Aśvins through wondrous deeds,
in union with the Sun through Etaśa,
in union with Vaiśvānara through the offering-food, through ghee, svāhā!
I yoke Agni with might, with ghee, the heavenly eagle, large in wingspan;
with him may we fly to the summit of the ruddy one, ascending to heaven in the highest firmament.
These two wings of yours are unaging, winged, with which you strike away the demons, O Agni;
with them may we fly to the world of the righteous, where went the first, the ancient seers.
The drop, the skilled one, the falcon keeping the order, golden-winged, the bustling bird,
great in his seat, firmly seated—
homage be to you,
do not harm me!
You are the head of the sky, the navel of the earth, the strength of the waters, of the herbs;
all-living, far-reaching protection; homage to the path; you dwell established on the head of the universe.
In the ocean is your heart, life within; give the waters, cleave the water-reservoir;
from the sky, from Parjanya, from the atmosphere, from the earth—from there aid us with rain!
I unfasten your halter, I unfasten your reins, I unfasten the harness straps that go around;
grant us here auspicious wealth, declare me as bestowing a portion among the deities.
Sacrificed is the sacrifice by the Bhṛgus as giver of wealth, by the Yatis as giver of blessings, by the Vasus;
may Agni, sacrificed and invoked, fulfill our offered oblation;
blessed be this homage to the gods!

With which austerity the ṛṣis sat at the sacrificial session, kindling Agni, bringing heaven;
which [Agni] men call the one with strewn sacred grass—on that firmament I deposit Agni.
May we follow him with wives, O gods, with sons, with brothers, or with gold,
seizing the firmament in the world of the well-done, on the third back, upon the radiance of heaven.
The bustling, perceptive Agni, the lord of the good, has mounted into the middle of speech;
laid down on the back of the earth, shining brightly, let him tread underfoot those who are hostile.
May this Agni, most heroic, bestower of vigor, belonging to the thousand, shine unheeding;
shining forth from the midst of the water, go forth unto the divine abodes!
Agni, move, go forth along together; make visible the paths that lead to the gods;
take hold of each well-done deed, where the ṛṣis have gone, who were the first, the ancient ones.
Move forth together, go forth together; make visible the paths that lead to the gods;
by these well-done deeds may we follow, O gods, where our ancient fathers have passed away.
Awake, O Agni, be watchful toward him; may you and he unite with what is sacrificed and bestowed;
making them young again, the fathers have woven along this thread of yours.
With that wherewith you bear a thousand, with that, O Agni, wherewith all property,
with that bear this our sacrifice to heaven, to go among the gods.
This is your—

May the seasons, the years increase you, O Agni, the ṛṣis, whatever things are true;
shine forth with divine radiance; illuminate all the four quarters.
Be kindled together, O Agni, and awake him; stand upright for great good fortune;
and may not your attendant come to harm, O Agni; may your priests be glorious, not others.
You, O Agni, these Brahmins choose; be gracious to us, O Agni, in the enclosure;
be a slayer of rivals, O Agni, conqueror of enemies; watch without slumbering in your own home.
Here indeed, O Agni, uphold wealth; let not the subjugators subjugate you at the first thought;
let the nobility be easily guided for you, O Agni; may your attendant increase unvanquished.
With your own nobility, O Agni, seize [power]; with Mitra, O Agni, strive in friendship;
for standing in the midst of fellow-born, of kings, O Agni, shine here worthy of invocation.
Beyond the scoffers, beyond the slights, beyond ignorance, beyond destruction today,
for do you cross over all perils, O Agni; then grant to us wealth with heroes.
Inviolable Jātavedas, unvanquished, ruling widely, O Agni, upholding nobility, shine here;
releasing the ailments of mortal men, guard us today with gracious [aids] for our increase.
O Bṛhaspati, O Savitṛ, awaken him; sharpen him yet further, though already sharpened;
increase him for great good fortune, and let all the gods rejoice with him.
From the afterlife, from the curse of Yama, which you released [him from], O Bṛhaspati,
may the two Aśvins, the physicians of the gods, avert death from him by their powers, O Agni.
Beholding the higher light beyond the darkness,
we have gone to the god, the sun among the gods, the highest light.

Sought in heaven, sought on earth, sought has Agni entered all plants;
Agni Vaiśvānara, sought by force, let him protect us by day, let him protect us from harm by night.
O youngest, protect the men of the giver; hear our songs;
guard the offspring, and [us] in person.
O Agni Jātavedas, god, self-willed one, your forms, the name of the immortal,
and the many wondrous powers of the craft-masters which they placed in you, O all-penetrating one, of tied back!

The measure is the meter;
that is the earth;
Agni is the deity;
with that meter, with that sacred word, with that deity, sit firm in the manner of Aṅgiras!
The fore-measure is the meter;
that is the atmosphere;
Vāyu is the deity;
the counterpart is the meter;
that is the sky;
Sūrya is the deity;
the Asrīvī is the meter;
that is the quarters;
Soma is the deity;
the Gāyatrī is the meter;
that is the she-goat;
Bṛhaspati is the deity;
the Triṣṭubh is the meter;
that is the gold;
Indra is the deity;
the Jagatī is the meter;
that is the cow;
Prajāpati is the deity;
the Anuṣṭubh is the meter;
that is life-span;
Mitra is the deity;
the Uṣṇih is the meter;
that is the eye;
Pūṣan is the deity;
the Virāj is the meter;
that is the horse;
Varuṇa is the deity;
the Bṛhatī is the meter;
that is agriculture;
Parjanya is the deity;
the Paṅkti is the meter;
that is man;
Parameṣṭhin is the deity;
with that meter, with that sacred word, with that deity, sit firm in the manner of Aṅgiras!

You are the earth, a barren cow by birth;
you held Agni as embryo;
come into being with me!
You are the atmosphere, a barren cow by birth;
you held Vāyu as embryo;
come into being with me!
You are the sky, a barren cow by birth;
you held the Āditya as embryo;
come into being with me!
You are the lunar mansions, a barren cow by birth;
you held the moon as embryo;
come into being with me!
You are the Ṛg, a barren cow by birth;
you held the Sāman as embryo;
come into being with me!
You are the commonalty, a barren cow by birth;
you held the king as embryo;
come into being with me!
You are speech, a barren cow by birth;
you held breath as embryo;
come into being with me!
You are the waters, a barren cow by birth;
you held sacrifice as embryo;
come into being with me!

You are the creator of abundance;
you are the creator of wide room;
you are facing east;
you are facing upward;
you are the atmosphere.
I seat you, the giver of strength, in strength.
I seat you, the giver of milk, in milk.
I seat you, the giver of fiery brilliance, in fiery brilliance.
I seat you, the giver of fame, in fame.
I seat you, the giver of luster, in luster.
I seat you in the wealth of the earth.
I seat you in the wealth of the atmosphere.
I seat you in the wealth of the sky.
I seat you in the wealth of the quarters.
I seat you, the giver of wealth, in wealth.
You are seated in the waters;
you are seated in the vulture;
you are seated in the falcon;
you are seated in the eagle;
you are seated in the vault of heaven.

The Kṛttikās are the lunar mansion;
Agni is the deity.
You are the splendors of Agni,
of Prajāpati, of Soma, of Dhātṛ.
For verse, you!
For splendor, you!
For radiance, you!
For light, you!
With that meter, with that sacred word, with that deity, sit firm in the manner of Aṅgiras!
Rohiṇī is the lunar mansion;
Prajāpati is the deity.
Invagā is the lunar mansion;
the Maruts are the deity.
Bāhu is the lunar mansion;
Rudra is the deity.
Punarvasu is the lunar mansion;
Aditi is the deity.
Tiṣya is the lunar mansion;
Bṛhaspati is the deity.
Āśleṣā is the lunar mansion;
the serpents are the deity.
Maghā is the lunar mansion;
the Fathers are the deity.
The Phalgunīs are the lunar mansion;
Bhaga is the deity.
The Phalgunīs are the lunar mansion;
Aryaman is the deity.
Hasta is the lunar mansion;
Savitṛ is the deity.
Citrā is the lunar mansion;
Tvaṣṭṛ is the deity.
Niṣṭya is the lunar mansion;
Vāyu is the deity.
Viśākha is the lunar mansion;
Indra and Agni are the deity.
Anūrādhā is the lunar mansion;
Mitra is the deity.
Jyeṣṭhā is the lunar mansion;
Varuṇa is the deity.
Mūla is the lunar mansion;
Nirṛti is the deity.
Aṣāḍhā is the lunar mansion;
the Waters are the deity.
Aṣāḍhā is the lunar mansion;
the All-Gods are the deity.
Abhijit is the lunar mansion;
Brahmā is the deity.
Śroṇā is the lunar mansion;
Viṣṇu is the deity.
Śraviṣṭhā is the lunar mansion;
the Vasus are the deity.
Śatabhiṣaj is the lunar mansion;
Indra is the deity.
Proṣṭhapadā is the lunar mansion;
Ahi Budhnya is the deity.
Proṣṭhapadā is the lunar mansion;
Aja Ekapād is the deity.
Revatī is the lunar mansion;
Pūṣan is the deity.
The Aśvayujau are the lunar mansion;
the Aśvins are the deity.
The Bharaṇīs are the lunar mansion;
Yama is the deity.
Brāhmaṇa is the lunar mansion;
Soma is the deity.
You are the splendors of Agni,
of Prajāpati, of Soma, of Dhātṛ.
For verse, you!
For splendor, you!
For radiance, you!
For light, you!
With that meter, with that sacred word, with that deity, sit firm in the manner of Aṅgiras!

Samīcī by name are you,
the eastern quarter;
of you, Agni is the overlord;
Asita is the protector;
he who is your overlord and he who is the protector—to both of them let homage be!
May those two be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place in their jaws!
Ojasyā by name are you,
the southern quarter;
of you, Indra is the overlord;
Tiraścīnarāji is the protector;
he who is your overlord and he who is the protector—to both of them let homage be!
May those two be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place in their jaws!
Prācī by name are you,
the western quarter;
of you, Soma is the overlord;
Svaja is the protector;
he who is your overlord and he who is the protector—to both of them let homage be!
May those two be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place in their jaws!
Suṣadā by name are you,
the northern quarter;
of you, Varuṇa is the overlord;
Sṛdāgu is the protector;
he who is your overlord and he who is the protector—to both of them let homage be!
May those two be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place in their jaws!
Avasthā by name are you,
the downward quarter;
of you, Viṣṇu is the overlord;
Kalmāṣagrīva is the protector;
he who is your overlord and he who is the protector—to both of them let homage be!
May those two be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place in their jaws!
Adhipatnī by name are you,
the upward quarter;
of you, Bṛhaspati is the overlord;
Citra is the protector;
he who is your overlord and he who is the protector—to both of them let homage be!
May those two be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place in their jaws!

He offers these six ādhīta-yajus formulas.
Six indeed are the seasons;
by the seasons he thus raises the earth's vigor.
He offers [them] separately,
for these vital breaths are of varied vigor,
for the maintaining of the vital breaths.
If he desires of one, 'May he be deaf,' he should offer all of them for him running [them] together at once.
He splits his vital breaths;
he becomes deaf.
Therefore a deaf person speaks with speech, but does not hear;
for his faculty goes along into speech.
The vital breaths indeed are these other meters;
speech is the anuṣṭubh.
In that he offers the seventh with an anuṣṭubh,
he thereby connects speech with the vital breaths;
therefore this speech is the seventh of the vital breaths.
'Do not break easily, do not be easily injured,' with this he sets [it] down.
This was an Asura's magic;
the gods took it away with this yajus formula: 'An Asuric magic made by svadhā were you.'
Thereby the sacrificer takes away the magic of his rival.
With two [formulas] he sets [it] down;
two-footed is the sacrificer,
for firm standing.
He should set [it] down for one desiring prosperity;
we live upon that which is to come;
he indeed approaches that which is to come;
day after day he becomes better.
One whose fortune has arrived should put in an existing [fire];
he whose fortune has arrived is indeed an existing one;
by means of the existing he generates him.
He should bring [it] from a baker's oven for one whom he desires, 'May he be an eater of food.'
This indeed among fires is the eater of food;
the baker's oven is an instrument of food;
he secures proper food for him.
He should bring [it] from a forest fire for one whom he desires, 'May his realm be victorious with an army of conquest.'
His realm becomes victorious with an army of conquest.
Having fetched [it] from anywhere, he should put [it] in for one whom he desires, 'May an evil rival be born as his second.'
This indeed is the sacrificer's abode;
in his own abode he thus generates an evil rival as his second.
'Having wood as food, having ghee as oblation,' with this he puts on a krumuka-stick anointed with ghee.
This indeed is Agni's dear body, namely the krumuka;
ghee is fiery brilliance.
He provides him fully with his dear body and with fiery brilliance.
'From the farther region,' [with this] an udumbara [stick].
Where the gods divided nourishment, from there the udumbara arose.
In that it is of udumbara,
he thus secures nourishment.
'From the highest distance,' [with this] a vaikaṅkata [stick].
When Agni was created, his brightness departed;
it entered the vikaṅkata.
In that it is of vaikaṅkata,
he thus secures brightness.
'Whatever [wood], O Agni,' [with this] one made of śamī,
for pacification.
Indeed, nothing not cut with an axe was savory to Agni;
for him Asanga Prāyogi made everything savory with this [verse].
In that [he says], 'Whatever [wood], O Agni,'
thereby he makes everything savory for Agni;
everything becomes savory for him.
'Bearing [it] night by night without neglect,' with this
he invokes a blessing.
'Kindling Agni on the navel of the earth,' with this
he makes him a conqueror in battle.
When Agni was created, the rakṣases wished to slay [him];
he drove them away with these very [verses]:
'Which armies, approaching,' with this;
thereby he drives away the rakṣases from Agni.
He should put on these very firewood sticks when he fears rakṣases,
for the driving away of the rakṣases.
Moreover, in that season they seize thieves.
Practicing sorcery, he should put on a tilvaka-stick.
This indeed is the vajra among trees;
he dies suddenly.
Whom he hates, him he should then meditate upon with his mind;
the mind indeed is the blessing.
He shuts him off as one having become an offering of speech to Agni.
'Whoever plots against us, and which person hates us,' with this.
Therefore of an agnicit nothing ugly should be spoken,
nor of one who knows Agni.
'Sharpened is my sacred power, sharpened is my vigor and strength,' with this;
by the sacred power he thus sharpens the ruling power,
by the ruling power the sacred power; moreover, he makes sacred power and ruling power yoked together.
With reference to this, this [verse] has been recited:
'Sacred power and ruling power, yoked together, do not falter;
the sacred power indeed quickens the ruling power of the kṣatriya,
the ruling power quickens the sacred power of the brāhmaṇa, when they combine their vigors.'

Now, this is the Vātsapra [hymn].
By this, indeed, Vatsaprī Bhālandana attained the dear domain of Agni.
Through this, he attains the dear domain of Agni.
What is dear comes to him.
Vatsaprī Bhālandana, indeed, the lying ones accused:
'They are thieves.'
He saw this hymn;
with it he conquered the accusation,
with it he attained honor.
Thus, with this he conquers accusation;
he attains honor.
He worships with twelve [verses].
Twelve months are a year;
having reached the year, he secures it.
They are triṣṭubhs,
for the securing of bodily power.
On alternate days he causes him to stride, on alternate days he worships.
He thereby maintains the acquisition and preservation of offspring;
therefore some offspring wander, others are settled.
Then, in that after striding he worships,
therefore the wanderer sets out against the settled one;
therefore the settled one became food for the wanderer.
On whichever day he is about to pile [the fire], on that day he should combine both:
he should stride forward and worship.
Hereby the sacrificer deprives his rival of acquisition and preservation, of food.
'Up may all the gods, O Agni,' [he says];
for all the gods are we here, namely humans.
'Bear thee with thoughts,' [he says];
for whichever thought the fire is set up, with that thought he enriches it.
'Go forward, O Agni, radiant,' [he says];
having enriched him with radiance, he causes him to blow forth.
'Agni has roared like thundering heaven,' thus he should recite if the axle should creak.
This [creaking axle] is capable, when creaking, of creaking out the offspring and cattle of the sacrificer;
he makes this [noise] a mere roaring for it;
then, having pacified it, he prays for the blessing.
'Honor Agni with fuel'—with a gāyatrī he should set it up for a brahmin;
for a brahmin is of gāyatrī nature;
with a triṣṭubh for a noble;
for a noble is of triṣṭubh nature;
with two gāyatrīs for a vaiśya;
for what are two gāyatrīs, that is a jagatī;
moreover, he thereby makes the common people food for the priesthood.
If the ashes should fill up, he should cause them to enter into the waters;
for waters are the womb of Agni;
he places it in its own womb.
He should make loose earth for one desiring cattle;
cattle, indeed, are loose earth;
he becomes possessed of cattle.
Or he should mix bricks;
thereby all his bricks become connected with Agni.
With vigor, indeed, with cattle he, departing, departs along [with it].
'Return again with vigor,' [he says];
thereby he secures vigor again, [and] cattle.
'Be aware of this speech of mine, youngest one,' [saying this] he worships with two [verses] containing 'be aware';
therefore creatures, having slept, awaken again.
He worships with two [verses];
the sacrificer is two-footed,
for firm standing.

Now, this is the meter-layer [chandaściti].
The meters, indeed, are the desirable good of the gods, [and] cattle.
By this, indeed, the sacrificer obtains the meters, the desirable good, cattle.
Yajñasena Caitra, indeed, piled this;
he prospered.
He prospers who piles this [layer].
He sets [the bricks] down in front with Gāyatrī [verses].
The Gāyatrī, indeed, is brilliance, spiritual luster.
He obtains brilliance, spiritual luster.
He places three [bricks].
For threefold is Agni.
He sets them down with [verses] containing the word mūrdhan [‘head’],
thinking: ‘May I be at the head.’
With Triṣṭubh [verses] on the south.
Vigor, indeed, and virility is the Triṣṭubh.
He obtains vigor and virility.
With Jagatī [verses] behind.
Cattle belong to the Jagatī;
he obtains cattle.
With Anuṣṭubh [verses] on the north.
The Anuṣṭubh, indeed, is all the meters;
all the meters, indeed, become piled for him.
Then the Paṅktis;
then the Bṛhatīs;
then the Uṣṇihs;
then the Kakubhs;
then the Virājs.
Thus the meters become piled for him in regular succession.
He sets down two-footed [verses] as the last [bricks].
Two-footed is the sacrificer;
[it is] for firm standing.
The meters, indeed, are the desirable good of the gods, [and] cattle.
The Āhavanīya, indeed, is the sacrificer’s domain;
the dhiṣṇya hearths are the rival’s domain.
If he were to place [them] behind, he would place the desirable good, cattle, in the rival’s domain.
Now, because he places [them] in front,
he thereby places the desirable good, cattle, in the sacrificer’s domain.
He places eight [bricks].
Eight-syllabled is the Gāyatrī;
Agni belongs to the Gāyatrī;
as great as Agni is, so great he piles him.
He should place eight with Gāyatrī [verses] in front of the Bahiṣpavamāna if a rival should surpass him;
eleven with Triṣṭubh [verses] in front of the Mādhyaṃdina Pavamāna;
twelve with Jagatī [verses] in front of the Ārbhava Pavamāna.
He should place these same [bricks] behind the Pavamānas if a rival should surpass him.
The Pavamāna, indeed, is the out-breath;
the Pavamāna is the fiery brilliance of Agni.
With fiery brilliance, indeed, the sacrificer thereby repels the powerful rival;
with fiery brilliance he thrusts him back;
with fiery brilliance he drives him away.
That, indeed, is this driving away of the rival.
Piling [the altar] for the first time, he should pile it knee-high, piled with the Gāyatrī;
piling it for the second time, he should pile it navel-high, piled with the Triṣṭubh;
piling it for the third time, he should pile it chin-high, piled with the Jagatī.
If, having piled a larger [altar], he should pile a smaller one, he would approach a smaller sacrificial performance,
smaller offspring,
fewer cattle,
less food;
he would become worse off.
Now, because having piled a smaller one, he piles a larger one,
he approaches a greater sacrificial performance,
more abundant offspring,
more cattle, more food;
he becomes wealthier.
This, indeed, is Agni’s layer named ‘possessing higher stages’ [uttaravatī].
He becomes better and better, higher and higher tomorrow and tomorrow, who knows thus.

It is to be made with five layers.
Fivefold is the sacrifice;
fivefold are cattle;
therefore it has five layers.
Five layers,
five [layers of] loose earth:
that amounts to the Virāj.
In that it amounts to the Virāj, therefore Agni’s praise is to be performed.
If it were to have five layers [without loose earth], it would fall away from the Virāj.
If it were to have ten layers, it would fall away from both the sacrifice and cattle.
Because it has five layers,
by that it does not fall away from the sacrifice and cattle.
Now, because there are five layers and five [layers of] loose earth,
by that it does not fall away from the Virāj.
By both these forms, indeed, he thereby obtains cattle for him:
that which of them is fivefold, and that which belongs to the Virāj.
Two-syllabled is hair [loman];
two-syllabled is skin [tvac];
two-syllabled is meat [māṃsa];
two-syllabled is bone [asthi];
two-syllabled is marrow [majjan];
that makes ten.
Ten-syllabled is the Virāj;
cattle belong to the Virāj;
he obtains cattle.
It is to be made with five layers.
These worlds, indeed, are three layers;
the sacrificer is two;
he has thereby established the sacrificer in these worlds.
‘What is the first layer? What is the loose earth?’ they say.
This [earth], indeed, is the first layer; plants are the loose earth.
‘What is the second layer? What is the loose earth?’ they say.
The intermediate space, indeed, is the second layer; birds are the loose earth.
‘What is the third layer? What is the loose earth?’ they say.
Yonder [sky], indeed, is the third layer; the constellations are the loose earth.
‘What is the fourth layer? What is the loose earth?’ they say.
The sacrifice, indeed, is the fourth layer; the sacrificial gift is the loose earth.
‘What is the fifth layer? What is the loose earth?’ they say.
The sacrificer, indeed, is the fifth layer; offspring are the loose earth.
So great, indeed, is [the universe];
as great as it is, so much he wins;
that he obtains.
Now, these are the season-bricks.
Agni Vaiśvānara, indeed, is the year;
this, indeed, is Agni’s dear body, namely Vaiśvānara;
the days and nights, indeed, are placed as his bricks.
He places [them] two by two,
for firm standing.
Having thrown [down] avakā plants, he sets [the brick] down,
for appeasement, for non-burning.
He places four [bricks] in the middle layer.
For the intermediate space is, as it were, unestablished;
[it is] for firm standing.
He places twelve [bricks].
Twelve months are the year;
Agni Vaiśvānara, indeed, is the year;
this, indeed, is Agni’s dear body, namely Vaiśvānara.
Agni is thereby piled in his dear body;
the sun is deposited possessing its body.
[With the formula:] ‘You are the inner clasp of Agni.’
Joined together, indeed, is the fire of him for whom these are placed;
joined together, indeed, is the fire of him for whom these are placed.
‘May heaven and earth be adjusted,’ [he says];
[everything] is adjusted, indeed, for him who has piled the fire;
he attains seniority before his equals, who knows thus.
He kindles Agni, indeed, when he piles [the altar];
he makes it blaze up with the season-bricks.

'Lead him up higher,' [saying this] he puts on fire-sticks.
He has made a portion for him just as he is being carried forward.
He puts on three;
for Agni is threefold.
'Let all the gods up... you,' [he says];
for we who are mortals are all the gods.
'O Agni, let them bring with intentions,' [he says];
for whichever intention Agni is set up, he makes him succeed with that intention.
'May the five divine directions favor the sacrifice, the divine [directions],' [he says];
he makes him radiant in accordance with these five directions.
'Song-winged, worthy of praise, seized,' [he says];
for he has songs for wings.
'Having embraced the sacrifice, they have come,' [he says];
for they go having embraced him.
'Golden-haired, sun-rayed in front,' [he says];
that sun, indeed, is golden-haired, sun-rayed in front.
'Savitṛ, the light rising continuously,' he says
just for procreation.
'In his impulse wise Pūṣan moves,' [he says];
Pūṣan, indeed, is cattle;
he obtains cattle.
'Then may the recitations favor our prayers,' [he says];
this is the milking of wishes;
he milks wishes.
'All [songs] have magnified Indra,' [saying this] they go with [a verse] containing the word vṛdh ('to magnify');
they enter upon magnification.
They go with an Anuṣṭubh;
Anuṣṭubh is speech;
speech belongs to all the gods;
Agni belongs to all the gods;
therefore they go with an Anuṣṭubh.
They go with six [verses] from the Āgnīdhra;
six are the seasons;
they go with the seasons.
'This measurer sat in the middle of heaven,' [saying this] he sets down the stone;
for that sun is the measurer of the heavenly world.
'May the benevolence-invoking sacrifice bring [them] here, may god Agni sacrifice, and may he bring the god here,' [he says];
this is a prayer.
'May the god-invoking sacrifice bring [them] here, may god Agni sacrifice, and may he bring the god here,' [he says];
this is the clasp of the prayer.
'By the impulse of food, with the upward-grabber, he grabbed me upward,' [he says];
that sun is the upward-grabber;
this [fire] is the downward-grabber;
rising, he grabs the sacrificer upward;
setting, he grabs his rival downward.
'Both the upward-grabber and the downward-grabber,' [he says];
the upward-grabber is the sacred utterance;
the downward-grabber is the sacred utterance;
with the sacred utterance he grabs the sacrificer upward;
with the sacred utterance he grabs his rival downward.
'Now may Indra and Agni scatter my rivals on all sides,' [he says];
Indra and Agni are force and heroic vigor;
with force and heroic vigor the sacrificer thrusts away his rival on all sides.
They go with four [verses] to the Āhavanīya;
four are the meters;
they go with the meters;
further, the meters are the sacred utterance;
they go with the sacred utterance.

'Stride up to the firmament with Agni,' he says,
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
'Having gone to heaven, the back of the sky,' [he says];
for they go by the back.
'Bestow vigor upon our two-footed and four-footed,' [he says];
he places vigor in two-footed and four-footed cattle.
'Desiring to sacrifice together with the Bhṛgus,' [he says];
for the Bhṛgus initially prospered through sacrifice,
for prosperity.
'From the earth I ascended into the atmosphere,
from the atmosphere I ascended to the sky,' [he says];
this is the ascent of these worlds.
He steps up with five [verses];
the sacrifice is fivefold;
by means of the sacrifice he ascends the sacrifice;
[it is] for non-repulsion.
He offers with milk of a black [cow] having a white calf;
the calf is the day;
the mother is the night;
he makes night give [it] up to the day;
both day and night are milked for him who knows thus.
All cattle, being of various forms, have the same form in milk alone;
because they have the same form [in it], thereby he obtains them.
'Night and dawn,' [he says],
'O thousand-eyed Agni,
you are the fair-winged Garutmat;
sit on the back of the earth!'
He deposits Agni luminous in these worlds;
further, for the maintenance of the vital breaths.
He sets it down with two [verses];
two-footed is the sacrificer;
for stability.
'Offered to, fair of face in front,' he says,
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
'That wondrous [glory] of Savitṛ the desirable,' [he says];
this milking of Agni, Kaṇva Śrāvayasa knew;
this is Agni's milking;
by this the fire-altar builder milks Agni;
Agni milks all milkings, all desires for him who knows thus.
'May we honor you in the highest birth, O Agni,' he says,
just for prosperity.
'Kindled forward, shine before us, O Agni,' [he says];
this is the pipe with ears, continuous light;
continuous light he has placed in him.
Vāsiṣṭha Sātyahavya asked those sitting at the sacrificial session:
'Do you know the pipe with ears?'
'We know,' they said, 'we know the one that is in the forests.'
Cattle are its ears;
he becomes possessed of cattle.
The gods rolled this thunderbolt, the hundred-slayer, the destroyer, against the Asuras;
they crushed them with a hundredfold blow;
the sacrificer rolls this very thunderbolt, the hundred-slayer, the destroyer, against his rival;
he crushes [him] with a hundredfold blow.
'O Agni, him today,' [saying this] he offers with a Paṅkti [verse];
the sacrificer is a Paṅkti;
he establishes the sacrificer in the Virāj;
further, he lays hold of the sacrifice with a Paṅkti oblation.
'Seven are your fire-sticks, O Agni, seven your tongues,' [he says];
so many are the bodies of Agni, sixfold, seven and seven;
whoever, having built the fire-altar, makes these of his dry up, dries up;
he satisfies these [bodies] of his.
'I offer the pile with my mind,' [he says];
this is Agni's undeceptive Vaiśvakarmaṇa oblation;
his rival does not deceive him;
he who has built the fire-altar attains preeminence among his equals, who knows thus.
Agni thought:
'Without a share, I will not hold this splendor,'
he held it with this share;
this Agnihotra is Agni;
with this the fire-altar builder holds Agni;
all milkings, all desires it milks for him who knows thus.
When he piles, he kindles Agni;
Agni inflames him with the Agnihotra.

Now there is this twelve-pot-sherd [cake] for Agni Vaiśvānara.
Agni Vaiśvānara is the year;
he pleases the year.
Moreover, he has made a portion for him just as he has been carried forward.
By the firm establishment of the oblations the sacrificer is firmly established.
By the non-establishment of the oblations he is not firmly established.
When he offers this,
[it is] for the firm establishment of the oblations.
Following them as they are firmly established, the sacrifice is firmly established;
following the sacrifice, the sacrificer [is firmly established].
Moreover, Vaiśvānara is desire;
whatever desire he has, that desire yields to him.
If it were to turn east, the divine subjects would be bewildered.
If south, it would belong to Yama as deity.
If west, it would belong to Sūrya.
If north, it would belong to Rudra.
It is to be offered straight forward,
for firm establishment.
He makes it an entirely offered [cake],
for firm establishment.
Vaiśvānara is the royal power,
the Maruts are the commonalty.
When, having offered this, he offers those to the Maruts,
he thereby binds the commonalty to the royal power.
Moreover, he makes the commonalty obedient to the royal power.
Prajāpati created cattle with Agni as the mouth;
cattle belong to the Maruts.
When, having offered this, he offers those to the Maruts,
Prajāpati generates cattle for him with Agni as the mouth.
They are [cakes] on seven pot-sherds;
seven by seven are the troops of the Maruts,
therefore they are on seven pot-sherds.
He offers troop by troop;
he pleases the Maruts troop by troop.
The troop that is to be recited in the forest, that he should offer in the middle;
this [troop] is the royal power
of the Maruts, the others are the commonalty.
He thereby makes the royal power central to the commonalty.
If he should desire: 'May I slay the royal power with the commonalty,' he should confuse the troop that is to be recited in the forest with the other troops;
he thereby slays the royal power with the commonalty.
If he should desire: 'May I slay the commonalty with the royal power,' he should confuse the other troops with the troop that is to be recited in the forest;
he thereby slays the commonalty with the royal power.
Its offering-verse and invitatory verse should be Triṣṭubhs;
the Triṣṭubh is force and virility.
Having taken away force and virility from the commonalty, he thereby bestows them upon the royal power.
If he should desire: 'May I slay his royal power with royal power, may he fall from his own place,' he should dismember the carriage-pole facing the fire with that troop that is to be recited in the forest;
the carriage-pole facing the fire is the royal power among the Maruts;
it bears the royal power of the kṣatriya;
with royal power he slays his royal power;
he falls from his own place.
'Followers along Indra's path, the divine subjects, the Maruts,' [he says];
this is the following path of the gods;
he makes both the divine subjects and the human [subjects] his followers.
He kindles Agni when he piles him;
he makes him blaze up with the [oblations] to the Maruts.
// Thus ends the third prapāṭhaka, named Stomabhāga, in the upper kāṇḍa.

He offers the shower of wealth,
for the formation of what is unformed, for the appeasement of what is unappeased, for the conquest of what is unconquered, for the containment of what is uncontained.
He offers continuously,
for the continuity of the vital breaths, for the continuity of these worlds, for the continuity of food.
He offers uninterruptedly,
for the non-interruption of food.
If he were to interrupt, he would interrupt the vital breaths.
If there is one whom he hates, he should interrupt for him;
he interrupts his food.
'Hungerlessness and food,' [he says];
these are the forms of food;
by means of forms he secures food.
'Fire and water,' [he says];
this is the womb of food;
he secures food along with its womb.
'May there be a shower of wealth for me,' [saying this] he offers the shower of wealth;
this is a shower of ghee;
[the drops] attend upon the sacrificer in yonder world.
He offers twelve by twelve;
twelve months are the year;
he secures the year.
He offers [oblations] half of which belong to Indra;
with the [oblations] half of which belonged to Indra the gods stripped off the Indra-power and virility of the Asuras;
with them the sacrificer strips off the Indra-power and virility of his rival.
The gods and the Asuras strove together.
Those gods said to Indra:
'With you as our leader let us conquer them.'
He said:
'Let there be a portion for me.'
'Choose!'
they said.
He said:
'May I have half-share of the deities.'
Thence they conquered;
therefore he has a half-share of the deities.
Since [oblations] half of which belong to Indra are offered,
[it is] for victory.
They have Indra at the end;
Indra is Indra-power and virility;
thereby the sacrificer is firmly established in Indra-power and virility, in the sacrifice.
'The Aṃśu and the Raśmi,' [he says];
the Aṃśu and the Raśmi are the face of the sacrifice;
he secures the face of the sacrifice.
'The Adhipati and the Adābhya,' [he says];
the rival does not harm him who has piled the fire-altar;
he attains seniority among his equals, who knows thus.
These are cup-drawings;
these are the forms of the sacrifice;
by means of forms he secures the sacrifice.
'The ladles and the cups,' [saying this] he gathers the sacrificial implements;
having gathered the sacrificial implements, he employs the sacrifice.
He offers vessels;
food is eaten with vessels;
according to its form he secures food.
'The sacrificial grass and the altar,' [he says];
this is a blessing.
'The concluding bath and the making of svagā,' [he says],
for firm establishment.

'Agni and the heat-vessel,' [he says];
this indeed is Agni's holy luster;
by this he secures both radiance and holy luster;
he becomes possessed of splendor and possessed of holy luster.
'Season and observance,' [he says];
season and observance are day and night;
day and night become desired and pleased for him by this.
'One and three,' [he says];
one and three are the meters of the gods;
he secures the meters of the gods.
'Four and eight,' [he says];
four and eight are the meters of men;
he secures the meters of men.
He prospers in the world of the gods by the meter of the gods,
in the world of men by the meter of men.
'One and three, and four and eight,' [he says];
this is the climbing of these worlds,
the stepping over to the heavenly world.
By the chant increasing by two, the Ādityas went to the heavenly world;
by that increasing by four, the two Aṅgirases [went].
When he offers these two chants,
[it is] for the attainment of the heavenly world.
These two, even and odd, are a pair
for procreation.
He places seed by the one increasing by two;
he brings forth the placed seed by the one increasing by four.
'One and three,' [he says],
up to thirty-three: thirty-three are the deities;
they become desired and pleased for him by this.
'Four and eight,' [he says],
up to forty-eight.
The Jagatī has forty-eight syllables;
cattle belong to the Jagatī;
he secures cattle.
'The three-year-old steer and the three-year-old heifer,' [he says];
these are the ages of cattle;
by means of cattle he secures cattle.
'May life-span be formed through the sacrifice,
may breath be formed through the sacrifice,' he says;
this is the formation of the sacrifice;
he has formed the sacrifice in this way.
'To vigor, svāhā!
To impulse, svāhā!' [he says];
these are thirteen oblations;
the year has thirteen months;
he places the year for him in the heavenly world;
he is established in it.
'The chant and the sacrificial formula,' [he says];
the chant and the sacrificial formula are food;
having placed food in himself, having become an eater of food, he moves forth at the end to the domain of the gods.
Agni is the Vasu;
this is his stream;
for him Agni yields all milkings, all desires, who knows thus.
Agni kindles this food when he builds [the altar];
he illuminates it with the shower of wealth.

He offers the vājaprasavya;
by this Agni won vigor, food;
by this the sacrificer wins vigor, food.
He offers for food after food with the vājaprasavya formulas;
vigor is food,
for securing food.
He offers both cultivated and wild,
for securing both kinds of food.
He offers with a ladle made of udumbara wood;
udumbara wood is nourishment,
for securing nourishment.
'At the impulse of the divine Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, with the voice of Sarasvatī, with the control of the Controller, I consecrate Bṛhaspati to imperial rule,' thus he should consecrate if a brahmin should sacrifice;
for this is the Bṛhaspatisava.
'I consecrate Indra to imperial rule,' thus he should consecrate if a nobleman should sacrifice;
for this is the Indrasava.
On a black antelope skin he should consecrate one desirous of holy luster;
this is the form of the Brahman, of the Ṛc and Sāman;
in the Ṛc and Sāman is holy luster;
he secures holy luster.
On a goat skin [he should consecrate] one desirous of cattle;
a goat skin is cattle;
he secures cattle.
He among the fires is consecrated who is piled up;
with his own consecration he causes him to prosper.
The gods ran a race when the plants ripened;
Agni won them;
this is the consecration of Agni;
he is consecrated for food.
This is one of the consecrations;
this is the Bṛhaspatisava;
a brahmin belongs to Bṛhaspati by deity;
he is consecrated with his own consecration.
Concerning this they say:
'It must indeed be offered; for one who has consecrated himself descends to no one.'
He is consecrated with a deity who is a fire-altar builder.
The seasons uphold the realm for one who has consecrated himself;
the seasons are six;
the seasons are the realm-sustainers;
when he offers with six [oblations],
he establishes his realm in the seasons;
they uphold the realm for him.
He offers with [a ladle] taken twelve times;
the year has twelve months;
having reached the year, he secures it.
These oblations are paired:
'To him svāhā, vaṭ!
To them svāhā, vaṭ!'
In that pair he places seed.
He offers the seventh on the chariot head,
for the conquest of the directions;
conquered by the chariot, the directions are won.
Now these are the offerings to the wind;
for the fire-altar builder are day and night yonder;
for the fire-altar builder this wind blows;
wind is breath;
he secures breath.
He offers with joined palms;
for there is no cutting off of this;
moreover, [it is] for enclosing.
Wind is breath;
the hand is most suited for breath;
therefore he offers with joined palms.
He offers three times;
three are these worlds;
these worlds blow the wind for him.
'A sea art thou, cloudy, dripping moisture,' [he says];
these are the forms of the wind;
by its forms he secures the wind.
'Pertaining to the Maruts art thou, the troop of the Maruts,' [he says];
for the wind pertains to the Maruts.
Agni kindles this when he builds [the altar];
he illuminates it with the offerings to the wind.

The gāyatrī is the out-breath;
the cart-shed belongs to the Gāyatrī.
The jagatī is the down-breath;
the sadas belongs to the Jagatī.
The triṣṭubh is the through-breath;
the Āgnīdhra belongs to the Triṣṭubh.
The life-spirit is speckled.
'Speckled stone placed in the middle of heaven,' [he says];
he places this between his out-breath and down-breath in the middle,
for the retention of the vital breaths.
He places [the stones] with the stone as the ninth in the Āgnīdhra,
for nine are the vital breaths;
he thus places the vital breaths in his sacrificer-world.
Twenty-one in the Hotṛ's [hearth],
for firm standing;
for the twenty-first is a firm standing.
Eleven in the Brāhmaṇācchaṃsin's [hearth];
the triṣṭubh has eleven syllables;
the triṣṭubh is heroic power;
he stands firmly in heroic power.
Eight each in the others;
the gāyatrī has eight syllables;
the gāyatrī is the Brahman;
he stands firmly in the Brahman.
Six in the Mārjālīya;
six are the seasons;
the seasons carried him around from the south;
the seasons are the Fathers.
Moreover, six are the meters;
he thus yokes the meters at the opening of the sacrifice.
'United, the year with the Āyus,' [he says];
these are Agni's deities having shares in front;
he gratifies them.
Moreover, here are the dear bodies of the gods;
he secures them.
'United, the year with the Āyus,' [he says];
the year is the abda;
the seasons are the Āyus;
by this the year and the seasons become desired and pleased for him.
'United, Dawn with the ruddy cows,' [he says];
he gratifies Dawn.
'United, the two Aśvins with wonder-works,' [he says];
the two Aśvins are day and night;
day and night become desired and pleased for him by this.
'United, Sūrya with Etaśa,' [he says];
he gratifies Sūrya.
'United, Vaiśvānara with Iḍā, with clarified butter, svāhā,' [he says];
Vaiśvānara is the year;
Iḍā is cattle;
clarified butter is cattle;
by this the year and cattle become desired and pleased for him.
He offers on a clump of darbha-grass;
this is his immortal-piled body;
thus Agni is piled in what is immortal.
Having placed gold, he offers;
he offers on what has fire, what has an abode;
blind would the Adhvaryu become if he were to offer on what has no abode.
For the heavenly world the chariot of the gods is yoked; the chariot of men for desire.
'I yoke Agni with strength, with clarified butter,' [he says];
he thus yokes Agni;
with him yoked he goes to the heavenly world.
If he were to yoke with all [verses], his sacrifice would be yoked, [but] the oblations would be without firm standing.
He does not yoke with two,
for the firm standing of the oblations.
When they stand firmly, the sacrifice stands firmly after [them];
the sacrificer stands firmly after the sacrifice. He yokes with three;
for threefold is Agni;
as great as Agni is, so great he yokes him;
when he is yoked, the whole oblational offering is placed.
He yokes with five;
the sacrifice is fivefold;
as great as the sacrifice is, so great he seizes it.
These two indeed scorch him: he who is piled and he who is Vaiśvānara.
When he touches with [two verses] containing 'waters' before the Yajñāyajñīya [sāman],
[it is] for appeasement.
This is the measure of the sacrifice, namely the Agniṣṭoma;
but what is done higher from this is an increase for him.

Whoever does not yoke Agni when the yoking has arrived does not yoke among those who yoke.
'O Agni, yoke those that are yours,' [he says];
he thus yokes Agni when the yoking has arrived, he yokes among those who yoke.
Whoever does not unyoke Agni when the unyoking has arrived does not unyoke among those who unyoke.
'I untie your halter, your reins,' [he says];
he thus unyokes Agni when the unyoking has arrived;
he unyokes among those who unyoke.
Just as the re-establishment [of the fires], so is the re-piling.
He who prospers by the establishment establishes [them] again.
He who, having piled Agni, should think, 'May this prosper for me,' he should pile this re-piling.
Even he who piles for the first time may pile [it],
for prosperity;
he indeed prospers;
moreover, for pairing.
Whatever oblation the fire-altar builder offers without a brick, it flows away;
flowing away, the sacrifice flows after it;
the sacrificer flows after the sacrifice.
When he places this re-piling,
[it is] for the firm standing of the oblations.
When they stand firmly, the sacrifice stands firmly after [them];
the sacrificer stands firmly after the sacrifice.
He places eight;
the gāyatrī has eight syllables;
Agni belongs to the Gāyatrī;
as great as Agni is, so great he piles him.
Having placed eight space-fillers, he covers them with loose earth;
the gāyatrī has eight syllables;
the gāyatrī is the Brahman;
with the gāyatrī meter, with the Brahman, he piles him.
Having placed eleven space-fillers, he covers them with loose earth;
the triṣṭubh has eleven syllables; it is heroic power;
with the triṣṭubh meter, with heroic power, he piles him.
Having placed twelve space-fillers, he covers them with loose earth;
the jagatī has twelve syllables;
cattle belong to the Jagatī;
with the jagatī meter, with cattle, he piles him.
This is Agni's high altar;
thus Agni is piled possessing a high altar.
Whatever fire was piled for him previously, having settled down beside it he should sacrifice.
Just as here one puts more [fuel] on what is burning, so indeed he successively places more fiery splendor in this [altar].
Concerning this they say:
'Who will awaken the superior when he is sound asleep?'
When he sacrifices, he deprives him of the oblation.
He who piles the fire makes a home;
whatever one gains from elsewhere, one brings it to the home.
He who sacrifices either with an iṣṭi, or an animal sacrifice, or with Soma, whatever fire was piled for him previously, having settled down beside it he should sacrifice;
just as here, having acquired [goods] elsewhere, one brings them to the house, so is that.
Concerning this they say:
'Again indeed is this done with the ladle in the sacrifice.'
Wherever he sacrifices, having placed these twenty-one [bricks] he should sacrifice, for firm standing;
for the twenty-first is a firm standing.

'May the years, O Agni, may the seasons make you grow,' [he says];
he kindles him with the years and with the seasons.
'Shine forth with the divine brightness,' [he says];
he piles him luminous indeed.
'Shine across all the four directions,' [he says];
therefore Agni shines across all the directions.
'When from being there you freed [him] from the curse of Yama, O Bṛhaspati,' [with this] he should act for someone who is ill.
Yama is death;
Bṛhaspati is the holy power;
with as much as the holy power is, with that he heals him.
'May the two Aśvins thrust death away from him,' [he says];
the Aśvins are the two physicians of the gods;
they thrust death away from him.
'Up from the darkness we [have risen],' [he says];
having struck away the darkness of evil, he ascends to the heavenly world.
Ahṛta Haitanāmana was the Hotṛ of Sarvatir Āpāṣṭhi for the fire-altar to be piled.
They said to him:
'Seek those [verses] that contain [the word] water.'
To him the gandharvas and apsarases declared them.
When the kindling verses contain [the word] water,
[it is] for appeasement.
He recites twenty-four;
twenty-four syllables has the gāyatrī;
the gāyatrī is the mouth of the sacrifice;
he takes hold of the mouth of the sacrifice.
Moreover, twenty-four half-months make a year;
this year is established prosperity;
he does not fall away from prosperity,
nor does his prosperity fall away.
He recites gāyatrī [verses];
the gāyatrī is brilliance and sacred luster;
he secures brilliance and sacred luster.
He recites triṣṭubh [verses];
the triṣṭubh is force and heroic strength;
he secures force and heroic strength.
Because there are both gāyatrīs and triṣṭubhs,
he thereby secures both.
Those addressed to Agni that are unexpressed should be used;
for this is unexpressed, as it were, undivided;
he departs divided from evil, from his rival.
He should perform them for the victim belonging to Agni and Soma.
For who would ascend the domain of the gods twice?
They say: 'Having borne [it] for three nights, the fire is to be piled.'
Three-footed is the virāj;
he obtains the virāj.
They say: 'Having borne [it] for six nights, the fire is to be piled.'
Six are the seasons of the year;
the virāj depends upon the year;
he obtains the virāj.
They say: 'Having borne [it] for ten nights, the fire is to be piled.'
Ten syllables has the virāj;
he obtains the virāj.
They say: 'Having borne [it] for twelve nights, the fire is to be piled.'
Twelve months make a year;
the virāj depends upon the year;
he obtains the virāj.
They say: 'Having borne [it] for twenty-four nights, the fire is to be piled.'
Twenty-four half-months make a year;
the virāj depends upon the year;
he obtains the virāj.
They say: 'Having borne [it] for a month, the fire is to be piled.'
Month by month he obtains the year.
For eight months the Vasus bore this;
they won this world, the gāyatrī meter.
For eleven [months] the Rudras [bore it];
they won the atmosphere world, the triṣṭubh meter.
For twelve [months] the Ādityas [bore it];
they won that world, the jagatī meter.
They prospered.
Prosper does he who, knowing thus, bears the fire for a year.
Therefore they say:
'The fire is borne for a year indeed.'
Now these āprī verses:
Prajāpati, having created offspring, thought himself emptied.
He saw these āprī verses;
with them he pleased himself.
Agni is Prajāpati;
when there are these āprī verses,
the sacrificer pleases Agni with them.
This meter is hairy, related to cattle, deficient and redundant,
for procreation.
They are virāj [verses];
the virāj is all the meters;
all his meters are piled.
He should make them all-piled.
These are the vital breaths, larger, as it were, and smaller, as it were;
for these vital breaths are larger, as it were, and smaller, as it were;
he places the vital breaths in himself.

If someone's pan-fire goes out, it is just as if a son, once born, were to die.
If it should go out, he should rekindle that very pan.
For this is Agni's womb;
he generates him from his own womb.
Indeed, he who piles the fire-altar is liable to give birth.
'Whatever moist wombs are yours, O Agni, whatever are nested, whatever drops are yours, O Agni, and whatever navels,
with both of these, being in agreement, knowing, sit down here with your body,'
[with these words he touches it].
In that it is moist [and] possessing a womb,
[it is] for pacification.
A nest is offspring;
a nest is cattle.
In that they are nested,
[it is] for procreation.
Indeed, he who piles the fire-altar becomes an oblation.
If he who has piled the fire-altar approaches someone's woman, it would be just as if an oblation were spilt.
Just as they seek an expiation for a spilt oblation, so they should seek an expiation for him.
If he should approach [her], he should sacrifice with a curd-mixture for Mitra and Varuṇa;
he enters into the state of being belonging to Mitra and Varuṇa,
so that he himself is not spilt.
Agni is an animal.
Whoever approaches an animal from the front harms it;
therefore he does not step from the front facing west;
and therefore he approaches from behind facing east,
for non-injury to himself.
He who piles the fire-altar according to the turns, according to form offspring and seasons are arranged for him.
Those that turn to the right he places on the south; those that turn to the left, on the north;
those marked with three lines he places in the back facing east.
He thus piles the fire-altar according to the turns; according to form offspring and seasons are arranged for him.
They say: 'The fire-altar must be piled with truth.'
'Bhūḥ, bhuvaḥ, svaḥ,' [saying this], the naturally perforated [brick is placed] in front;
for this is as much as truth is in speech;
he piles the fire-altar with truth itself.
The naturally perforated [brick] is the breath;
gold is immortality.
When, having placed a gold brick, he places the naturally perforated [brick],
he places his breaths in immortality itself.
'You are brilliance; grant me brilliance,' [with this] he places the gold bricks.
By these indeed these worlds are held apart;
and by these he piles the fire-altar;
these worlds shine forth.
The gods, having slain the Asuras, were afraid of death.
They saw the meters;
they entered them.
Whatever covered them, with that they covered themselves;
that is the meter-ness of the meters.
Having placed layer after layer, he touches the domain, the meter, with [a verse] addressed to Agni;
he enters into his own deity,
for non-injury to himself.
One desiring heaven should pile the falcon-pile;
for having become a falcon, the gāyatrī flew to yonder world;
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
One who has a rival should pile the chariot-wheel-pile;
for having made a chariot-wheel a thunderbolt, the gods rolled it toward the Asuras;
they crushed them with a hundredfold blow.
Making this a thunderbolt, the sacrificer rolls it toward his rival;
he crushes [him] with a hundredfold blow.
One who has a rival should pile the fore-part-of-a-shaft-pile;
for the Asuras had surrounded the gods from both sides, some in front, some behind;
they drove them away with this;
this is indeed the driving away of the rival.
One desiring food should pile the trough-pile;
for food is eaten from a trough;
by what corresponds to it he secures food.
There is a pot behind,
for correspondence.
One who desires, 'May I go straight to the world of the fathers,' should pile the burial-mound-pile;
he goes straight to the world of the fathers.
And one desiring a village should pile beside another;
just as he piles this up, so he piles up a village for him.
One desiring cattle should pile the swept-together-pile;
just as he sweeps this together, so he sweeps together cattle for him from the directions.

He who piles the fire-altar without a womb, without an abode, becomes without a womb, without an abode.
Water is the womb of the fire.
In that he lays down the pot-bricks,
he piles the fire with a womb, with an abode; he becomes possessed of a womb, possessed of an abode.
'The fire-altar must be piled with its rival beneath,' they say.
Water is the rival of the fire.
In that he lays down the pot-bricks,
he piles the fire with its rival beneath; evil becomes beneath him, his rival beneath him.
Fire is grief;
water is pacification.
In that he lays down the pot-bricks,
[it is] for pacification.
In that there are male pots and female pots, that is a pair;
in that they are in couples,
[it is] for propagation.
He lays down twelve;
twelve months make the year;
reaching the year, he secures it.
A mess of wild rice is the thirteenth;
there is a thirteenth month;
reaching that with this, he secures it.
It is in milk;
by what is milk,
he secures cultivated food;
by what is wild rice,
[he secures] wild food;
thereby he secures both of them.
Thus ends the fourth prapāṭhaka on the shower of wealth in the upper book.

He indeed cuts himself off from Prajāpati, who lays down the head and not the other limbs.
His [altar] becomes a bone-pile, a pile-layer on a burial mound.
In that he lays down this man-pile,
he does not cut himself off from Prajāpati;
his [altar] becomes not a bone-pile, not a pile-layer on a burial mound.
He lays down thirty-six of these;
thirty-six syllables has the bṛhatī;
cattle belong to the bṛhatī;
man belongs to the bṛhatī.
The likeness of the man is laid down,
for he is thus placed.
And this sacrificer indeed does not die prematurely into that world, for whom these are laid down.

Now these pañcāpañcīna [bricks]:
The fire to be piled is loose, as it were.
In that he lays down the pañcāpañcīna [bricks],
[it is] for the firmness of the fire, for non-looseness.
He lays down five;
fivefold is the sacrifice.
As great as the sacrifice is, so great he takes hold of.
'You are the abundance-maker,
you are the wide-space-maker,' [he says];
he places abundance right in the fire;
he becomes greater in offspring, in cattle.
'You sit in the waters,
you sit like a vulture,' [he says];
the fire is indeed a bird;
he makes it a bird capable of sight.
Having become a bird, he goes to the heavenly world, for whom these are laid down.

He should offer an eleven-potsherd [cake] for Agni and Viṣṇu.
Agni is all the deities;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice.
He takes hold of both the deities and the sacrifice.
Agni is the end of the sacrifice from below;
Viṣṇu is from the front.
From both sides he takes hold of the ends of the sacrifice.
Regarding this they say:
'Let there be no sacrifice without Indra.'
In that it is on eight potsherds, by that it belongs to Agni;
in that it is on three potsherds, by that it belongs to Viṣṇu;
in that it amounts to eleven potsherds, by that it belongs to Indra.
Agni is the purifier of the sacrifice;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice.
He takes hold of the sacrifice purified by the purifier.
This is the form of the sacrificial substance of the sacrifice: namely, the cake;
therefore, a cake alone is to be made.
[Some] make two carus.
Clarified butter and milk belong to the cow;
rice-grains belong to the ox.
That is a pair;
he places a pair right at the mouth of his sacrifice.
Clarified butter is brilliance;
he is born from brilliance.
This man is taken hold of for the sacrificial substance;
for this is the likeness, as it were, of a man,
since the form is from it, as it were;
therefore, a caru alone is to be made.
He consecrates himself on [ground] possessing an altar.
With 'May I find!' he consecrates himself on [ground] possessing an altar.
He indeed finds who consecrates himself on [ground] possessing an altar.
Moreover, he has consecrated himself right on the path of the sacrifice.
By the altar the gods found this [earth] from the Asuras;
he indeed finds this [earth] who consecrates himself on [ground] possessing an altar.
Moreover, he takes hold of the path of the sacrifice.
They make a hall with the roof-beam pointing eastward.
When they partitioned these quarters, they partitioned this [eastern quarter] for the gods, this for the Fathers, this for the Asuras, this for men.
He who is consecrated approaches the deity;
he turns toward the quarter of the gods;
he turns toward the eastern quarter.
Moreover, he moves into the domain of the gods.
They enclose it.
Hidden from the divine abode is the human abode;
they hide him from the human abode.
Moreover, [it is] for non-approach by the Rakṣases.
He who is consecrated departs from this world,
for he goes to the people [of the gods];
he ascends toward the world of the gods.
While enclosing, they make openings;
thereby he does not depart from this world;
thereby he is held fast in this world.
He should make the entrance in the front for one desiring heaven;
that sun is the heavenly world;
he brings him into the presence of that sun.
He should make the entrance in the south for one whom he might desire: 'May he prosper in the world of the Fathers';
that is the quarter of the Fathers;
he prospers right in the world of the Fathers.
He should make the entrance in the west for one desiring offspring;
in the west seed is placed;
the consecrated one is seed;
he places seed in him.
He should make the entrance in the north for one whom he might desire: 'May he prosper in the world of men';
that is the quarter of men;
he prospers right in the world of men.
He should make the entrance in the north-east for one whom he might desire: 'May he prosper in both worlds';
that belongs to both worlds;
he prospers right in both worlds.
He should make the entrance on all sides for one whom he might desire: 'May he prosper in all quarters';
he prospers in all quarters.

He salves [his eyes] toward the front,
for procreation.
He salves with a reed-stalk;
for human beings salve with a quill.
He salves with one possessing a tuft;
for human beings salve with one devoid of a tuft.
He salves the right [eye] first;
for human beings salve the left first.
He salves three times on one, three times on the other;
for human beings salve without measure.
He does not apply it repeatedly;
for human beings salve with recurring turns.
Indra slew Vṛtra;
his pupil flew away;
it went to the three-peaked [mountain].
He salves with that, salve from the three-peaked [mountain].
Truth is the eye;
he does not place faith in speech, as it were;
having grasped truth alone, he enters upon consecration.
'Between us, with you, I place hatred, between us [I place] enemies with the great mountain,' [he says];
with a mountain he puts hatred and enemies between.
'You are the protector of sight;
protect my sight,' he says;
he invokes a blessing.
He purifies him upward and downward;
for this breath is both upward and downward.
If he should hate someone, he should purify him obliquely;
he confuses his breaths;
he becomes liable to die.
'May the lord of thought purify you,' he says;
the lord of thought is the sacrifice.
'May the lord of speech purify you,' he says;
the lord of speech purifies him for the sacrifice.
'May the divine Savitṛ purify you,' he says;
impelled by Savitṛ, he purifies himself for the sacrificial substance through these deities.
'With an unbroken strainer,' he says;
this unbroken strainer is the rays of the sun;
with an unbroken strainer he purifies him.
'With your strainer, O lord of the strainer,' [he says];
the lord of the strainer is the sacrifice.
Truly, for the sacrifice he purifies himself.
'May I master the sacrifice,' he says.
Indra slew Vṛtra on the waters;
what was worthy of sacrifice and fit for sacrifice among them, that stepped upward;
those became these plants.
Their brilliance is darbha-grass;
these are dried waters;
whatever brilliance they have, that he secures.
Threefold are the waters: celestial, earthly, belonging to the ocean;
darbha-grass has pervaded them all;
therefore darbha-grass is a strainer.
He purifies with two;
two are the strainers;
two-footed is the sacrificer;
as great as his self is, that much he purifies.
He purifies three times;
for the gods have threefold truth;
and these breaths are three: prāṇa, apāna, vyāna;
as great as his self is, that much he purifies.
He purifies with seven;
seven are the meters;
he purifies him with the meters;
moreover, the meters are sacred formulation;
he purifies him with sacred formulation.
He purifies with twenty-one;
ten are the fingers of the hands, ten of the feet;
the self is the twenty-first;
as great as his self is, that much he purifies.
Three are connected with destruction: dice, women, sleep.
In that he consecrates himself, thereby he turns away from dice and women.
In that the consecrated one stays awake the first night, thereby he turns away from sleep.
In that the consecrated one spends the first night, thereby he secures the sacrifice;
when the Soma is bought, [the night] he [spends], offspring by that;
[the night] when the morrow's pressing [takes place], cattle by that.

The other butter-portions are sacrificed with 'enjoying', but here he sacrifices on both sides with a verse,
for firmness, for lack of slackness.
Seventeen enkindling verses are to be used:
five seasons, twelve months—that is the year;
from the year indeed he extends the mouth of the sacrifice.
Regarding this they say:
"Fifteen enkindling verses are to be used,
not seventeen;
only for the yearly course are seventeen to be used,
for the prosperity of the year."
That is not to be heeded:
seventeen indeed are to be used.
The introductory [offering] should have fore-offerings [and] be without after-offerings;
the concluding [offering] should have after-offerings [and] be without fore-offerings.
On both sides indeed the sacrifice is joined by the fore-offerings and after-offerings,
for this sacrifice is the same.
He deposits the scraping and the stirring-stick;
by that the sacrifice is made continuous.
The self indeed is the fore-offerings;
the domain of the gods is the fore-offerings and after-offerings.
The sacrifice, stepping into the domain of the gods, would exclude offspring if he should omit the after-offerings.
The introductory [offering] should have fore-offerings [and] have after-offerings;
the concluding [offering] should have after-offerings [and] have fore-offerings.
In milk should be the introductory [offering]; in milk the concluding [offering].
This is the bright essence of the waters;
he obtains the bright essence of the waters.
Cattle indeed are the bright essence;
he obtains cattle;
and also he places cattle for him at the entrance and at the conclusion.
Sacred to Aditi should be the introductory [offering]; sacred to Aditi the concluding [offering].
This [earth] indeed is Aditi;
on her indeed he stands firmly with the mouth of the sacrifice;
on her above.
Where indeed at the beginning half of the sacrifice what is prosperous is done, whatever is being done prospers for him.
The introductory [offering] is to be completed,
for the prosperity of the sacrifice.
The verses that are invitatory verses at the introductory [offering] should be the offering verses at the concluding [offering];
those that are invitatory verses at the concluding [offering] should be the offering verses at the introductory [offering],
for recognizing the path,
and also for continuous connection.
If they should do otherwise than this, they would make an interruption.

That which is of two colors is Vṛtra-slaying.
If he should buy with that, his realm would become victorious.
Now, one whose funeral cow is such, another of them quickly dies.
That which is red, black-eyed, black-tailed, black-hoofed belongs to the fathers.
If he should buy with that, the sacrificer would be prone to die.
Now, one whose funeral cow is such, another of them dies in due season.
That which is reddish-brown, brown-haired, white-glancing, bright-eyed—that is the form of the cow that buys Soma.
With her own proper form indeed is [Soma] bought.
She should not be one-eyed, not stunted, not lame, not seven-hoofed;
thus with a complete [cow] is it bought.
With a one-year-old [cow] it is to be bought.
Speech indeed is the cow that buys Soma;
therefore creatures speak speech at [the age of] a year.
It is to be bought in waters.
Plants indeed are Soma;
water is the sap of plants;
thus that sap is produced.
"In buying Soma indeed do I secure the third pressing;
I know [this],"
said Aruṇa Aupaveśi.
Animals indeed are the third pressing;
the skin is of animals;
in that he pours it out on the skin,
thereby he secures the third pressing.
He pours it out on a red [skin];
therefore the form of animals is red.
He pours it out on [the skin of] a draft-ox;
the draft-ox indeed is all the ages of life of animals;
having attained all the ages of life of animals, he secures [them];
moreover, he secures much of the sacrifice.
They pick through the Soma,
for the discrimination of the worse and the better;
moreover, they purify it for the gods.
The Adhvaryu should not pick through the Soma,
nor the sacrificer,
nor the men of the sacrificer, nor should there be onlookers of it being picked through.
If there should be onlookers of it being picked through, creatures would incur hunger;
distress would seize the sacrificer; the Adhvaryu would become hungry.
"Purify the Soma! Throw out what has fallen into distress!" he should say.
Swallowed indeed is that of Soma which has fallen into distress;
those who pick through the Soma tear out the swallowed [portion] of Soma.
Therefore sellers of Soma, though buying much and finding much, are hungry,
for they tear out the swallowed [portion] of Soma.
With "You are ours," [he touches it];
this is his stalking [of it].
With "Bright are your cups,"
he grasps the bright [portion] of it.
With "Towards that god Savitṛ,"
impelled by Savitṛ he grasps [it].
He grasps with an atichandas-verse;
all the meters indeed are the atichandas;
with all the meters indeed he grasps it.
The highest point of the meters indeed is this [verse];
he makes him go to the highest point of his equals.
With five [fingers] he grasps;
fivefold is the sacrifice;
as great as the sacrifice is, so great he has undertaken.
He measures [it], letting go each single [finger];
with [powers] that are not exhausted he measures.
For only thus far is the virility of each of them attained.
Therefore he extends these at will;
he bends [them] back at will.
Ten times he measures;
of ten syllables is the virāj;
he attains the virāj;
moreover, animals belong to the virāj;
he secures animals.
Twice he grasps [portions];
for there are two pressings.
As much Soma as is grasped, that belongs to the sacrificer;
what he heaps up, that belongs to the members of the assembly.
With "For offspring, you!" he heaps [it] up;
for they creep forward from family to family.
Plants indeed are Soma;
unmeasured is livelihood;
if he were to grasp a measured [amount], livelihood would be measured;
now, in that he heaps [it] up,
[it is] for the securing of the unmeasured.
The sacrificer indeed is Prajāpati;
the shoots are creatures;
in that he ties up the Soma,
he ties up the breath of creatures.
In that he says,
"May offspring breathe after you,"
he establishes breath in them.
He ties it up in linen;
linen indeed is made of plants;
plants are the womb of Soma;
he places him in his own womb.
When the sacrifice first went to the gods, the rakṣases wished to slay it;
it crept into the deities.
Of all the deities indeed is the garment;
in that he ties [it] up in linen,
with all the deities he smites away the rakṣases from the sacrifice;
moreover, with all the deities he makes the sacrifice prosper.

There is clarified butter taken four times.
Four-footed indeed are cattle;
he secures cattle.
Having placed gold [into it], he sacrifices.
Clarified butter belongs to Agni;
gold is born of Agni.
With its very body, with its very brilliance, it is offered.
This indeed is Agni's dear body, namely clarified butter;
brilliance is gold.
When he sacrifices having placed gold [into it],
he makes Agni's dear body swell with brilliance;
and also he causes him to grasp the forms.
Clarified butter indeed is cattle;
semen is gold.
When he sacrifices having placed gold [into it],
he places semen into cattle.
Therefore from boneless semen boned cattle are born.
He sacrifices with [the gold] bound [to a string];
therefore the embryos of cattle are not prone to drop.
Prone to drop would be the embryos of cattle if he should sacrifice with it unbound.
"You are swift," [he says];
when indeed she hastens, "May it be so, may it be so," that is her swiftness.
"Held by mind," [he says];
for by mind one holds speech.
"Pleasing to Viṣṇu," [he says];
he has made her pleasing to the sacrifice, to Viṣṇu.
"At the impulse of you of true impulse," [he says];
for impelled by speech [a deed] is done.
"May I obtain the restraint of the body, svāhā!" [he says];
he prays for a blessing.
"You are bright, you are shining," [saying this] he scoops it out,
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
Speech indeed is the cow that buys Soma;
he indeed directs her in this way.
Therefore, having produced [offspring], creatures bring forth offspring.
The newborn calf seeks the teat;
the young child seeks the teat.
"You are thought," [he says];
when indeed she reflects, "May it be so, may it be so," that is her thought.
"You are mind," [he says];
for what one thinks with the mind, what one approaches with the mind, that one speaks with speech.
"You are reflection," [he says];
when indeed she meditates, "May it be so, may it be so," that is her reflection.
"You are the sacrificial fee," [he says];
whatever indeed is given with speech, that is her nature as a sacrificial fee.
"You are worthy of sacrifice," [he says];
for speech is worthy of sacrifice.
"You are of the ruling class," [he says];
for whom speech favors, he belongs to the ruling class.
"You are Aditi, two-headed," [he says];
inasmuch as the introductory [offering] belongs to Aditi and the concluding [offering] belongs to Aditi, that is her two-headedness.
"Be well-facing-forward, well-facing-backward to us," [he says];
for she faces forward at the introductory [offering], and she faces backward at the concluding [offering].

If he should buy with one seized by the ear, she would be Vṛtra-slaying;
if with one tied by the horn, she would belong to the gods of men;
if with one bound by the foot, she would belong to the fathers;
if with one unbound, she would be unextended.
When he says:
"May Mitra bind you by the foot,"
he restrains her with Mitra's noose.
"May Pūṣan protect the paths," [he says];
this [earth] indeed is Pūṣan;
this [earth] indeed brings together her path.
"For Indra as supervisor," [he says];
Indra indeed is the mightiest of the gods;
him he has made her supervisor.
"May your mother consent to you, may your father [consent]," [he says];
consented to by her mother, father, brother, [and] friend he buys her.
"O goddess, go to the god," [he says];
for she is a goddess, [and] Soma is a god.
"To Soma for Indra," [he says];
for she goes to Soma for Indra.
"May Rudra turn you around," [he says];
he places Rudra beyond her.
"Safely, a friend of Soma, come again," [he says];
he has seized [her] visibly.
To Rudra indeed he shuts up the cattle
when he says:
"May Rudra turn you around."
When he says:
"Safely, a friend of Soma, come again,"
he places safety upon her as she returns.
Speech indeed is the cow that buys Soma;
she enters the forms of the gods.
When he says:
"You are Vasu, you are Rudrā,"
"You are this, you are this," speech indeed says to her, whatever it is;
whatever he says here, having obtained this, he secures it.
He steps forth after six footprints;
six indeed are the days [across which] speech is distributed;
speech does not exceed the sixth day;
all speech is obtained on the sixth day;
as much as speech is, that [much] he obtains.
With the right side he steps forth along the right side;
therefore food is eaten with the right hand;
therefore the right side of the body is more vigorous;
therefore birds turn around along the right side.
In the seventh footprint he sacrifices;
seven are the meters;
speech is the meters;
as much as speech is, having obtained it by the oblation, he secures it.
Seven are the meters,
seven the Hotṛ's offices,
seven the village cattle;
those very cattle he secures.
"May Bṛhaspati delight you in favor," [he says];
Bṛhaspati indeed is the sacred power;
with the sacred power he restrains her.
"Rudra has turned [towards you] with the Vasus," [he says];
he has made Rudra with the Vasus turn towards her.
"On the head of the earth I sprinkle you," [he says];
the place of sacrifice indeed is the head of the earth.
"In the footprint of Iḍā," [he says];
the cow indeed is Iḍā;
this indeed is her footprint.
"Possessing clarified butter, svāhā!" [he says];
with the utterance of svāhā he restrains her.

Having placed gold down, he offers.
He offers in that which has fire, in that which has a proper place.
The Adhvaryu would become blind if he were to offer in what has no proper place.
Clarified butter indeed is cattle;
cattle is the footprint.
The wooden sword is the vajra.
When he cuts around the footprint with the wooden sword,
he encloses cattle for the sacrificer with the vajra.
He cuts around after with the horn;
he encloses cattle for him having the same origin.
He pours the footprint into a pot.
From this [earth] indeed she is produced;
for this [earth] is the womb of cattle;
he places them in their own womb.
Cattle mourn when severed from the womb;
when he pours water upon [it],
he appeases it.
He hands over the footprint to the sacrificer.
Thereby the Adhvaryu becomes without cattle;
for he has handed over cattle to the sacrificer.
When he says:
"With your wealth," [he says],
thereby he makes the Adhvaryu share in cattle;
thereby the Adhvaryu, possessing cattle, deposits the footprint in his houses;
he places cattle in his houses.
The heavenly world indeed is the Āhavanīya;
if he were to scatter it near the Āhavanīya, he would place his cattle in another world.
He scatters it near the Gārhapatya;
for cattle attend near to the Gārhapatya;
he places him united with cattle.
Cattle indeed are the footprint;
Agni is Rudra;
if he were to scatter it on that which has fire, he would surrender his cattle to Rudra.
Where it is cooled down, there it should be scattered near,
for the non-burning of cattle.
When they cause the wife to look at the cow that buys Soma,
[it is] for pairing.
"Possessing Tvaṣṭṛ, with you," [he says];
for Tvaṣṭṛ fashions forms.
"May we copulate," [he says];
for from copulation offspring are born.
"I have ascended the eye of Sūrya," he says;
this indeed is the unobstructed, safe path by which that Āditya goes.
"From that path of Āditya," [he says],
he turns aside; "he finds good things," he says;
for he finds good things who buys Soma.
"O Soma-seller, let me buy your Soma, great, of much worth, shining brightly!
Let me buy yours with a sixteenth part!
Let me buy yours with a twelfth part!
Let me buy yours with a hoof!
Let me buy yours with a foot!"
Thus he proclaims the greatness of the cow;
and also he magnifies her;
and also he measures out the sacrifice syllable by syllable.
"Sūvāṅ, Nabhrāṭ, at the ember, Bambhāra," he says;
these indeed are the Soma-guardians of the gods;
from them the metres brought away Soma;
to them he points her out;
and also whatever of the sacrifice is joined here, that he buys around.
He buys with ten;
the Virāj has ten syllables;
thus it is made connected with the Virāj.
He buys with eleven;
ten indeed are the vital breaths of a sacrificial animal;
the self is the eleventh;
thus it is made whole.
He buys with a milk cow;
he buys his milk-mixture;
and also he grasps speech.
He buys with gold;
he buys his bright portion;
and also he grasps brilliance.
He buys with a garment;
he buys what belongs to all the gods;
he buys with a she-goat;
he buys his heat.
When those metres brought away Soma, they did not perceive it because of darkness;
then the Gāyatrī flew up, taking a she-goat;
she made it shine forth for them;
then indeed the metres brought away Soma.
"Bring (ājām) Soma to us": hence the she-goat (ajā).
He buys with an ox;
he buys his draft-animal;
he buys with a bull;
he buys what possesses Indra.
If he were to buy with a bull, he would sell Prajāpati away;
[Soma] is to be bought with a non-castrated young bull; he buys Indra's portion;
he does not sell Prajāpati away.
He buys with a pair of cattle;
he buys his pair.

Soma was yonder.
Those gods sent Gāyatrī:
"Bring that Soma!"
She looked down upon the extended sacrifice;
she considered:
"If I go outside of the sacrifice, I shall go outside of myself."
Soma spoke to her this life-grasping [mantra]:
"Self-born you are,
self-existing you are."
This indeed is the life-grasping [mantra] of Soma.
He grasps this life-grasping [mantra] of Soma;
he, being the Adhvaryu, does not meet with misfortune, who knows thus.
He indeed is Varuṇa at that time, belonging to Varuṇa, when he is tied up; having become Varuṇa he would enter him.
When he says:
"Come as Mitra to us, providing good friends,"
he has made him Mitra;
he just appeases [him].
"Enter Indra's right thigh," [he says];
belonging to Indra indeed is the sacrifice;
Indra is the womb of Soma;
he places him in his own womb.
Indra indeed held him close with his thighs when he arrived at first;
following that pattern, he seats him on the thigh; he uncovers [him];
just as one uncovers when a superior comes, so is that.
"Go through the wide intermediate space," [he says];
he indeed has the intermediate space as his deity at that time,
having moved from here, not having reached yonder.
"Sit on the seat of Aditi," [he says];
belonging to Aditi indeed is the sacrifice;
Aditi is the womb of Soma;
he places him in his own womb.
Being Varuṇa, he has now made him Mitra;
thereby he deprives him of his own deity.
When he says:
"The bull propped up the sky, measured the breadth of the earth; / the sovereign sat upon all worlds; all these are the vows of Varuṇa,"
he has made him Varuṇa;
he makes him swell with his own deity.
"He spread the intermediate space among the woods," [he says]; he wraps it around with a garment;
the garment indeed belongs to all the gods;
with all the gods he encloses the bought sacrifice.
"A yoke-pin you are; injure the injurer," he says,
for the injury of the Rakṣases, for the exclusion of the Rakṣases.
"Belonging to Varuṇa you are;
may Varuṇa prop you up," [he says];
for belonging to Varuṇa is he at that time, having Varuṇa as deity.
With the mind they go towards [it];
towards his greatness indeed they go.
With a cart they carry [it];
therefore the plants ripen into fruit that is to be carried by cart.
The plants would ripen into fruit that is to be carried on the head if they were to carry the bought Soma on the head;
therefore on the mountain the plants ripen into fruit to be carried on the head;
for on the head they carry the bought Soma.
"Move forward, lord of the world," [he says];
for he is the lord of beings.
"Towards all abodes," [he says];
for towards all abodes he moves forward.
"May not highwaymen, may not waylayers, may not evil-wishing wolves find you," [he says];
where indeed the semi-Gandharva Viśvāvasu robbed him when he was being brought yonder, from such Rakṣases indeed he makes welfare for him out of fear.
He indeed is Varuṇa when bought; he is driven to the sacrificer's house;
he is liable to harm the sacrificer if not appeased.
When he says:
"Homage with the eye of Mitra and Varuṇa,"
he just appeases [him];
appeased he is driven,
for the non-harming of the sacrificer.
For all the deities indeed he is driven;
if he were to step forward with a single firebrand, his sacrifice would belong to the fathers.
A great fire is to be piled up;
Agni indeed is all the deities;
with all deities indeed he steps forward to the approaching sacrifice.
Agni and Soma indeed come together;
they would come upon the sacrificer, or upon the sacrificer's cattle;
that animal which is kept, they come upon that as their portion.
The deities indeed did not move towards that animal which was being kept;
from the one hoping in this sacrifice, which other one it was, towards that Agni and Soma moved.
Therefore this is the victim for Agni and Soma.
For Agni and Soma indeed he offers himself as a sacrificial victim, whoever is consecrated;
he redeems his cattle and himself with that very [animal].
It should be stout and fat,
for the redemption of himself.
Therefore one should not eat of the animal for Agni and Soma;
for by it the man redeems himself.
Therefore when the victim for Agni and Soma is completed, one should eat in the sacrificer's house;
then he is redeemed, free from debt.
This indeed is Vṛtra-slaying;
Soma indeed is Vṛtra;
on the previous day indeed Indra slew Vṛtra;
having attacked him on the previous day, he then presses [him] on the next day.
With the two nooses of Varuṇa indeed he is bound, whoever is consecrated;
day and night are the two nooses of Varuṇa;
if he were to complete [it] by day, he would be unreleased from the two nooses of Varuṇa;
it is to be completed at night;
he is released from the two nooses of Varuṇa,
for the non-harming of himself.

One ox is yoked,
the other unyoked.
Then he pours out [the oblations for] the hospitality [offering],
for the continuity of the sacrifice.
He pours out from the hands of the wife.
The wife indeed rules over the household goods;
it is performed given and consented to by the wife alone.
What the wife does in the sacrifice, that is a pair;
for pairhood indeed he pours out from the hands of the wife.
This indeed is the wife's taking hold of the sacrifice, that she acts in the sacrifice;
for taking hold [of the sacrifice] indeed he pours out from the hands of the wife.
"You are the body of Agni, for Viṣṇu you!" [he says];
by that [formula] he takes with the gāyatrī metre.
"You are the body of Soma, for Viṣṇu you!" [he says];
by that with the triṣṭubh.
"You are the hospitality of the guest, for Viṣṇu you!" [he says];
by that with the jagatī.
"For Agni who gives increase of wealth, for Viṣṇu you!" [he says];
by that with the anuṣṭubh.
"For the falcon, the bringer of Soma, for Viṣṇu you!" [he says];
the falcon, the bringer of Soma, is indeed Gāyatrī;
that one indeed he takes again at this time,
not exhausted, for fivefold character.
The sacrifice is fivefold;
as great as the sacrifice is, him he has grasped.
Thus indeed he grasped the metres;
with those metres grasped, he grasps the sacrifice.
As many as follow along after a guest, of them also there is a share;
the metres indeed follow along after him from yonder;
for them this share is prepared.
The sacrifice, when bought, is Varuṇa;
Viṣṇu is [the sacrifice] extended;
extended indeed is the sacrifice at this time;
therefore it is dedicated to Viṣṇu, on nine potsherds.
Indeed, they expand it threefold at the mouth of the sacrifice; with a threefold [rite] they go forth.
Those are three on three potsherds;
[the cake] of three potsherds belongs to Viṣṇu by deity.
The gods indeed led Viṣṇu having made him a dwarf:
"As far as this one strides three times, that is ours!"
He indeed strode across this [earth] first,
then this [intermediate region], then yonder [sky];
therefore it is on three potsherds, dedicated to Viṣṇu.
This indeed is the hospitality of Soma, which is the hospitality [offering];
or rather this is the hospitality of Agni, that they churn Agni in Agni;
or rather, for the sacrifice when bought, they generate a deity;
or rather, they generate spiritual brilliance for him;
or rather, in the Upasads they generate a heroic son for him;
a heroic son is indeed born to him.
Those gods who, being Sādhyas, looked down upon the sacrifice—theirs indeed is whatever redundant [act] is performed in the sacrifice;
a redundant [act] indeed is performed in the sacrifice when they offer fire into fire;
that which is above the top-ring of the sacrificial post, that alone touches them.
They deliberate: "Is it to be brought to conclusion or not to be brought to conclusion?"
If he should bring it to conclusion, he would bring the sacrifice to conclusion at the mouth of the sacrifice.
The fore-offerings of the Upasads are the hospitality [offering]; the blessing is the Tānūnaptra;
therefore it should be made ending with the Iḍā.
The fore-offerings of the Upasads are the hospitality [offering];
therefore they are without fore-offerings.
The after-offerings of the hospitality [offering] are the Upasads;
therefore they are three;
for the after-offerings are three.
The hospitality [offering] is the head;
the Upasads are the neck;
then this other sacrifice is connected [therewith].
Prajāpati's eyelashes are these: that there are [blades of] horse-tail grass [for the prastara]; his eyebrows are the two sugarcane stems.
Prajāpati is the mouth of the sacrifice;
from the mouth of the sacrifice indeed he extends the mouth of the sacrifice.
The horse is sacrificial substance;
Prajāpati is the sacrifice;
the horse belongs to Prajāpati;
therefore the prastara is made of horse-tail grass.
The enclosing-sticks are made of kārṣmarya wood,
for the warding off of the Rakṣases.

Now these Āprī verses:
Prajāpati, having created creatures, thought himself emptied out.
He saw these Āprī verses;
with them he pleased himself.
The sacrifice is indeed Prajāpati.
When there are these Āprī verses,
with them the sacrificer pleases the sacrifice itself.
By means of the omentum of the victim the gods went to the heavenly world at the morning pressing.
When they proceed with the omentum at the morning pressing,
by means of the omentum of the victim the sacrificer goes to the heavenly world at the morning pressing.
Going to heaven, they shook off their bones and bodies.
When they proceed with the limbs at the third pressing,
[it is] for the ransom of the bones and bodies.
They bathe the victim;
whatever impurity and unfitness for sacrifice has settled upon it, they cleanse that.
Having brought up the victim, they churn the fire,
for the securing of cattle.
There are indeed two thunderbolts:
one terrible,
the other auspicious.
That which is dry is terrible;
that which is moist is auspicious.
By the thunderbolt indeed heroic deed is performed;
he thus takes up the thunderbolt
for heroic deed.
"For refreshment, thee!" [he says];
for refreshment [he designates] it.
"Thou art a protector," [he says];
for it protects.
"The divine clans have approached the gods," [he says];
cattle indeed are these divine clans.
"The conveyers, the eager ones," [he says];
the officiating priests and the dhiṣṇya hearths are indeed these conveyers, the eager ones.
"O Bṛhaspati, hold fast the treasures," [he says];
Bṛhaspati is indeed the bráhman;
by means of the bráhman he holds it.
"May they sweeten the oblations for thee," [he says];
made sweetened he seizes it.
"O divine Tvaṣṭṛ, delight in wealth," [he says];
for Tvaṣṭṛ transforms shapes.
"Rich in cattle, the unoffered," [he says];
cattle are indeed the rich ones;
he holds it.
"At the impulse of the god Savitṛ," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ he takes it up.
"With the arms of the Aśvins," [he says];
the two Aśvins are indeed the two Adhvaryus of the gods.
"With the hands of Pūṣan," [he says];
by means of the deities indeed [he takes it up].
There are indeed two nooses:
one terrible,
the other auspicious.
That which is sacrificial is terrible;
that which is non-sacrificial is auspicious.
"With the noose of divine sacrificial oblation of Truth," [he says];
with whichever noose is sacrificial, with that he seizes it.
"I fasten [thee]," [he says];
for men fasten [it].
"Pleasing to such-and-such [deity], thee!" [he says];
for whichever deity the victim is seized, pleasing to that [deity] he makes it.
"Bold men," [he says];
for men hold fast.
Dry indeed is this [post], unfit for sacrifice.
When he binds the victim,
he makes both of them fit for sacrifice.
He binds [it] facing westward;
for facing westward cattle approach the sacrifice.
In its vital breaths the deities are present.
When he binds the victim,
he binds the deities facing westward for him.
"From the waters, from the plants, thee!" [he says];
for it [comes] from the waters;
"Pleasing to the plants, I sprinkle thee," [he says];
for whichever deity the victim is seized, pleasing to that [deity] he makes it.
"May thy mother approve thee, may thy father [approve thee]," [he says];
approved by mother, father, brother, and friend, he seizes it.
"Bring the approving gods to the god-seeking sacrificer," [he says];
to the deities he assigns it.
"Thou art the swell of the waters," [he says];
for it is the swell of the waters.
"A well-tasted oblation being, may the divine waters sweeten [it]," [he says];
made sweetened he seizes it.
Then, when he holds [water] underneath and sprinkles upon [it],
he endows it with sacrificial essence on all sides.
"May thy wind unite with the wind," [he says];
he causes its breath to follow the wind.
"Thy limbs with the adorable ones," [he says];
he has made it sacrificial.
"The lord of sacrifice with the prayer," [he says];
he endows the sacrificer with the prayer of the sacrifice.
The Āghāra is the head;
the oblation is the body.
When he anoints the Āghāra that is to be sprinkled with the victim,
he places the head of the sacrifice on its body.
The chip is indeed a thunderbolt;
the knife is a thunderbolt.
When he anoints with the knife,
he lays it low with the thunderbolt itself.
With that [blade] which has been anointed, he cuts off a portion of the cooked [victim];
for this has become an oblation;
with an oblation he cuts off [a portion] of the oblation.

He cuts off from the heart;
he cuts off by that from the mind.
Moreover, from whatever extent the animal victim is, from that it is cut off.
He cuts off from the tongue;
he cuts off by that from speech.
Moreover, with this [part] alone he eats the sap from the tips of the plants.
He cuts off from the breast-piece;
he cuts off by that from the chest.
Moreover, here that sap is established.
Having cut off from the foreleg, he cuts off from the two flanks,
for the embracing of the sacrifice.
He cuts off from the liver;
he cuts off by that from the middle.
He cuts off from the two kidneys;
he cuts off by that from form.
Having cut off from the rump, he cuts off from the rectum;
he cuts off that last;
for the life-breath is last.
If he were to cut off from another, subsequent [limb], he would close up his breath;
he would be prone to die.
Therefore he cuts off from that last;
for the life-breath is last.
Having cut off from the heart, he cuts off from the tongue;
he thus approaches the animal victim in regular order.
Moreover, so it is cut off from this whole animal victim.
He cuts off eleven times.
Ten indeed are the breaths of the animal victim;
the self is the eleventh;
thus it is cut off from this whole animal victim.
Eleven indeed are these cut-off portions.
He cuts them off twice each;
that makes twenty-two.
Three are the triple limbs;
that makes twenty-five.
By the twenty-fivefold stoma Manu created offspring;
this is indeed the Manu-stoma,
for procreation.
This indeed is cut off from the whole animal victim;
now, of the out-breath, in-breath, and diffused breath, from these it is not cut off.
When he cuts off from the triple limbs,
by that alone it is cut off from their out-breath, in-breath, and diffused breath.
Viśvarūpa, son of Tvaṣṭṛ, vomited upon the cattle.
Therefore cattle have all forms.
The animal victim vomited upon became putrid;
therefore the marrow of the ribs is not to be eaten.
It did not reach below;
that indeed is living.
Therefore he cuts off from here.
He cuts off from the limbs diagonally;
he thereby makes the animal victim lame.
Therefore cattle go moving their limbs diagonally.
The whole animal victim belongs to Agni;
and yet it is seized for another deity.
When he cuts off for Agni from the foreleg in the front part, from the rectum in the middle, [and] from the rump in the rear part,
thus it is cut off for Agni from this whole animal victim.
He makes the rectum threefold;
for the animal victim is threefold at that time.
The triple limbs and the cut-off portions are placed together;
as much as the animal victim is, in that he places life.
He makes the fat threefold;
for cattle have the form of fat.
All the limbs of a fattening animal become fat;
as much as the animal victim is, in that he places the form of fat.
“The sacrifices of men are full of corpses,” said Yajñavacas Rājastambāyana;
“men drive the animal victim away from here,
[yet] they do not go yonder.”
When he sacrifices having placed gold [under it],
with gold as its light he causes him to go to the heavenly world.

He stirs the fat-oblation;
he thereby stirs the sap.
He stirs it with the axe;
for with this he cuts off the other limbs;
thereby he makes it a cut-off portion.
Moreover, by the thunderbolt he secures the oblation.
With soup he appeases whatever follows it;
sap and nourishment, indeed, is the soup;
with nourishment and sap he makes the animal victim prosper.
Moreover, he unites into it as much as the animal victim is.
He covers it with the flank,
for the enclosing of nourishment.
Killed, indeed, dead, gone to yonder world is this [victim].
When he touches the animal victim,
he causes it to come into being again.
They say: 'Having first caused the offering to reach the deities, then the body should be caused to mount [after it].'
When, having offered the oblations, he touches the animal victim,
he indeed, having first caused the offering to reach the deities, then causes the body to mount [after it].
He offers at the half-verse;
this [earth], indeed, is the half-verse;
yonder [sky] is the half-verse;
between these two is the atmosphere;
these creatures are the atmosphere;
he places the sap in the creatures alone.
He sacrifices to Vanaspati;
Vanaspati is Soma;
these creatures belong to Soma;
he places the sap in the creatures alone.
In-breath and out-breath, indeed, are the speckled butter;
the body is the oblation.
When he offers the speckled butter,
he places in-breath and out-breath in the body of the animal victim.
He offers to the quarters;
he moistens these quarters with sap.
He offers the last to the east;
they turn back toward the east.
They bring the part above the udder to the Hotṛ;
the Hotṛ belongs to the All-gods;
these creatures belong to the All-gods.
Creatures, indeed, live along upon the udder;
he makes them all rich in milk.
He performs the subsidiary sacrifice with the rectum;
the rectum, indeed, is the vital breaths;
from the vital breath creatures are born,
for procreation.
He performs the subsidiary sacrifice from the thick end;
for creatures are born from the thick end.
If he were to perform the subsidiary sacrifice from the thin end, he would obstruct procreation;
therefore he performs the subsidiary sacrifice from the thick end.
Without severing he performs the subsidiary sacrifice,
for the non-severing of the vital breaths.
If he were to sever, he would sever the vital breaths.
If he should hate someone, he should sever his [rectum];
he severs his vital breaths.
[Some say:] 'It is not to be offered in subsidiary sacrifice;
if he were to offer it in subsidiary sacrifice, he would offer the vital breaths in subsidiary sacrifice; he would be prone to die.'
Regarding this they say:
'It is indeed to be offered in subsidiary sacrifice;
the subsidiary sacrifice is procreation;
even he is procreated who offers in subsidiary sacrifice.'
'O Ābhāradvāja,' Śucivṛkṣa Gaupālāyana used to say,
'how then were there creatures when the sacrifice was not accompanied by subsidiary sacrifices?'
Creatures were not procreated;
plants did not bear fruit.
When indeed the sacrifice was joined with subsidiary sacrifices, then creatures were procreated,
then plants bore fruit.
Eleven are the fore-offerings;
eleven are the after-offerings;
eleven are the subsidiary sacrifices;
that makes thirty-three.
Thirty-three are the deities;
they are sought after and pleased by these [offerings] for him.
Then this animal victim of his has been sacrificed to not without reaching, not without attainment.
Whatever is its measure, to that he causes it to go.
The entire animal victim is heavenly;
if he were to offer elsewhere than in the Āhavanīya, it would not be heavenly.
They bring the fire from the animal-cooking [fire];
thereby the entire animal victim is heavenly.
From where he cut off the former [portion], from there he cuts off the next;
therefore from the same womb animal victims of various forms are born.
The sacrifice indeed overflowed the gods;
the gods dammed it up with the subsidiary sacrifices, so as not to overflow.
When there are these subsidiary sacrifices,
with them they dam up the sacrifice,
so as not to overflow.
Just as a man has an aperture behind, so the sacrifice has an aperture behind;
the gods stopped it up with the subsidiary sacrifices, that it might be without apertures.
When there are these subsidiary sacrifices,
with them he stops up the sacrifice,
that it might be without apertures.

The gods and the Asuras contended.
Their faculties and heroic powers departed.
Ṛc and Sāman departed from them then,
[as did] cattle, speech, faculty, [and] the in-breath and out-breath.
With them Indra desired, "May I attain union."
"Let Indra possessing bay steeds eat the roasted barley!" [he says];
Ṛc and Sāman are Indra's two bay steeds;
with Ṛc and Sāman he attained union.
"Possessing Pūṣan, the porridge!" [he says];
Pūṣan is cattle;
with cattle he attained union.
"Possessing Sarasvatī, possessing Bhāratī, the puffed rice!" [he says];
Sarasvatī is speech;
with speech he attained union.
"The flatbread of Indra!" [he says];
Indra is faculty;
with faculty he attained union.
"The curd-mixture of Mitra and Varuṇa!" [he says];
Mitra and Varuṇa are the in-breath and out-breath;
with the in-breath and out-breath he attained union.
Then the gods prospered,
the Asuras were defeated.
So he who knows thus attains union with these very faculties, heroic powers, and these greatnesses;
he prospers in himself;
his rival is defeated.
Dīrghajihvī licked the morning pressing of the gods.
It became fermented.
It became the curd-mixture.
Therefore the curd-mixture has as it were the appearance of being fermented.
It should belong to Maitrāvaruṇa at the morning pressing.
Mitra and Varuṇa are the in-breath and out-breath;
the in-breath and out-breath are thereby placed in the mouth of the sacrifice.
The rectum is not to be turned around.
If he were to turn the rectum around, an upward-swelling would kill the offspring.
[It is done] so that the demons do not overpower [the sacrifice].
The sacrificial post is erected in front.
If they were to carry the animal victim in front of the sacrificial post, then the demons would overpower the sacrifice.
It is to be carried between the fire and the sacrificial post,
so that the demons do not overpower [the sacrifice].

"Go to the ocean, svāhā!" he says;
he thereby sacrifices to the place of waters.
"Go to the atmosphere, svāhā!" [he says];
he thereby deposits semen.
"Go to divine Savitṛ, svāhā!" he says,
for procreation indeed.
"Go to day and night, svāhā!" he says;
for after day and night offspring are born.
"Go to Mitra and Varuṇa, svāhā!" he says;
Mitra and Varuṇa are the in-breath and out-breath;
he deposits in-breath and out-breath in them.
"Go to Heaven and Earth, svāhā!" he says;
by these two he encloses them after they have been created.
"Go to the metres, svāhā!" he says;
the metres are speech;
he deposits speech in them.
"Go to Soma, svāhā!" he says;
Soma is food;
he deposits food in them.
"Go to the sacrifice, svāhā!" he says;
he makes them worthy of sacrifice.
"Go to the heavenly cloud, svāhā!" he says;
he pours down rain for them.
"Go to Agni Vaiśvānara, svāhā!" he says;
Agni Vaiśvānara is the year;
he establishes them in the year;
he makes them possess the lifespan of a year.
"Hold back mind, heart!" he says,
for the protection of the vital breaths.
"To the plants, thee!" he says;
he deposits sap in the plants.
Whoever, whether knowing or unknowing, offers subsidiary sacrifices making pairs, he pairs these vital breaths.
When he says:
"Hold back mind, heart!",
he thereby arranges the vital breaths according to the seasons.
The animal victim, when seized, grieves;
its sorrow comes together in the middle;
it enters the heart.
If it should touch a man, sorrow would reach humans;
if it should touch a cow, sorrow would reach cattle;
if it should touch wood, sorrow would reach trees;
if it should touch grass, sorrow would reach plants;
if it should touch water, sorrow would reach the waters.
What is moist and without water, that is appeased;
into that it should be cast down;
thereby he averts sorrow from the deity.
Thus ends the tenth prapāṭhaka named Pātnīvanta in the third kāṇḍa.

King Soma, immortal, pressed, the sap of plants, of the waters;
by truth [is] real faculty, the special drinking, the bright [juice] of the stalk;
this faculty of Indra, milk, immortal honey.
From the waters the curlew, the Āṅgirasa, drank off [the milk] through thought;
by truth [is] real faculty, the special drinking, the bright [juice] of the stalk;
this faculty of Indra, milk, immortal honey.
From the waters the swan, dwelling in the clear, drank off Soma with the meters;
by truth [is] real faculty, the special drinking, the bright [juice] of the stalk;
this faculty of Indra, milk, immortal honey.
From food, from the flowing [liquor], the nobility drank off the sap with the sacred word;
by truth [is] real faculty, the special drinking, the bright [juice] of the stalk;
this faculty of Indra, milk, immortal honey.
Semen abandons urine as the faculty enters the womb;
the embryo, covered with the chorion, abandons the amnion through birth;
by truth [is] real faculty, the special drinking, the bright [juice] of the stalk;
this faculty of Indra, milk, immortal honey.
Having seen the two forms, Prajāpati distinguished truth and untruth;
Prajāpati placed lack of faith in untruth, faith in truth;
by truth [is] real faculty, the special drinking, the bright [juice] of the stalk;
this faculty of Indra, milk, immortal honey.
Through the Veda Prajāpati drank off the two forms, the pressed and the unpressed;
by truth [is] real faculty, the special drinking, the bright [juice] of the stalk;
this faculty of Indra, milk, immortal honey.
Having seen the sap of the flowing [liquor], Prajāpati drank off the bright with the bright,
the milk, the Soma;
by truth [is] real faculty, the special drinking, the bright [juice] of the stalk;
this faculty of Indra, milk, immortal honey.

At the impulse of god Savitar, with the arms of the two Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, I take you.
Three times eleven are the thirty-three gods, rich in gifts,
having Bṛhaspati as purohita; at the impulse of god Savitar
let the gods protect you with the gods!
Let the first ones anoint you with the second ones;
the second ones you with the third ones;
the third ones you with truth;
truth you with the sacred word;
the sacred word you with the yajus-formulas;
the yajus-formulas you with the chants;
the chants you with the verses;
the verses you with the invitatory formulas;
the invitatory formulas you with the offering verses;
the offering verses you with the vaṣaṭ-calls;
let the vaṣaṭ-calls anoint you with the oblations!
Let the oblations fulfill your desires, O so-and-so!
With the brilliance of the two Aśvins I anoint you for spiritual splendour!
With the heroic power of Sarasvatī I anoint you for fame, for edible food!
With the faculty of Indra I anoint you for vigour, for strength!
Bhūḥ, svāhā!
My head is fortune, my face is fame, my hair and beard are flash [of glory];
my breath is the king, immortal, my sight the emperor, my hearing the sovereign.
My tongue is auspicious, my speech great, my mind fury, my wrath self-ruler;
joys and supreme pleasures are my fingers, limbs, Mitra my overwhelming might.
My arms are strength, faculty; my two hands are action, heroic power;
my torso is nobility, my chest [dominion].
My ribs are the realm, my belly, shoulders, neck, and hips;
my two thighs, two cubits, two knees are the clans, my limbs on all sides.
My navel is intellect, discernment; my anus respect, my pudenda [dignity];
my two testicles are joys and delights, my penis is Bhaga, good fortune.
My bodily hairs are forward effort, my skin compliance, approach;
my flesh submission, wealth my bone, my marrow compliance.
With two shanks, with two feet I am steadfast, an established king among the clan;
I am established in the priesthood, I am established in the nobility;
I am established among horses, I am established among cattle;
I am established in offspring, I am established on the back [of the sky];
I am established in the breaths, I am established in the self;
I am established in heaven and earth, in the sacrifice!

The kindling [quarter] of the quarters, lying near, winning the sun, may Mādhava protect our sweet semen.
May the god Agni, hard to overcome, unbeguiled, protect this dominion, may he protect us.
May the Rathantara protect us through the Sāmans, the Gāyatrī of all forms among the metres;
May the Trivṛt stoma with its division, the ocean, the wind protect this vigor for us through the day.
The fierce one of the quarters, overpowering, bestowing strength, pure on the bright day, vigorous;
Do you protect us from this by Indra's overlordships, uphold this great dominion on all sides.
The Bṛhat Sāman upholding dominion, whose bull has grown, the Triṣṭubh, vigor adorned, fierce with heroes;
May Indra protect this middle with the fifteenfold stoma, with the wind, with the ocean.
The eastern of the quarters, accompanied by glory, glorious; the All-Gods, the rainy season among days, sun-winning;
May I be able to achieve that in the Vairūpa Sāman; I cause him to enter among the clans with the Jagatī.
May this dominion be hard to overcome, vigor unassailable, forceful, full of force;
The All-Gods with the seventeenfold [stoma], brilliance, this dominion, fierce with the watery wind.
The supporter of the quarters upholds this dominion; may the vigor of the reaches of the quarters be friendly;
Mitra and Varuṇa, note [us] with the autumn, with the day; grant great shelter to our realm.
In the Vairāja Sāman is my thought, with the Anuṣṭubh is manly vigor [and] force gathered;
This dominion is friendly, bestowing moisture; O Mitra and Varuṇa, protect [it] with your overlordships.
The sovereign of the quarters, having a thousand Sāmans, full of force; may the winter season protect us with its division;
With wind seeking help, lofty like the Śakvarī; may the [quarter] dripping with ghee protect us [...];
Sun-winning, yielding well, rich in milk for us; may she who drips with ghee favor this sacrifice;
You are the guardian, walking in front and behind; O Bṛhaspati, yoke the speech relating to Yama.
The upward one of the quarters, the delighting ones reaching among plants; Savitṛ [favor] us with the year, with the day;
The Revat Sāman, and the Atichandas among metres; may she without enemies be gentle to us.
The stoma in the thirty-threefold, the mistress of the world; with wind shining bright, sing praise to us;
Dripping with ghee under Savitṛ's overlordship, rich in milk; may the bestowal, the quarter, be for us.
May Anumati today approve our sacrifice among the gods;
And may Agni, carrier of oblations, be a joy to the worshipper.
Do you, O Anumati, approve, and make well-being for us;
Impel us to purpose, to skill; may he prolong our lifespans.
May Vaiśvānara come to our aid from afar,
Agni with the draft of praise.
Asked in heaven...
The firm one of the quarters, wife of Viṣṇu, not terrible, ruling over this [dominion], who is the Manotā of force;
May Bṛhaspati, Mātariśvan, and Vāyu, the winds blowing together, sing praise to us.
The prop of heaven, support of the earth, ruling over this moving world, the wife of Viṣṇu,
Spacious, invigorating, prosperous, may she be auspicious to us in the lap of Aditi.
With what help for us...
Who yokes today to the pole the draft-oxen of Ṛta, laborious, radiant, bearing grudge against wrong,
Having arrows in their mouth, piercing the heart, bringing joy? Whoever accomplishes their maintenance, he shall live.

"At the impulse of god Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, I take you," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, he takes it with the deities.
Prajāpati indeed knows the plants joint by joint.
The horse belongs to Prajāpati.
When he cuts the barhis with a horse-rib sickle,
[it is] for the non-injury of the plants.
"You are cow-dwelling," [he says];
he places wealth in the sacrificer.
"Scorched is the demon," [he says];
[it is] for the smiting of the demons.
"Forward has this Dhiṣaṇā gone to the barhis," [he says];
Dhiṣaṇā is indeed knowledge;
with knowledge indeed he goes to it.
"Fashioned by Manu, fashioned according to self-nature," [he says];
for it was fashioned by Manu, fashioned according to self-nature.
"With it the sages carry [the sacrifice] forward from the east," [he says];
the Brahmins are the sages;
the ṛṣis are the sages.
"Pleasing to the gods, here to sit upon the barhis," [he says];
he makes it pleasing to the gods.
"You are impelled by the gods," [he says];
whatever [exists] here, that is impelled by the gods.
As one performs an action having announced it to a superior, even so, having announced it to the gods, he cuts the barhis.
As much as he marks out, all that he cuts.
If he does not cut all of it, he makes that redundant in the sacrifice.
What becomes redundant in the sacrifice, with that he strengthens his rival.
He marks out a single clump;
all of that he cuts,
for the non-redundancy of the sacrifice.
"The crest of Viṣṇu," [he says];
Viṣṇu's crest was indeed cut off;
it entered the earth.
If he should not cast off the first [stalk], he would meet with misfortune.
"At the impulse of god Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, I cut you, the barhis, the seat of the gods," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, he cuts it with the deities.
"May I prosper below your joint, not above," [he says];
[it is] for the non-injury of the plants.
"May I, your cutter, not be injured," [he says];
as much as an unknowing Adhvaryu thus cuts the barhis, so much of his own self is diminished;
the self of him who knows thus is not diminished.
"From here, O barhis, grow out with a hundred shoots!" [he says];
he places abundance in the plants.
"May we grow out with a thousand shoots!" [he says];
he utters a blessing.
"You are the girdle of Aditi," [he says];
Aditi is indeed this [earth];
he makes it her girdle.
"The tying-cord of Indrāṇī," [he says];
Indrāṇī indeed in the beginning, desiring prosperity, tied herself up among the deities;
she prospered;
[it is] for prosperity;
he prospers who knows thus.
He ties together the barhis;
the barhis is indeed offspring;
[it is] for the continuity of offspring, for the non-scattering of offspring.
Therefore offspring are tied together by sinews.
"May Pūṣan tie your knot," [he says];
Pūṣan is indeed nourishment;
he places nourishment in the sacrificer.
"May it not stand against you," [he says];
[it is] for the non-injury of the sacrificer.
"The waters, the Aśvins, the seven ṛṣis have cleansed you; / With the rays of the sun I take the barhis, the banner of the dawns," [he says];
the Aśvins are indeed the physicians of the gods;
the two heal it.
"With the arms of Indra I raise you up," [he says];
with the arms of Indra he raises it up.
"With the head of Bṛhaspati I bring [you]," [he says];
Bṛhaspati is indeed the sacred utterance;
with the sacred utterance he brings it.
"The broad atmosphere," [he says];
[it is] for fulfillment.
"I seat you on the back of Aditi," [he says];
Aditi is indeed this [earth];
on her he has seated it.
"You are the barhis that goes to the gods," with this [formula] he addresses it once seated;
indeed, much of this, when it was being brought the day before, spills here and there;
he has delivered it unspilled to the deities;
[it is] for the non-injury of the sacrificer.

On the previous day they prepare the kindling and the barhis.
Taking hold of the sacrifice, he fasts.
Prajāpati created creatures.
His two pots slipped down.
With these two pots he restored [them].
In that these two pots are [used],
he restores the two pots of Prajāpati.
"Be purified for the divine work," [he says];
he purifies them for the gods.
"You are the filter of the Vasus," [he says];
this is indeed the portion of the Vasus;
he makes it for them.
He covers [it] with the filter;
he unites the milk of plants and of cattle.
He restrains his speech,
for the support of the sacrifice.
He sits holding [it];
for, as it were, they milk while holding.
"You are sky; you are earth," [he says];
with these two he heats it.
"The cauldron of Mātariśvan," [he says];
free atmosphere indeed is the cauldron of Mātariśvan,
for the support of the atmosphere.
"Which one have you milked?
She is all-life;
she is all-being;
she is all-action," [he says].
Yonder [sky] indeed is all-life;
the atmosphere is all-being;
this [earth] is all-action.
He causes these worlds to yield in proper order;
he who knows thus milks these worlds yielded in proper order.
He addresses three [cows] with a yajus;
three indeed are these worlds;
he places sap in these worlds;
therefore creatures live upon these worlds.
"Offered is the drop; offered is the spark," he addresses [it],
for non-spilling.
"For Indra, for the gods, milk much oblation!" he proclaims three times;
for the gods are thrice-true.
He should not milk into a wooden vessel;
the wooden vessel is fiery;
he would sacrifice with an exhausted oblation.
Regarding that the Dātreyas used to say:
"The oblation has the sacrificial cake for its mouth;
not from this side is there an exhaustion of the oblation of the cake;
one should milk indeed."
A Śūdra should not milk;
he is born from non-being;
he would become non-being.
What passes through the filter is oblation;
a Śūdra should not milk the agnihotra,
for that they do not purify.
"Mix together, O righteous ones, sweetest with the wave, / mingling milk with milk, gladdening, for the winning of wealth!"
Thus he pours [it] in return,
for cookedness;
for the gods desire what is cooked.
"I curdle you as a portion for Indra with Soma," [he says];
he makes it Soma.
Continuous indeed is the Soma-drink for him who, knowing thus, drinks the sānnāyya.
"You are unexhausted; for Viṣṇu you [I take]," [he says];
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
he makes it unexhausted for the sacrifice.
"O Viṣṇu, protect the oblation," [he says];
as one says, "This is your share, protect it," so indeed he says to Viṣṇu: "This is your oblation, protect it."
He should not cover it with an earthen vessel;
the oblation would belong to the Fathers as deity.
He should cover it with a wooden vessel;
the wooden vessel is fiery;
Agni himself protects the oblation.
It should be made watery;
water is demon-slaying;
demons do not cross water;
[it is] for the smiting of the demons.

He who sacrifices without taking hold of faith—gods and men do not put faith in his sacrifice.
He leads forward the waters;
the waters are faith;
taking hold of faith he sacrifices.
The waters are the sacrifice;
having extended the sacrifice, he performs [it].
The waters are the dear domain of the gods;
having brought forward the dear domain of the gods, he performs [it].
The waters are truth;
taking hold of truth, he sacrifices.
The waters are demon-slaying;
[it is] for the smiting of the demons.
The waters are a thunderbolt;
he hurls a thunderbolt at his rival,
for laying [him] low.
The waters are faith;
gods and men put faith in the sacrifice of him, knowing thus, for whom one knowing thus brings forward the waters.
"O divine waters, moving first," [he says];
he brings forward the sacrifice.
"At the impulse of god Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, I take you," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, he takes it with the deities.
"Wide atmosphere," [he says],
for the sake of the successful sacrifice.
"Scorched is the demon," [he says];
[it is] for the smiting of the demons.
"You are the yoke-pin; injure the injurer," [he says];
this Agni is indeed named 'belonging to the yoke';
if he should pass over [him] without taking hold, he would afflict the sacrificer with heat.
Two indeed are a man's rivals:
he whom he hates and he who hates him; those two he afflicts with heat.

"You are of the gods the best conveyer, the most winning, the most satisfying, the most pleasing, the best caller of the gods," [he says];
he makes it for the gods the best conveyer, the most winning, the most satisfying, the most pleasing, the best caller of the gods.
"You are the unswerving cart of oblation;
be firm,
do not waver," [he says];
he makes it firm.
"You are the stride of Viṣṇu;
broad for the wind," [he says].
Whatever the wind does not blow upon, that belongs to Varuṇa.
He makes it un-Varuṇic.
"I gaze upon you with the eye of Mitra," [he says];
he makes them friendly.
"Take strength," [he says];
he places strength in the sacrificer.
"For abundance to you," [he says];
he causes them to prosper.
"At the impulse of god Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan,
let the five restrain [you];
for your protection, not for malice;
I pour you out pleasing to Agni," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, he pours them out with the deities.
"Let the five restrain," [he says];
the seasons indeed are five;
he thus gratifies the seasons;
gratified, they serve him.
"For your protection, not for malice," [he says];
[it is] for the smiting of the demons.
"Pleasing to Agni, you," [he says];
he makes them pleasing to Agni.
Moreover, to whatever deity he pours out, pleasing to that one he makes them.
"This for the gods, this for us together," [he says],
for separation.
"Let the doorposts stand firm," [he says];
when the oblation was taken, these worlds trembled upwards;
the gods made them firm with this sacrificial formula;
when he knows this sacrificial formula, [it is] for the support of these worlds.
"I have been freed from the fetter of Varuṇa," [he says];
he is freed from the fetter of Varuṇa,
for the non-injury of himself.
"I have looked upon the light of heaven," [he says];
he enters as it were into darkness, into the cart's enclosure;
he looks forth upon the light of heaven.
"Light belonging to all men," [he says];
that sun indeed is the light belonging to all men.
"I follow the broad atmosphere," [he says].
If he were to say "Go [apart]!", the Adhvaryu would run away.
"I follow the broad atmosphere," [he says];
the Adhvaryu does not run away.
"In the lap of Aditi I seat you," [he says];
this [earth] indeed is Aditi;
upon her he has seated them.
"O Agni, protect the oblation," [he says, and] sets [it] down in front;
Agni protects the oblation.

"Purified are you two by the mind of Viṣṇu," [he says];
Viṣṇu indeed is the sacrifice;
for the sacrifice he purifies the two.
"Let god Savitṛ purify you upwards," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, he purifies them upwards.
"Pleasing to Agni, you," [he says];
he makes them pleasing to Agni.
Moreover, for whichever deity he sprinkles [them], pleasing to that one he makes them.
"What impure thing has touched you, purify that," [he says];
he purifies them.
"You are the skin of Aditi," [he says];
this [earth] indeed is Aditi;
he makes it the skin of her.
"Shaken off is the demon," [he says];
[it is] for the smiting of the demons.
He spreads out the black-antelope skin with its neck turned westward;
therefore cattle approach the sacrifice facing westward.
"You are a broad pressing-stone, belonging to the tree," [he says];
he makes it a pressing-stone.
"You are Agni's tongue, the releasing of speech," [he says];
for this is Agni's tongue, the releasing of speech;
for when cattle eat of the plants, then they release speech.
"For life, you two," [he says];
he places life in the sacrificer.
"You are a great pressing-stone, belonging to the tree," [he says];
he makes it a pressing-stone.
"Prepare the oblation for the gods," [he says];
he prepares it for the gods.
"Slain is the demon," [he says];
[it is] for the smiting of the demons.
"O maker of oblation, come!" [he says];
whoever is the maker of oblation of the gods, him he calls.
Three times he calls;
for the gods have three truths.
When Manu Śraddhādeva was sacrificing, speech that slays the asuras entered the sacrificial weapons.
As she was speaking, as many asuras as heard [her], so many ceased to be on that day.
A brāhmaṇa who knows thus, having settled in the midst of his rivals, should sacrifice;
as many of his rivals as hear the sacrificial weapons, their brilliance, sensory faculty, and manly vigor he takes away.
"You are a cock, honey-tongued; with you may we conquer encounter after encounter," [he says];
by this indeed the sacrificer conquers his rival encounter after encounter.
"Speak nourishment, speak strength, speak increase of wealth," [he says];
he prays for a blessing.

"You are rain-grown;
let rain-grown [water] receive you," [he says],
for a firm foundation.
With blood indeed the gods appeased the demons away from cattle, with husks from plants.
In that he says, "Winnowed away is the demon, winnowed away is malice,"
with just so much he appeases the demons away from the sacrifice.
"Let god Savitṛ separate you," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, he separates them.
"Separate with a good sifting," [he says];
he prays for a blessing.
He should make them well-husked;
he makes them pure and sacrificial.
Three times he husks [them];
for the gods have three truths.
"You are the skin of Aditi," [he says];
this [earth] indeed is Aditi;
he makes it the skin of her.
"Shaken off is the demon," [he says];
[it is] for the smiting of the demons.
He spreads out the black-antelope skin with its neck turned westward;
therefore cattle approach the sacrifice facing westward.
"You are the support of Aditi," [he says, and] places the yoke-pin beneath.
These two [worlds] were together;
they separated by the measure of a yoke-pin after yoke-pin.
In that he places the yoke-pin beneath,
[it is] for the upholding of these two.
"You are grain; gladden the gods," [he says];
by the vigor of this sacrificial formula, as far as one deity desires,
as far as one [desires], so far her oblation spreads out;
he spreads out with offspring and cattle who knows thus.
"For out-breath you, for in-breath you, for through-breath you," [he says];
out-breath, in-breath, and through-breath—these he places in the sacrificer.
"Touch together along the long reach of the hand;
for life, you two," [he says];
he places life in him.
"I look upon you with the eye of Mitra," [he says];
he makes them friendly.
"Let god Savitṛ, the golden-handed, receive you," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, he receives them.

"Burnt up is the demon," [he says],
for striking away the demons.
When he rolls a live coal underneath,
by that he places light in this world;
when [he rolls one] above, by that [he places light] in the atmosphere.
Yonder sun becomes light for him in yonder world.
Moreover, it becomes daylight for him in yonder world; all these worlds become full of light for him who knows thus.
He puts down three [potsherds] joined together;
three indeed are these worlds;
for the upholding of these worlds.
"O Agni, cast away the raw-flesh-eating Agni," [he says];
having struck away that which is raw-flesh-eating, carrion-eating, he puts down the sacrificial [potsherds] on the place of sacrifice to the gods.
"You are firm, make firm the earth," [he says];
he makes the earth firm.
"You are the supporter, make firm the atmosphere," [he says];
he makes the atmosphere firm.
"You are the maintainer, make firm the sky," [he says];
he makes the sky firm.
"You are the ordinance, make all the regions firm," [he says];
he makes the quarters firm.
Prajāpati indeed, when he came into being, came into being in just this measure.
First one potsherd of the head comes into being,
then the second, then the third, then the fourth, then the fifth, then the sixth, then the seventh, then the eighth.
In that he puts down eight,
the sacrificer thereby prepares himself;
prepared, he goes after [him] in yonder world.
In that he puts down eight,
thereby he attains the gāyatrī meter;
in that [he puts down] eleven,
thereby the triṣṭubh;
in that [he puts down] twelve,
thereby the jagatī.
Moreover, he puts down all [potsherds] commensurate with the meters.
"You are piled up, you are piled around," [he says];
he makes them possessive of yajus formulas.
"Be heated with the heat of the gharma of the Vasus, Rudras, Ādityas, Bhṛgus, and Aṅgirases," [he says];
he heats them with the heat of these deities.
"Which potsherds the wise pile upon the gharma, / may Indra and Vāyu unyoke them in accordance with Pūṣan's ordinance," [he says];
by the yajus they are yoked, by the yajus they are unyoked.

"At the impulse of god Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, I pour you together," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, with the deities he pours it together.
He pours it together over a strainer;
he makes it an oblation.
"Let god Savitṛ purify you upwards," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, he purifies them upwards.
He adds the waters;
the waters are immortality;
he makes the oblation immortal.
"Let the waters unite with the plants, / the plants with the sap, / the wealthy ones with the jagatīs," [he says];
some of these indeed invigorate the others;
the waters invigorate the plants,
the plants [invigorate] the waters.
"Auspicious waters have united with the auspicious," [he says];
the waters are the wealthy ones,
the plants are sweet,
cattle are the jagatīs;
having united the waters, the plants, and cattle for him, he makes them together full of sweetness.
"You are the head of Makha," he says;
Makha is the sacrifice;
this head of his is prepared.
"Let the clans sit; / to give birth to you," [he says];
he makes a pair.
"You are the gharma, possessing all life; / O gharma, rest in the gharma; / spread wide; / may the lord of the sacrifice spread wide," [he says];
he spreads it out.
"Spread wide towards the earth and the sky," [he says];
he spreads these two.
This gharma indeed is placed on the fire every half-month;
unappeased, it might burn up the sacrificer's cattle with its glow.
When he carries fire around it,
he makes it cattle;
for appeasement, so as not to burn [them].
Three times he carries fire around it;
three indeed are these worlds;
from these worlds he appeases it.
The demons wished to slay the sacrificial cake when it was set upon the fire;
there was in the sky an Agni named Nāka, slayer of demons;
he struck the demons away from it.
In that he says,
"Let god Savitṛ cook you on the highest vault of the earth,"
[it is] for striking away the demons.
The head of the sacrifice is this sacrificial cake;
the veda-grass is the hair;
as are the potsherds of the head, so are the potsherds;
as is the brain, so is the sacrificial cake.
It should be made well-cooked,
for purity.
If he were not to cover the sacrificial cake, the brain would become visible.
Because he covers it,
therefore the brain is hidden; he causes it to take skin;
therefore the brain is covered around. He covers it with ashes;
therefore bone is covered with flesh. He covers it with embers;
therefore the head is covered with hair.
If he were to cover it with ashes without causing it to take skin, the Adhvaryu would become gray-haired;
he covers it with embers;
the Adhvaryu does not become gray-haired.
Those gods indeed did not find on whom they could wipe off the cruel part of the sacrifice.
Agni said:
"I will produce for you the one on whom you will wipe off the cruel part of the sacrifice."
He cast a live coal onto the waters;
from that Ekata was born;
a second [coal];
from that Dvita;
a third [coal];
from that Trita.
In that he fashioned [them] from the waters, that is the water-nature of the Āpeyas;
in that he fashioned [them] from himself, that is the self-nature of the Ātmeyas.
He offers inside the altar;
thereby he secures it.
He pours clarified butter over it,
for cooking;
for the gods desire what is cooked.
Those gods went wiping off [the guilt];
they wiped it off onto one on whom the sun rises while sleeping.
One on whom the sun rises while sleeping is the sūryābhyudita;
onto one on whom the sun sets [while sleeping, is] the sūryābhinimrukta;
onto one with black teeth, the black-toothed one;
onto one with bad nails, the bad-nailed one;
onto an agredadhū, the agredadhū;
onto an elder brother passed over in marriage, the parivitta;
onto a younger brother married before his elder, the parivividāna;
onto a slayer of a hero, the hero-slayer.
The guilt does not pass beyond the slayer of an embryo.

"At the impulse of god Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, I take you," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, with the deities he takes it.
"Thousand-pointed, hundred-edged," [he says];
he whets a vajra, intending to strike at his rival.
"O earth, place of sacrifice to the gods, let me not harm the root of your plants," [he says],
for the non-harming of the plants.
"Go to the enclosure, the cow-pen," [he says];
the enclosure, the cow-pen, is the metres;
he makes the metres an enclosure, a cow-pen for him.
"Let Parjanya rain for you," [he says];
from Parjanya he wins rain.
"Bind, O god Savitṛ, with a hundred fetters in the farthest distance," [he says];
two indeed are a man's rivals: whom he hates and who hates him;
those he binds in the farthest distance.
"Whoever hates us and whom we hate, him bind here; / let him not be freed from here," [he says];
he prays for a blessing.
"Let not your drop fall to heaven," [he says];
what is her sap, that is the drop;
upon that these creatures live;
that he holds in her,
so that it does not fall.
"Let not your Araru fly up to heaven," [he says];
Araru was indeed an Asura by name;
he lay crouching against the earth;
Indra perceived him;
he struck him away: "Away [with] Araru, the non-sacrificer to the gods, from the earth!";
he wished to fly to heaven;
he repelled him from heaven: "Let not your Araru fly up to heaven."
He who knowing thus carries the stambayajus, from these worlds the sacrificer excludes his rival.
Three times he carries it;
three indeed are these worlds;
from these worlds he excludes his rival.
Silently he carries it a fourth time;
from whichever world is unexpressed, boundless, from that he excludes his rival.
This earth belonged to the Asuras;
the gods said:
"Give us [a share] of this earth."
[The Asuras said:] "Speak yourselves!"
Then the Vasus won the eastern quarter,
the Rudras the southern,
the Ādityas the western,
the Maruts the northern.
Then the gods found this [earth] from the Asuras;
then the gods excluded the Asuras from these worlds.
Then the gods prospered, the Asuras were defeated.
When thus with these deities he encompasses the vedi,
this [earth] the sacrificer appropriates from his rival;
from this he excludes his rival;
he prospers himself, his rival is defeated.
From the earth the sacrificial and the non-sacrificial departed in different directions;
the sacrificial departed towards the east and north,
the non-sacrificial towards the south and west.
He should make the vedi sloping towards the east and north;
he makes them pure and sacrificial.
Moreover, whatever cruel deed they did to her while digging out, that he made non-cruel;
he appeases it;
and also for the separation of the sacrificial and the non-sacrificial.
He extends his arms eastward;
therewith he encompasses the Āhavanīya;
westward [are] the buttocks [of the vedi];
he encompasses the Gārhapatya.
The demons drink along the root;
if he were to cut it with his fingernail, he would become bad-nailed;
he cuts it with the wooden sword;
the wooden sword is a vajra;
with the vajra he slays the demons.

Whoever, not knowing the yoking of the ladles, responds when addressed with "O Adhvaryu!", is cut off from the deities, becomes cattleless.
Now, whoever, knowing the yoking of the ladles, responds when addressed with "O Adhvaryu!", is not cut off from the deities, becomes possessor of cattle.
Heaven is the juhū,
the atmosphere is the upabhṛt,
the earth is the dhruvā,
the quarters are the butter-holder,
yonder sun is the sruva.
When he says: "Make [them] hear!", therewith he yokes the juhū;
with "Let there be hearing!" the upabhṛt;
with "Sacrifice!" the dhruvā;
with "We who sacrifice..." the butter-holder;
with the vaṣaṭ-call the sruva.
From the juhū he bastes the dhruvā; from the dhruvā, the juhū;
for yonder [heaven] gives to this [earth], this to yonder.
Continuous, uninterrupted food is given to him in this world and in yonder world;
his fame reaches the people before he arrives, who knows thus.

Prajāpati was both ghee and honey.
From where the honey was, thence he created creatures;
therefore from honey there is procreation.
Therefore they do not perform the ritual with honey,
for that is exhausted.
They perform the ritual with ghee;
unexhausted indeed is this ghee belonging to Prajāpati;
unexhausted among the gods is Prajāpati.
He puts it on the Gārhapatya fire.
The wife looks at it;
this indeed is the wife's taking hold of the sacrifice;
he causes her to take hold of the sacrifice.
Moreover, what the wife does in the sacrifice, a pairing is thereby made,
for propagation.
Unfit for sacrifice indeed, not belonging to sacrifice is that which the wife looks at;
he puts it on the Āhavanīya fire;
he purifies it indeed;
he makes it fit for sacrifice, belonging to sacrifice.
He sets it down on the track of the wooden sword,
for the warding off of the Rakṣases.
"Let god Savitṛ purify you upwards," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, he purifies it upwards.
"You are the Vaiśvānara oblation," [he says];
twelve are these utterances;
twelve months are the year;
by means of the year the sacrificer strips away the brilliance, the faculty, the virility of his rival.
On both sides it belongs to Vaiśvānara;
for on both sides it is belonging to Vaiśvānara;
devoid of brilliance, devoid of virility does his rival become;
moreover, he becomes even more sluggish.
The gods and the Asuras were in conflict;
Indra saw this opening;
thereby he overcame the Asuras.
He overcomes his rival by the sacrifice who, knowing thus, sacrifices.
"With ghee they besprinkle the other oblations; with what the ghee?" so speak the theological disputants.
With truth indeed the ghee is besprinkled.
When he says:
"With truth I besprinkle you," [he says];
sight indeed is truth;
the sacrificer gives up his sight.
Closing his eyes he speaks this yajus;
he does not give up his sight.
The ghee is the sacrifice;
the sacrifice is Viṣṇu.
"Through this Viṣṇu strode, thrice he set down his foot; / gathered [is the world] in its dust," [he says].
When, having put it on the cooking fire, he then puts it on the Gārhapatya, then on the Āhavanīya,
this indeed is that: thrice Viṣṇu strides.
"Burnt up is the Rakṣas," [he says],
for the warding off of the Rakṣases.
He wipes the ladles;
he purifies them indeed;
he makes them fit for sacrifice, belonging to sacrifice.
"Unsharpened, destroying rivals," [he says];
for these are unsharpened, destroying rivals.
He wipes the sruva;
the male of them he thus sharpens first;
then the juhū,
then the upabhṛt,
then the dhruvā.
Yonder sun is the sruva,
heaven is the juhū,
the atmosphere is the upabhṛt,
the earth is the dhruvā.
These worlds he thus wipes in regular order;
to these worlds he entrusts himself,
for non-injury.
He wipes the juhū first;
the self indeed is the juhū;
he approaches his own self;
then the upabhṛt;
offspring indeed is the upabhṛt;
he approaches offspring,
for offspring is beyond the self as it were;
then the dhruvā;
cattle indeed are the dhruvā;
he approaches cattle,
for cattle are beyond offspring as it were.
If he were to wipe them upward-turned, the seed would be unstable;
if downward-turned, the seed would be unstable;
he wipes them transversely;
transversely indeed is seed emitted;
stable does the seed become.
If he were to wipe the bowl and the handle from the tip, he would bring strife to the Rakṣases and the non-existences;
he wipes the bowl from the tip, the handle from behind;
he does not bring strife to the Rakṣases and the non-existences.
From within he wipes the juhū forwards, from outside backwards;
from outside the upabhṛt forwards, from within backwards;
having brought [the wipe] all around, [he wipes] the dhruvā;
he arranges his vital breaths in regular order.
The juhū has the sacrificer as deity; the upabhṛt has the rival as deity;
taking four times in the juhū, he takes more ghee;
taking eight times in the upabhṛt, [he takes] less;
he makes the rival an underling.

He should set down many [waters].
As many sprinkling waters as he sets down, so many waters are his in yonder world;
therefore many are to be set down.
The sacrificial essence departed from Agni;
becoming a black antelope, it entered the trees.
When he says:
"You are black, dwelling in the lair; become ghee for Agni!",
he thereby secures that very sacrificial essence.
"You are the vedi; I sprinkle you, pleasing to the barhis," [he says];
the barhis is offspring,
the vedi is the earth;
he thereby makes offspring suitable on this [earth].
When, having sprinkled from above, he sprinkles from below,
therefore from rain from above, plants are born below.
"Hold the earth with vigor!" [he says];
he placed vigor in this [earth];
therefore creatures subsist on this [earth].
"You are the crest of Viṣṇu," [he says, and] takes the prastara from the front;
thereby he makes him chief, headed.
He should take as much as he can grasp with his hand;
the sacrificer becomes free from hunger.
He should not wipe upward;
if he were to wipe away whatever is his acquired [wealth] or to be acquired; he should not wipe downward;
what would come to him, that he would thrust away.
He should not shake it out;
he would shake out offspring and cattle.
What might stand upright, that he should gather up.
Just as one heaps up [food] for one who has eaten, even so he spreads the grass-cord on the south;
therefore the undergarment-tuck is on the south.
"Spread wide, soft as wool!" [he says];
he widens it indeed.
"A good seat for the gods!" [he says];
for the gods he makes it.
Holding the prastara, he places the enclosing sticks around;
the sacrificer thereby surrounds himself,
for non-harming.
He does not place [one] in front;
rising indeed, yonder sun smote away the rakṣases;
rising indeed, yonder sun smites away the rakṣases for him.
He places two upright kindling sticks;
thereby he smites away rakṣases from above him.
He places two,
for pairing.
"You two are the dividers," [he says];
he makes them two dividers indeed.
"In the lap of Aditi I seat you," [he says];
Aditi is this [earth];
upon this very [earth] he seats it.
"You are the seat of the Vasus, Rudras, and Ādityas," [he says];
these deities indeed conquered this [seat] in the beginning;
for them he makes this seat.
"You are heaven by birth, juhū by name, dear to the gods; sit in the firm seat with your dear name," [he says];
this indeed is their dear name, that it is ghee-laden;
that very name he secures.
"Protect them, O Viṣṇu;
protect the sacrifice;
protect the lord of the sacrifice;
protect me, the leader of the sacrifice!" [he says];
just as one says: "This is your share, protect it!", so indeed he says to Viṣṇu: "This is your ghee, protect it!"
The vedi departed from the gods;
the gods found it with the veda;
that is the veda-nature of the veda.
The vedi is female,
the veda is male;
when he cleanses the vedi with the veda,
[it is] for pairing.
Spreading continuously from the Āhavanīya fire he goes,
for the continuity of the sacrifice.
He sacrifices continuously on it in the next fortnight.
The poker is the attendant of the trees;
prosperous become the settlers of him who knows thus.
He throws it down from the east towards the west;
therefore settlers are settled toward the west.
He throws it down from the thicker end;
his settlers do not speak insolently to him.
Indeed, it is performed in the sacrifice for everyone.
The poker is a serpent,
the rubbish-heap is an anthill;
when he throws down the poker on the rubbish-heap,
therefore the anthill is the home of serpents.

"You are being," [he says];
he prays for welfare.
"Spread wide!" [he says];
he widens it indeed.
"Standing from here, Indra performed heroic deed, taking hold together with the deities," [he says];
for standing from here Indra performed heroic deed, taking hold together with the deities.
"Come, O juhū!
Agni calls you;
let us two sacrifice to the gods for divine worship.
Come, O upabhṛt!
God Savitṛ calls you;
let us two sacrifice to the gods for divine worship," [he says].
These two, indeed, are not easy to take by one who does not know;
for he steps upon the bonds of Varuṇa.
He takes them up with these deities,
so as not to step upon the bonds of Varuṇa.
"You are the step of Viṣṇu," [saying which] he strides across,
so as not to step down upon the sacrifice.
"Upward is the rite, touching heaven, uncrooked is the sacrifice of the sacrificer," [saying which] he pours the libation straight and upward,
so that the sacrifice does not stumble.
"Accompanied by Indra, rich in property," [he says];
he makes the sacrifice possess Indra;
moreover, by that he secures sensory power.
"May I stand in your shadow, rich in Vasus," [he says];
Agni indeed is rich in Vasus;
he stands in the shadow of Agni.
He should pour the libation straight and upward;
the libation is breath;
the sacrificer attains full life.
Against one whom he hates, having poured his libation, he should cut it short;
he cuts off his breath;
he dies suddenly.
The head of the sacrifice, indeed, is the libation;
the body is ghee.
If, having poured the libation, he were to anoint it with the upabhṛt, he would cut off the head of the sacrifice.
He anoints it with the butter-holder [saying]: "Anointed with Agni is the oblation, anointed with the oblation is the ghee."
He set the head back upon the body of the sacrifice;
moreover, he sets mind in the sacrifice.
Creatures possessing udders live upon the udder;
"Upon what do those without udders [live]?"—the theological disputants say.
"Licking with water-drops, may the branches extend," [he says];
upon this very thing do they live.
"Becoming a dappled barren cow, go to the Maruts," [he says];
the Maruts indeed rule over rain;
they bring down rain for him.
"Thence aid us with rain," [he says];
he wins rain from the Maruts.
"You are the eye-protector, O Agni;
protect my eye," [he says];
he protects his eye indeed.
"You are the ear-protector, O Agni;
protect my ear," [he says];
he protects his ear indeed.
"You are the body-protector, O Agni;
protect my body," [he says];
he protects his body indeed.
"Whatever of my body is deficient, O Agni, fill that up for me," [he says];
he prays for a blessing.
"Which enclosing-stick you put around yourself, O divine Agni, being pursued by the Paṇis,
that one I bring to you according to your pleasure, lest this one depart from you," [he says];
by a yajus indeed they are yoked, by a yajus they are released.
"You who partake of the overflow are these great ones," [saying which], having thrown in the enclosing-sticks, he offers upon [them];
these are Agnis indeed; he pleases them;
he appeases them with their share.
The theological disputants say:
"That one indeed would be an Adhvaryu
who, from where he yokes the sacrifice, there establishes it."
"O gods finding the way, having found the way, go on the way!
O Lord of Mind, place this sacrifice well in heaven, among the gods, in the wind, svāhā!" [he says];
from here indeed the Adhvaryu yokes the sacrifice;
there itself he establishes it.

Now this triple combination.
As for the former triple combination,
by it he supports these worlds upright from here for him.
As for the latter triple combination,
by it he supports these worlds downward from there for him.
That surely is for holding apart these worlds.
As for the former triple combination, that is the generation of heroes.
As for the latter, that is the generation of cattle.
As for the former triple combination,
heroes indeed he generates for him by it.
As for the latter,
cattle by it.
As for the former triple combination,
seed indeed he places by it.
As for the latter,
he brings forth by it.
With the former triple combination one desirous of sacrifice should sacrifice.
Agni indeed is all deities;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
he has taken hold of both the deities and the sacrifice.
Belonging to Indra indeed is the sacrifice;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
that is the undertaking of the sacrifice.
Now, since there is one for Viṣṇu,
Viṣṇu indeed is the sacrifice;
he is established in the sacrifice.
With the latter triple combination one desirous of cattle should sacrifice.
Soma indeed is the placer of seed;
Pūṣan is the progenitor of cattle.
Soma indeed places seed for him;
Pūṣan brings forth cattle.
By virile power indeed cattle are brought forth;
Pūṣan brings [them] forth;
by virile power alone Pūṣan brings forth cattle for him.
Now, since there is one for Pūṣan,
cattle indeed are Pūṣan;
he is established in cattle.
Now this [offering] to Vaiśvānara and Varuṇa.
Agni Vaiśvānara indeed is the year;
his own indeed are these seasons;
of them he is the consecrated king.
Varuṇa indeed is the consecrated among the gods.
These two indeed are the stirrers among the gods;
these two are the lords of consecration;
these two rule the consecration;
they two consecrate him.
As many as are his own, they indeed follow him;
the gods follow Varuṇa;
as many as are his own, them he makes compliant to him, not disputing.
The pap is this big;
so big indeed is the body;
as big as his body is, having released it from Varuṇa,
Agni Vaiśvānara indeed is the year,
he establishes him in the year;
he makes him possess the lifespan of a year.
Therefore the teeth of one consecrated by the Rājasūya fall out from old age;
for he establishes him in the year,
he makes him possess the lifespan of a year.
One who is chronically sick, him he should make sacrifice with this.
By Varuṇa, by evil, indeed he is seized;
therefore he is chronically sick.
Since there is [an oblation] to Varuṇa,
from Varuṇa indeed he frees him by that.
The pap is this big;
so big indeed is the body;
as big as his body is, having released it from Varuṇa,
Agni Vaiśvānara indeed is the year,
he establishes him in the year;
he makes him possess the lifespan of a year.

"O divine waters, rich in honey, mingle, winning great ruling power for the kṣatriya," [he says];
for these are the winners of ruling power.
"Sit down, unassailed, full of sap, bestowing great splendor on the kṣatriya," [he says];
for these are the sustainers of ruling power.
"You are uncrushed," he says;
he has indeed made the realm uncrushed.
"Born of ascetic heat," he says;
for the realm is born of ascetic heat.
"The gift of Soma," he says;
for this is the gift of Soma.
"Pure ones, I purify you with the pure; gleaming ones, I purify you with the gleaming," he says;
life indeed is gold;
he has made them prolongers of life;
splendor indeed is gold;
he has made them bestowers of splendor.
"May the god Savitṛ purify you with an unslit strainer," he says;
this indeed is the unslit strainer, namely the rays of the sun;
with an unslit strainer indeed he purifies them.
"Svāhā, belonging to the royal consecration!" he says;
for these belong to the royal consecration.
"Feasting together, splendid, together with invigorating drink," he divides [the water] with a [verse] to Varuṇa;
for the waters belong to Varuṇa,
by his own deity.
"O Rudra, whatever name of yours extends beyond the mountains," he says;
thereby he appeases Rudra away from him.
"In that it is offered;
it is sacrificed to Yama, svāhā!" he says;
Yama indeed is death;
thereby he appeases death away from him.
"May Soma, Indra, Varuṇa, Mitra, Agni—these gods who uphold cosmic order—uphold cosmic order," he says;
these gods who uphold cosmic order are these breathings;
if they were to consecrate him without announcing him to them, they would be excluders of him;
now, in that they consecrate him having announced him to them, thus they do not exclude him.
At the tip, at the edge of the leaf [of the parṇa tree], they conversed about the brahman;
that indeed was Suśravas, who heard it;
with a vessel of parṇa wood he consecrates [him];
the Brahman-priest consecrates [him];
with brahman-splendor indeed he consecrates him;
moreover, the Brahman-priest is the brahman;
by the brahman indeed the Brahman-priest consecrates him.
With a vessel of aśvattha wood a vaiśya [consecrates him];
the heroic power of the people went away;
it entered the aśvattha tree;
it trembled with that heroic power;
thereby indeed he obtains the heroic power of the people.
With a vessel of udumbara wood a rival [consecrates him];
udumbara trees are invigorating drink;
the sacrificer thereby deprives the rival of invigorating drink, of food.
He who is a stranger, an ally, [consecrates] with a vessel of nyagrodha wood;
by an ally indeed ruling power is spread out;
the nyagrodha tree has downward roots;
by an ally indeed he spreads out ruling power,
for firmness, for not becoming loose.

"You are the womb of ruling power, / you are the amnion of ruling power, / you are the navel of ruling power," [he says].
When Indra was born, that was indeed his womb which is the tārpya garment;
the amnion was the white garment;
the navel was the turban.
He was born from a barren cow that had calved.
The empty udder had four teats;
the first-milk was nīvāra rice.
Therefore the sacrificial cake is made of nīvāra rice.
He anoints fifty [pebbles] on one axle, fifty-one on the other.
A man has a lifespan of a hundred [years], a hundredfold heroic powers.
He obtains lifespan and heroic power.
He touches water.
Water indeed is immortality.
By immortality he causes him to take hold.
"Announced is Agni the house-lord," [he says];
he announces him to the Gārhapatya fire.
"Announced is Indra of exalted fame," [he says];
Indra is virile power;
he announces him to virile power.
"Announced are Mitra and Varuṇa of steadfast vows," [he says];
Mitra and Varuṇa are day and night;
he announces him to day and night.
"Announced are Heaven and Earth, increasers of order," [he says];
he announces him to Heaven and Earth.
"Announced is Pūṣan the all-possessing," [he says];
Pūṣan is cattle;
he announces him to cattle.
"Announced is the goddess Aditi," [he says];
these creatures are the Ādityas;
he announces him to them.
"Announced is this one, so-and-so, son of so-and-so, son of such-and-such mother, in such-and-such community," [he says];
he announces him to the community.
Therefore he is dear to the community;
for he announces him to the community.
"This is your king, O people," [he says];
he announces him to the people.
"Soma is the king of us Brahmins," [he says, and] sets aside the priesthood.
He announces him to all other beings,
[and] sets aside the priesthood.
Therefore they say:
"The priesthood is set aside;"
for he sets aside the priesthood.
"You are Indra's thunderbolt, slayer of Vṛtra; / with you may this one slay Vṛtra," [he says, and] gives the bow.
The bow is a thunderbolt;
the rājanya is a thunderbolt;
with the thunderbolt he makes the thunderbolt prosper.
"You are crushers of enemies," [he says, giving] quivers.
The quivers are the end;
the rājanya is the end;
with the end he makes the end prosper.
He gives three [arrows].
Three indeed are the arrows: celestial, earthly, and of the sea;
may he keep them away from him.
"Protect to the front;
protect to the rear;
protect across;
protect along;
protect upward."
"Protect him from the quarters!" [he says];
they protect him from the quarters.
"You are Mitra," [he says,] addressing this one;
for this is Mitra's form.
"You are Varuṇa," [he says,] addressing that one;
for that is Varuṇa's form.
With "Golden-hued, at the break of dawns," an [invocation] containing [the word] "up", he should address him.
He sprinkles him while standing with arms raised;
the chant is the Bṛhat [Sāman];
he connects him with the heavenly world.

He causes him to step out toward the quarters.
With the thunderbolt he conquers these quarters.
Therefore all the quarters are conquered by him toward whichever one he turns around.
The quarters are the heavenly world;
these quarters belong to these deities;
according to the deities he goes from these quarters to the heavenly world.
"You are Soma's brightness; possessor of brightness, / may my brightness become like yours," [he says, and] unrolls the tiger skin.
One brightness is in Soma,
one in the tiger,
one in the serpent;
he wins them.
"The head of Namuci is thrown down," [he says, and] throws lead at a eunuch.
Namuci's head is evil;
he throws evil away from him, ascending to the heavenly world.
"The biting serpents are driven away," [he says, giving] copper to a barber;
thereby he averts the biting serpents from him.
Therefore he has no sorcery against him;
for however many deaths there are, he averts them from him.
"Protect from death," [he says, and] pushes under a silver plate from below.
The tiger is the form of death;
gold is immortality;
with immortality he hides death away.
"Protect from the sky-fire," [he says, and] places a golden plate on top;
he keeps him away from the divine lightning bolt.
This [earth] is the silver one;
that [heaven] is the golden one;
enclosing him with these two,
gold is lifespan;
[waters] imparting lifespan flow over him;
gold is splendor;
[waters] imparting splendor flow over him.
When Varuṇa was being sprinkled, the waters knocked out his virile power and heroic power.
In that he puts the plate in between,
[it is] for the non-destruction of virile power and heroic power.
It weighs a hundred mānas, has a hundred syllables.
A man has a lifespan of a hundred [years], a hundredfold heroic powers;
he obtains lifespan and heroic power.

Now these Pārtha [oblations]:
the Pārtha [oblations] are the year;
he enters into the year from the middle;
therefore this one is hard to push away,
for he enters into the year from the middle.
These two are the mountains of the rājasūya:
the [libation] to Bṛhaspati of some is the last, that to Indra of some is the first.
Bṛhaspati is heroic power;
Indra is heroic power;
he unites him with heroic power.
These are the names of Āditya;
these creatures belong to Āditya;
for them indeed he is consecrated.
"I consecrate you with the brightness of Soma, with the fiery energy of Agni, with the virile power of Indra, with the mental power of all the gods," [he says];
with these virile powers, with these deities he consecrates him.
"You are the womb of Indra; / beget!" [he says];
from here indeed Indra was born;
from his own womb he begets him.
"Protect beyond the days," he says,
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
"They have turned around downwards, upwards," [he says and] wipes up;
when he is consecrated, virile power, heroic power goes away from him;
that very thing he wipes up;
with it he prospers him.
With a Triṣṭubh verse addressed to Indra the Brahman priest follows.
The nobleman belongs to Indra and has the Triṣṭubh meter;
with his own meter he follows him.
Moreover, the Brahman priest is the Prajāpati of the sacrifice;
Indra was consecrated from Prajāpati;
from Prajāpati he is consecrated.
"You are Indra's thunderbolt, winning booty; / through you may this one win booty," [he says and] brings down the chariot,
with his own deity.
"I yoke you at the impulse of Mitra and Varuṇa, the two directors," [he says and] yokes [it],
with his own deity.
"You are the stride of Viṣṇu, slayer of the rival," [he says and] mounts the chariot.
From here indeed Viṣṇu conquered these worlds;
following the conquest of Viṣṇu he conquers these worlds;
he thrusts the rival forth from these worlds.
"Conquer at the impulse of the Maruts," [he says];
having become a host of the Maruts, he conquers;
he defeats the nobleman.
He has stepped to the end;
then no one remains undefeated for him.
At him he shoots that arrow;
then his shooting was not in vain.
Having said, "Mind is obtained together with virile power," he turns around;
having gone to the end, he turns around,
for the non-injury of himself.
"This is the thunderbolt, best winner of booty; with it may our son win booty," [he says and] hands the bow and the end of the bow to his wife;
this indeed is the wife's taking hold of the sacrifice;
they are together in the heavenly world.

"So much you are; / lifespan you are; / bestow lifespan on me," [he says];
he bestows lifespan on him.
"You are joined; / splendor you are; / bestow splendor on me," [he says];
he bestows splendor on him.
"Sap you are; / bestow sap in me," [he says].
This [earth] is the silver [shoe];
that [sky] is the golden [shoe];
these two having touched,
for the udumbara is sap,
he places sap in the middle of himself and of creatures.
"You are Mitra," [he says and] takes down this one;
for this is the form of Mitra.
"You are Varuṇa," [he says and takes down] this one;
for this is the form of Varuṇa.
"You are the existent; / may I [be united] with all the gods," [he says and] brings his hands down toward the curd-mixture.
The curd-mixture is the existent;
he brings them down toward the existent.
There he gives those two pieces of gold;
that is the sacrificial fee.
He indeed injures animals when descending upon the earth;
"Homage to mother earth," [he says and] descends onto the boar-skin shoes;
he descends upon wild animals;
only the boar is able to endure this;
so the boar endures him.
"That royal power has been established as name; Varuṇa has consecrated his own body," [he says];
this is the Varuṇa-consecration;
he announces him to Varuṇa.
"Trita has brought old age down upon us," [he says];
he unites old age with him.
"Pleasant you are, comfortable to sit upon," [he says];
for it is pleasant, comfortable to sit upon.
"Sit on the pleasant, / sit on the comfortable to sit upon," [he says];
he seats him on what is comfortable to sit upon.
"Varuṇa of steadfast vow has sat down among the dwellings, / the very skillful, for universal rule," [he says];
this is the Varuṇa-consecration;
he has made him Varuṇa.
"To Agni svāhā! / To Soma svāhā! / To Indra's vigor svāhā! / To the strength of the Maruts svāhā!" [he says and] offers the chariot-unyoking [offering].
With a yajus they are yoked, with a yajus they are unyoked.
"The swan dwelling in the clear, the Vasu dwelling in the atmosphere," [he says];
he praises the chariot.
Together with the charioteer they place the chariot in the chariot-stand,
for totality, for impulse.
"O Brahman, you are the Brahman," [he says];
"What have I become? What have I become?" is what he says.
"You are Savitṛ, of true impulse," [he says];
"You are this, you are this," is what he says to him, whatever he becomes.
"This is the thunderbolt; with it subdue for me," [he says and] hands over the wooden sword.
The wooden sword is the thunderbolt;
with the thunderbolt he subdues the realm.
He hands it over from one to the next;
with it he goes, subduing the realm from one to the next;
therefore these, subdued by him from one to the next,
bring tribute.
With that wooden sword they make the gaming ground;
there they gamble for a four-year-old cow.
He consecrates toward hope;
the four-year-old cow is hope;
he has made hope for him.
Then, having drawn out a hundred and four dice, he says:
"Won for the king!"
There are four men:
the brāhmaṇa, the nobleman, the vaiśya, the śūdra;
from among them he makes him win out.
Then, handing over five dice, he says:
"This one has overcome the quarters."
These five quarters he hands over to him for food.
He gives field;
thereby he is supported in the field.
He chooses a wish;
that wish of his is fulfilled, whatever he desires.
He calls [him] by auspicious names;
"Lest what I uttered previously be in vain," [he thinks].
"So-and-so, son of so-and-so; of so-and-so, so-and-so is the son," [he says and] intertwines the two names,
for the attainment of the heavenly world.

This sacrifice is the same for a year.
The Daśapeya is cut off when they go down to the final bath.
When they come making offerings with: "To the son of waters, svāhā! To the son of strength, svāhā! To Agni, lord of the house, svāhā!",
[it is] for the continuity of the sacrifice, for non-interruption.
The sacrifice vanished from the gods;
the gods sought it in the Saṃsṛps.
When there is one for Savitṛ,
impelled by Savitṛ they sought [it].
When there is one for Sarasvatī,
Sarasvatī is speech;
in speech they sought [it].
When there is one for Pūṣan,
Pūṣan is cattle;
in cattle they sought [it].
When there is one for Bṛhaspati,
Bṛhaspati is the sacred word;
in the sacred word they sought [it].
When there is one for Indra,
Indra is virile power;
in virile power they sought [it].
When there is one for Varuṇa,
in Varuṇa they sought [it].
When there is one for Tvaṣṭṛ,
Tvaṣṭṛ is the year;
in the year they sought [it].
When there is one for Agni,
Agni is all the deities;
in the deities they sought [it].
When there is one for Soma,
plants belong to Soma;
in plants they sought [it].
When there is one for Viṣṇu,
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
in the sacrifice they sought [it].
They found it in Viṣṇu, the tenth;
therefore this is the tenth;
for they found it in the tenth.
There are ten cups;
there are ten cup-Adhvaryus;
he counts out ten cups on both sides up to the tenth generation of men,
for success;
for this sacrifice is ten-fold successful.
They sought that sacrifice then;
they prospered.
Therefore he who sacrifices with this prospers.
Everything becomes seventeenfold;
Prajāpati is seventeenfold;
he reaches Prajāpati.
This is a discontinued sacrifice;
a discontinued sacrifice is obtained from the year;
having reached it from the year, he obtains it.
Indra killed Vṛtra;
these forms approached him:
this [earth] with bright forms, that [sky] with stars.
In the reflection of the stars, a lotus grows;
this is the form of royal power;
he puts on the form of royal power.
There are twelve lotuses;
twelve months are the year;
having reached the year, he obtains it.

A gold ornament is for the Hotṛ;
the Hotṛ belongs to Agni;
[the dawn] does not shine out for him;
he causes it to shine out for him.
A garland is for the Udgātṛ;
the Udgātṛ belongs to Sūrya;
moreover, having reached yonder sun, he leads it up for him.
Dice are for the Adhvaryu;
for the Adhvaryu is like the twin.
A horse is for the Prastotṛ;
the horse belongs to Prajāpati;
the Prastotṛ belongs to Prajāpati;
and also because he snorts forward, as it were;
the Prastotṛ is forward, as it were.
A milch cow is for the Pratihartṛ;
for she takes toward, as it were;
the Pratihartṛ is toward, as it were.
A barren cow is for the Maitrāvaruṇa,
[with the thought]: "May he bring me under control."
A bull is for the Brāhmaṇācchaṃsin,
for possession of virile power.
A garment is for the Potṛ,
for purity.
A draft-ox is for the Neṣṭṛ, another for the Agnīdh,
just for yoking.
A single-horse load of barley is for the Acchāvāka;
for this office of the Acchāvāka is like a one-horse cart leading to heaven;
and the barley is for being free from Varuṇa.
At that time gifts are not given to the Sadasyas for this [sacrifice];
by that they are sought and pleased for him.
Twelve four-year-old pregnant cows are for the Brahman;
speech is a milch cow;
the mantra is an embryo;
he places the mantra in his speech;
he becomes one who is to be addressed.
They are about to become milch cows;
there are twelve milks;
he places them in him.
Therefore they say of him:
"A rich king is auspicious,"
for as much milk as there is, that is placed in him.

The Śrāyantīya is the Brahman's sāman.
They transpose the Yajñāyajñīya into anuṣṭubh verses.
The Vāravantīya is the Agniṣṭoma-sāman.
Indra slew Vṛtra;
he was deprived of vigor and heroic power.
He saw this sāman;
by it he supported himself.
He who is consecrated by the Rājasūya is deprived of vigor and heroic power;
so by this very [sāman] he supports himself.
When the sacred word (brahman) differentiated itself in the beginning as ṛc, sāman, [and] yajus,
the essence of it is the Yajñāyajñīya.
When the Yajñāyajñīya is sung,
he places essence right into the sacred word.
He who is consecrated by the Rājasūya is deprived of speech;
speech is the anuṣṭubh;
when the Yajñāyajñīya is in anuṣṭubh verses,
he places essence right into his speech.
He who is consecrated by the Rājasūya is deprived of faculty and heroic power;
the Vāravantīya is faculty and heroic power;
when the Vāravantīya is the Agniṣṭoma-sāman,
[it is] for the securing of faculty and heroic power.
They leaned on [it] with the Śrāyantīya;
they warded off with the Vāravantīya;
that is for the encompassing of his faculty and heroic power.
When he makes him step out in those directions there, then he ascends to the heavenly world.
If he were not to descend again to this world, he would either go beyond the people or run wild.
When there are these Aveṣṭis of the quarters,
he descends again to this world.
One desiring food should sacrifice.
These quarters have these deities;
according to the deity, from these quarters he secures proper food.
If a Brahmin should sacrifice, having placed [an offering] to Bṛhaspati in the center and having made offering after offering, he should baste it.
If a Vaiśya, [an offering] to the Viśve Devas.
If a Rājanya, [an offering] to Indra.
He establishes proper food in him in a single manner.
This sacrifice is the same for a year.
The sacrifice is cut off when it is concluded.
When he undertakes these Prayuj offerings, some in the evening, some in the morning—
two seasons are day and night;
those two he joins;
six are the others, six the others;
six are the seasons;
he joins the seasons;
both together are twelve;
twelve months are the year;
he joins the year.
Two draft-oxen are the sacrificial gift,
for the sake of yoking.
[There is an offering of] a dappled four-year-old cow, pregnant, for the Maruts;
the Maruts are the common people;
he makes him an embryo of the common people;
therefore he is dear to the common people,
for an embryo is dear.
[There is an offering of] a barren (?) pregnant cow for Aditi;
Aditi is this [earth];
he makes him an embryo of this [earth];
therefore he is hard to drive away,
for he is the embryo of this [earth].
Then these are the true messengers:
for impulsion, the one to Savitṛ.
The gods, going to victory over the Asuras, deposited truth in the two Aśvins and Pūṣan.
Sarasvatī is speech;
by speech he sends forth truth.
'I have been consecrated, I have become king,' [he says];
they place trust in him who has pressed [Soma].
For a year he offers the Agnihotra,
for continuity.

At the beginning he brings forward the Agniṣṭoma, the Jyotiṣṭoma.
The Agniṣṭoma is the mouth of the sacrifice;
having grasped the mouth of the sacrifice, he steps into the consecration.
Then this Triṣṭoma:
its morning pressing is threefold (trivṛt), its midday pressing fifteenfold (pañcadaśa);
the threefold is brilliance,
the fifteenfold is lordship;
he places brilliance in his lordship.
Not even the seventeenfold is extended here;
Prajāpati is the seventeenfold;
the sacrifice is Prajāpati;
since the sacrifice is extended,
thereby the seventeenfold is not excluded.
The midday pressing is fifteenfold,
the third pressing twenty-one-fold;
the twenty-one-fold is the common people,
the fifteenfold is lordship;
he establishes his lordship firmly in the common people.
All the Pavamāna [chants] of the Abhiṣecanīya are thirty-four-fold;
thirty-three are the deities;
they are reached and gratified by this for him.
Then this thirty-fourth [stoma]:
Prajāpati is the thirty-fourth;
he reaches Prajāpati.
This is a destruction of the stomas out of order when the stomas are unequal;
since the Pavamānas are equal,
thereby there is no destruction, thereby [they are] in order.
By the Agniṣṭoma he thrives through himself, he becomes meritorious through himself;
offspring are the ukthas,
cattle are the ukthas;
since there is the Ukthya,
[it is] for continuity.
Whoever departs from the consecration, the consecration does not fall to his lot;
whoever departs from the sāmans becomes worse off after pressing Soma;
since he does not depart from the sāmans of the divine kings,
thereby he does not depart from the consecrations.
These sāmans are the pṛṣṭhas;
since they undertake the pṛṣṭhas,
thereby he does not depart from the sāmans;
by the sāmans of the divine kings he thrives in this world, by those of human kings in yonder [world];
so by these he thrives in both worlds, in this one and in yonder.
They chant on verses containing [the word] 'ud';
what contains 'ud' is the form of the anuṣṭubh;
the Rājanya belongs to the anuṣṭubh;
therefore they chant on verses containing 'ud'.
The first is an anuṣṭubh, the last is an anuṣṭubh;
the anuṣṭubh is speech;
by speech they set forth, by speech they conclude.
The last anuṣṭubh belongs to Sūrya,
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
The Bahiṣpavamāna of the Keśavapanīya is twenty-one-fold; the last [stoma] of the Abhiṣecanīya is twenty-one-fold;
the twenty-one-fold is the common people,
the seventeenfold is lordship;
he is consecrated from the midst of the common people;
therefore he is dear to the common people,
for he is consecrated from the midst of the common people.
When he makes him step out in those directions there, then he ascends to the heavenly world.
If he were not to descend again to this world, he would either go beyond the people or run wild.
Because this stoma is directed backward,
he descends again to this world.

Vṛtra lay encompassing the waters.
Those which escaped beyond were the living, the sacrificial ones.
Vṛtra is a mountain; those which run having escaped are the living, the sacrificial ones.
When he draws from flowing [waters],
he draws from those very ones that are living and sacrificial.
He brings [the vessel] near against the current,
so that there be no failure.
He draws in the sun’s view;
he draws them with Indra indeed.
'These waters are rich in oblation,' [he says];
he draws them as rich in oblation.
They are to be drawn when the sun is visible;
he takes hold of the higher sacrificial rite.
He draws with an anuṣṭubh;
the anuṣṭubh is all metres;
with all metres he draws them.
This is the summit of the metres;
he causes him to reach the summit of his equals.
With four [verses] he draws, with three he sets down;
that makes seven.
The śakvarī has seven feet;
cattle belong to the Śakvarī;
he secures cattle.
Night enters into the waters when day has come;
therefore waters are dark by day.
Day [enters into] the waters at night;
therefore waters are bright at night.
When he draws by day,
he draws both day and night.
By day men perform the sacrifice, at night the gods;
therefore he seeks fame by nights.
Having concluded the sacrifice, men lie down.
The waters are indeed All-gods.
When they carry around the waters,
he delivers the sacrifice over to the waters.
[The sacrifice] is made continuous for his sacrifice.
What they omit in the sacrifice is a hole;
along that hole the rakṣases weave into the sacrifice.
The waters are slayers of rakṣases;
the rakṣases do not cross the waters.
When they carry around the waters,
[it is] so that the rakṣases do not descend upon [the sacrifice].
Those which he draws by day, they are the womb of those creatures that are eaters;
they are born along after them.
Those which he draws at night, they are the womb of those creatures that are to be eaten;
they are born along after them.
He generates both kinds of creatures: eaters and those to be eaten.
He sets [them] down in the Āgnīdhra [fire-shed];
for this is unconquered;
and also because the deities wait fasting here for that night.
The gods did not see the morrow of the sacrifice;
they saw this very morrow of the sacrifice, namely the Vasatīvarī [waters].
When they wait fasting having drawn the Vasatīvarī waters,
having seized the morrow of the sacrifice, they wait fasting.
And also, the waters are the All-gods;
having seized all deities, they wait fasting.
If the sun should set upon him when they are not drawn, they should be drawn from the jar of one who has established his fires and is a soma-sacrificer;
for they are drawn for him. They should hold fire above [the jar];
then he should draw;
he draws them as full of light.
Gold is in his hand;
then he draws;
gold is truth;
with truth he draws them.
A boon is the sacrificial gift;
with a boon he draws them.
Whatever wealth he might intend to give there, that should be given.

He draws first with the pracaraṇī [ladle];
for this goes forward first.
The sap of Soma entered the waters;
Agni is all the deities;
the waters belong to the Viśvedevas;
the pressing-stones are the mouth of the sacrifice.
Impelled by Savitṛ, he goes after the sap of Soma [that is] of the waters with all the deities.
'Let Agni hear my call with the kindling stick,' he says;
he speaks to that very fire in which he offers.
'Let the waters hear,' he says;
he speaks to those very waters that he goes after.
'And the divine Dhiṣaṇā,' [he says];
speech is the divine Dhiṣaṇā;
he speaks to speech.
'Hear, O wise pressing-stones, the sacrifice now,' he says;
for the pressing-stones are the mouth of the sacrifice.
'Let the god Savitṛ hear my call,' he says,
for impulsion.
'Send the waters, O Hotṛ,' he says;
'impel the sacrifice,' is what he says by that.
'Forward with the sacred word (brahman),' he says;
with the sacred word he goes after the sacrifice.
He goes after [it] with the cup of the Maitrāvaruṇa;
for this goes forward first.
Moreover, Mitra and Varuṇa rule over the waters;
Mitra and Varuṇa are the mouth of the sacrifice;
the waters are the mouth of the sacrifice;
by the mouth of the sacrifice he goes after the mouth of the sacrifice.
Where the Adhvaryu would hear the Hotṛ reciting the morning recitation (prātaranuvāka), there he should draw the waters.
If he should not hear, he would become deaf,
for he is cut off from speech.
Then that he offers,
he draws them having become an oblation;
and also they are anointed with ghee.
Throwing in a blade of grass, he offers;
he offers right on what has fire, on what has an abode.
The Adhvaryu would become blind if he should offer on what has no abode.
Having made ghee a vajra, the gods slew Soma.
Indeed they besprinkle these [waters] with ghee;
therefore he washes away the oblation,
and also saying, 'Lest I burn the oblation.'
'Thou art furrow-born,' [saying this] he washes away the oblation.
'I draw you up for the non-exhaustion of the ocean,' he brings [it] near;
for the non-exhaustion of the waters.
The sap of Soma entered the waters;
the sap of the waters [entered] Soma.
Apāṃ Napāt rules over the sap of the waters [and] of Soma;
he begged the sap of the waters [and] of Soma away from both of them with an oblation as their share.
They say: 'Both day and night are to be taken in the Vasatīvarī [waters];
he draws one on the previous day, the other before the rising of the sun;
thus both day and night are drawn for him in the Vasatīvarī [waters].'
He draws one on the previous day, the other before the rising of the sun;
he has made both of them drawn on the same day.
Enemies indeed are drawn: those in the cup of the Maitrāvaruṇa and those which are the Nigrābhyā [waters];
he anoints both of them together above the pit (cātvāla);
he makes agreement for them;
he gives a friend to them.
They are drawn out from the pit;
this is the womb of the waters;
they are drawn out from their own womb,
so that there be no spilling.
'Hast thou obtained the waters, O Adhvaryu?' [one asks];
'Hast thou found the sacrifice?' is what he says by that.
'And they have bowed down,'
'and behold this one!'
'This [sacrifice] has succeeded for me,' is what he says by that.
If [the sacrifice] is an Agniṣṭoma, he offers;
they do not go beyond Agni by the stotra nor by the śastra of the Agniṣṭoma;
he connects the Agniṣṭoma sacrificial rite continuously.
If it is an Ukthya, he anoints the enclosing-stick;
the enclosing-sticks are beyond Agni;
the ukthas are beyond the enclosing-sticks;
he connects the ukthas sacrificial rite continuously.
If it is an Atirātra, uttering this very yajus, he should enter the Havirdhāna shed;
the Havirdhāna shed is beyond the enclosing-sticks;
the night is beyond the ukthas;
he connects the night sacrificial rite continuously.
The mind is a thief;
speech tells lies.
Now, with what are the somas drawn, with what are they offered? If one should ask,
gold is truth and cosmic order (ṛta);
by truth, by cosmic order they are drawn; by cosmic order they are offered.

The sacrifice is brought forward for the gods, for humans, [and] for the heavenly world.
When [he says], 'For your heart,'
thereby [it is] for the gods.
When [he says], 'For your mind,'
thereby [it is] for humans.
When he says,
'For heaven you, for the sun you,'
thereby he makes the sacrifice ascend after him to the heavenly world.
The sacrifice is indeed severed from him for whose sake it is brought forward;
therefore he makes him ascend after it to the heavenly world.
When he says,
'Extend the hotrā among the gods in heaven,'
the sacrifice is continuous through the hotrās;
by means of the hotrās he continues the sacrifice.
'Recite for the gods who go early in the morning,' [he says];
the meters are the gods who go early in the morning;
to the meters he thereby speaks the direction to recite.
Before speech speaks forth, he recites the morning litany (prātaranuvāka);
he grasps speech for the sacrificer alone.
The sacrificer does not wash his hands at that time;
when he recites as the first [verse] one containing the word 'water' (apsumatī),
that is the morning washing of the sacrificer.
Where they carry the waters around over there, he then handed over the sacrifice to the waters;
when he recites as the first [verse] one containing the word 'water',
he thereby takes hold of the sacrifice again from the waters.
The morning litany belongs to all the gods;
the waters are indeed all the gods;
when he recites as the first [verse] one containing the word 'water',
he thereby obtains all the deities, he completely reaches all.
Just as the sāmidhenī verses, so is the morning litany;
having placed the oblation, they recite the sāmidhenī verses;
the oblation at that time is Soma;
therefore having brought down Soma, he recites.
He brings about both the Gāyatrī and the Jagatī;
those are two meters;
he brings them about into one meter, the Bṛhatī.
And the Triṣṭubh and the Kakubh;
those are two meters;
he brings them about into one meter, the Bṛhatī.
And the Anuṣṭubh and the Paṅkti;
those are six meters;
he brings them about into one meter, the Bṛhatī.
He should make the meters have an Anuṣṭubh opening for one whose sacrifice has been disrupted;
the Anuṣṭubh is speech;
with speech he heals him.
He should recite the Triṣṭubh and the Jagatī evenly for one desiring cattle;
the Jagatī is cattle;
he becomes possessed of cattle.
He recites all the meters;
the meters are Prajāpati;
he obtains Prajāpati.
The morning litany is this hymn (uktha) of Prajāpati;
he should recite at dawn before the rising of the sun;
for this is the world of Prajāpati.
The morning litany is a means of purification;
by this he purifies the sacrificer.
He should recite one hundred [verses] for one desiring life;
man is of a hundred years of life, of a hundredfold vigor;
he obtains life and vigor.
He should recite seven hundred and twenty [verses] for one desiring cattle;
so many are the days and nights of the year;
by means of the year he wins cattle for him.
He should recite one thousand for one desiring heaven;
as far as a thousand [steps], so far reached is that world;
[it is] for the attainment of the heavenly world.
He recites an unlimited number [of verses];
[it is] for the winning of the unlimited.
He concludes with a Paṅkti;
he takes hold of the higher sacrificial rite.

These are the foot-washings of the sacrifice.
The Agnīdh belongs to the males;
the Neṣṭṛ belongs to the females.
When the Agnīdh sits upon the lap of the Neṣṭṛ,
they two indeed unite as a pair.
When at that time she rolls the waters forward,
in that very pair he places seed.
The wife rolls [them] forward;
for from the wife offspring are born.
She rolls [them] forward along her thigh;
for offspring are born along the thigh.
She rolls [them] forward along her right thigh;
for offspring are born along the right thigh.
She rolls [them] forward on the inside;
for offspring are born from the inside.
If she were to roll [them] forward southward, she would sweep them away into the world of the ancestors.
Now when she rolls them forward northward,
she brings them forth into the world of humans.
The Udgātṛ belongs to Prajāpati;
being looked at [by the wife], the Udgātṛ takes the seed of the wife.
When she says,
'Pleasing in your sight, may I deposit all seed,'
thus indeed he does not take [it].
At the twelfth stotra she rolls [them] forward;
twelve months are the year;
twelve are the stotras in the Agniṣṭoma;
waters are appeasement.
When at that time she rolls the waters forward,
he simply appeases [it].
'At the impulse of the god Savitṛ,' [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ he takes it.
'With the arms of the Aśvins,' [he says];
the Aśvins are the two Adhvaryus of the gods.
'With the hands of Pūṣan,' [he says];
[he takes it] through the deities indeed.
'You are a pressing-stone,' [he says];
for this [stone] offers Soma to the gods.
'Sacrifice-maker for the gods,' [he says];
for this [stone] makes the sacrifice for the gods.
'For Indra, you best pressed, sweet, rich in milk,' [he says];
for the sacrifice belongs to Indra.
Indeed, at this time the other deities follow Indra.
'For Indra accompanied by the Vasus, accompanied by the Rudras,' [he says];
for the Vasus and the Rudras follow him at this time.
'For Indra accompanied by the Ādityas,' [he says];
for the Ādityas follow him at this time.
'For Agni who gives increase of wealth,' [he says];
Agni is all the deities;
for these deities follow him at this time.
'For Viṣṇu, you,' [he says];
for Viṣṇu is the sacrifice.
The Upāṃśusavana [pressing-stone] is the out-breathing;
the Soma shoots are offspring;
he places the out-breathing in offspring.
'Invigorating are you, overcoming Vṛtra,' [he says];
this is the Soma-drink of the waters.
Viśvāmitra knew this Soma-drink of the waters;
to him the rivers yielded milking.
This is the Soma-drink of the waters;
he enriches the waters with the Soma-drink.
Whoever knows the Soma-drink of the waters suffers no misfortune among them; he performs valor among them.
'The light of yours, O Soma, which is in heaven,' [he says];
Soma is prize-wealth;
a third of it is the moon;
that which purifies itself here is a third;
that with which they sacrifice is a third.
By means of that one [above] he upholds the embryos; by means of this one he extends the life-breaths;
that which purifies itself here, by means of it he upholds food;
[that] with which they sacrifice—of that prize-wealth indeed, truth is the prize-winner,
food is the prize-winner,
the sacrificial gift is the prize-winner.
Having gathered these bodies of his, they swell him when he has become whole with body.
The pressing-stone is a thunderbolt;
the two pressing-boards are jaws;
something cruel, as it were, is done here in the sacrifice.
When he says,
'O praised Dhiṣaṇās, be praised,'
he simply appeases [it].

With the Vasatīvarī [waters] they swell the Soma;
for Soma is not swelled by Soma;
for Soma is swelled by waters.
With the Nigrābhyā [waters they swell] the Upāṃśu [cup];
for from this the other Somas are drawn.
The Upāṃśu is breath;
the other cups are the limbs.
If they were to swell [it] from elsewhere, they would sever the sacrifice from the breath.
He presses eight times in the morning.
The gāyatrī has eight syllables;
they thereby stretch out the gāyatrī at the morning pressing.
The gods and the Asuras contended.
The gods saw the sacrifice as one to be established in the Upāṃśu;
they established it in the Upāṃśu.
Therefore the sacrifice is to be established in the Upāṃśu.
He presses eight times in the morning;
the gāyatrī has eight syllables;
he obtains the gāyatrī.
When eleven,
thereby [he obtains] the triṣṭubh.
When twelve,
thereby [he obtains] the jagatī.
Three times he draws out;
three indeed are the pressings;
he obtains the pressings.
Thus indeed they established the sacrifice in the Upāṃśu.
With that established, uninjured, existing [sacrifice] they proceed.
Three times he draws out;
for these breaths are three: prāṇa, apāna, [and] vyāna.
Moreover, these worlds are three;
he obtains these worlds.
Yonder [world] is the Upāṃśu;
the intermediate space is the Antaryāma;
this [earth] is the Upayāma.
When [cups] held by an Upayāma are drawn,
they are drawn by this [earth].
Therefore they are unspilled;
for they are drawn by this [earth].
When they are drawn with an earthen pot,
they are drawn by this [earth];
for this [pot] is made of it.
Therefore they are unspilled;
for they are drawn by this [earth].
When they are drawn with a wooden [vessel],
they are drawn by this [earth];
for this [earth] is the womb of trees.
Therefore they are unspilled;
for they are drawn by this [earth].
'This is your womb,' he says, setting it down;
this [earth] is the womb of Soma;
from this Soma is born.
He places it in its own womb.
This [earth] is the vessel of the gods.
Therefore whoever knows thus obtains the vessel of one who is wealthier.
But he should not investigate a Brahmin at the drinking vessel who would be worthy of the vessel.
It is drawn not held by an Upayāma;
for offspring subsist on the breath not held by an Upayāma.
It is drawn unpurified by a strainer;
for offspring subsist on the breath unpurified by a strainer.
It is offered unseated;
for offspring subsist on the breath unseated.
The other Somas are purified by a strainer;
now this Upāṃśu is purified by speech.
Therefore he says, 'Purify yourself for the lord of speech!';
for it is purified by speech.
He purifies it with Soma-stalks;
the stalks are the breaths;
he purifies it with the breaths.
He purifies it with two by two;
for these breaths are two by two.
'A god, you are the strainer of the gods,' [he says],
when indeed he purifies Soma with Soma.
'You are prepared for oneself,' [he says];
he has made the breath his own.
'May the mind reach you,' [he says];
he obtains the breath with the mind.
'Svāhā to you, well-being to the sun,' [he says];
for all Somas are offered having the sun as their deity.
Moreover, here indeed are the dear bodies of the gods;
he secures them.
'To you for the gods who drink the rays,' [he says];
for the gods who drink the rays are the rays [of the sun];
the enclosing sticks are the rays;
yonder sun is the Āhavanīya [fire];
he pleases them.
Standing upright, he offers;
for standing upright he is more vigorous.
For him whom he hates, he should offer standing obliquely;
he crushes his breaths;
he becomes tottering.
If he performs sorcery, he should stand until he faints;
Agni, hastening toward the oblation, immediately follows.
If he should desire, 'May Parjanya rain,' having wiped [the cup] around, he should wipe upward;
he rains from yonder for the plants;
he brings the plants nearer to the rain.
If he should desire, 'May Parjanya rain,' having wiped around, he should wipe downward;
with Vaiśvānara he strikes down the rain.
It is not to be covered;
if he were to cover it, he would cover his breath, [and] he would be liable to die.
For him whom he hates, he should cover it;
he covers his breath.
If he performs sorcery, he should cover it [saying],
'Here I cover the breath of so-and-so, son of so-and-so';
he covers his breath.
'Here I set [this] down in the breath of so-and-so, son of so-and-so,' [so] performing sorcery he should set it down;
he sets it down in his breath;
he becomes liable to die.
'For breath, you,' he should set it down when not performing sorcery.

The expounders of the Veda say:
'What is done in the sacrifice because of which some animals take with the hand, others with the mouth?'
Because the Upāṃśu is drawn with the hand,
therefore the monkey, man, and the elephant—these take with the hand.
The Vāyavya is the mouth.
Because the other cups are drawn in the Vāyavya,
therefore the other animals take with the mouth.
The expounders of the Veda say:
'What is done in the sacrifice because of which newborn animals stand firmly on the same day, but men [only] in a year?'
The other animals follow the Upāṃśu and Antaryāma;
man, however, is commensurate with the sacrifice.
Upāṃśu and Antaryāma are in-breath and out-breath.
Because these two cups are offered without being set down,
therefore newborn animals stand firmly on the same day.
The Agniṣṭoma is the year;
twelve months are the year.
Because these other cups are set down,
therefore man when born stands firmly in a year.
When [he says], 'What uncheatable name of yours is watchful, O Soma...',
this indeed is the Soma-drink of Soma.
He thereby enriches Soma with the Soma-drink.
He who knows the Soma-drink of Soma is not severed from the Soma-drink.
Soma desired these quarters:
east, west, downward, [and] north.
He brings it together with these quarters;
he enriches it with these;
he causes it to attain desire.
He goes to his desire;
women become desirous of him.
Whomever he should desire, upon her he should then meditate in mind;
she desires him.
'O mother, remember,' [he says];
'Remember' is what he thereby says.
'May I find with the stranger,' [he says];
'Find together with this one' is what he thereby says.
There were four teats of Pṛśni;
from there she milked for the gods with three.
One was tied round with points of kuśa grass.
Indra alone saw it;
with it she milked for Indra alone.
That indeed is his Kauśika nature,
when he says, 'O Brahmin, like the Kauśikas.'
Four times he approaches the Nigrābha;
there are four milks;
whatever milk there is, that he obtains.
Three times he gathers [it];
therefore there are three shares in this [cow]: in the morning, at midday, in the evening.
Four times he approaches the Nigrābha, three times he gathers [it];
that is seven.
The śakvarī has seven feet;
animals are connected with the Śakvarī;
he secures animals.
Nine times he approaches the Nigrābha, three times he gathers [it];
that is twelve.
Twelve months are the year;
twelve are the chants in the Agniṣṭoma;
this is the measure of the sacrifice.
Svarbhānu the Asura pierced the sun with darkness;
the gods struck away its darkness.
The first darkness that they struck away, that became a black sheep;
the second, that [became] a reddish one;
the third, that [became] a white one.
It was freed in its own form.
Because the strainer is white, from it the Śukra [cup] is drawn.
This is the sacrificer's strainer;
he purifies the sacrificer with it.
It should be unspun [by others];
he made it from his own self.
What is spun elsewhere is not a strainer.
That belongs to the sacrificer.

The gods indeed held a sacrificial session at Kurukṣetra: Agni, Makha, Vāyu, Indra.
They said:
'Whichever of us shall prosper first, that shall belong to us together.'
Makha indeed among them prospered.
He desired it exclusively for himself;
he did not share it.
They wished to take it from him by force.
From here he produced three [arrows];
from here, a bow.
That is the origin of the three [arrows] and of the bow.
Having strung [the bow], he stepped back; they did not dare approach him.
He stood resting against the bow-end.
Indra said to the ants:
'Gnaw through this bowstring.'
They said:
'When she (the earth) is trampled upon, we will not be able to live.
Let there be a share for us.'
He said:
'Live off the sap of this [earth].'
Therefore they throw up moist [earth] from the dry,
for they live off her sap.
They indeed gnawed through the bowstring.
The bow-end of it, springing up, cut off his head.
He became the Samrāj.
Then the rest they divided into three parts: Agni [took] the front half, Indra the middle, Vāyu the hind half.
Therefore the morning pressing belongs to Agni, the midday pressing to Indra, the third pressing to the All-gods,
for Vāyu is the All-gods.
The Upāṃśu and Antaryāma [cups] are in-breath and out-breath;
the Upāṃśusavana is diffused breath;
the Aindravāyava is speech;
the Maitrāvaruṇa is skill and resolve;
the Āśvina is hearing;
the Śukra and Manthin are the two eyes;
the Āgrayaṇa is the self;
the Ukthyas are the limbs;
the Dhruva is life-span;
the two Ṛtupātras are the breasts;
the droṇakalaśa is the head;
the two kalasas are the flanks;
the two adhiṣavaṇa [boards] are the jaws;
the adhiṣavaṇa [skin] is the tongue;
the pressing-stones are the teeth;
the enclosing-sticks are the collarbones;
the uttaravedi is the nose;
the sacrificial post is the crest of hair;
the havirdhāna is the head;
the Āgnīdhra is the back;
the sadas is the belly;
what is within the belly, that is the dhiṣṇyas; the two hands
and the Āgnīdhra and Mārjālīya are the two heels;
the Gārhapatya is the firm standing; the altar [is the hips].
This Prajāpati indeed is embodied in the vessels;
he truly knows him, who knows thus.

Nine of these cups are drawn in the morning.
These nine vital breaths, indeed, are drawn.
The Āśvina is drawn as the tenth.
The out-breathing (vyāna), indeed, is drawn [in this];
the navel, indeed, is drawn as the tenth.
It is offered third,
for this breath is the third,
for the continuity of the vital breaths.
All these cups possess a chant (stotra), possess a recitation (śastra), and possess a primary cause (nidāna).
He comes to possess a primary cause who knows thus.
The Hotṛ, indeed, sacrificed today in the morning to the Upāṃśu;
thereby these [cups] possess Ukthas.
Moreover, what silent recitation (tūṣṇīṃśaṃsa) the Hotṛ recites, thereby indeed these [cups] possess Ukthas.
Nine of these cups are drawn in the morning; with nine [verses] they chant in the Bahiṣpavamāna;
thereby these possess sāmans.
With the hiṃkāra the Āśvina possesses a sāman;
with the Aindravāyava [chant] the Aindravāyava [possesses a sāman];
with the Maitrāvaruṇa [chant] the Maitrāvaruṇa;
with the Aindra [chant] the Śukra and Manthin;
with the Vaiśvadeva [chant] the Āgrayaṇa.
For the recitation (uktha) the Ukthya [cup] is drawn;
for the recitation the Dhruva [cup];
for continuity the Aindrāgna [cup];
for continuity the Marutvatīya [cups].
The Adhvaryu offers, the Hotṛ pronounces the vaṣaṭ-call;
thereby the two bestow the vital breaths.
With the mantra by which the Adhvaryu hands it over, with that the Hotṛ receives it,
for the guarding of the vital breaths.
Prajāpati himself, indeed, was the Hotṛ;
he became exhausted.
The gods ran to him with the [cups] for two deities.
The vital breaths, indeed, are the [cups] for two deities.
When the Adhvaryu, having offered the cups for two deities, quickly runs to the Hotṛ,
he thereby refreshes the Hotṛ with the vital breaths.
At every vaṣaṭ-call the Hotṛ reaches the end of his breath.
The vital breaths, indeed, are the [cups] for two deities.
When the Adhvaryu, having offered the cups for two deities, quickly runs to the Hotṛ,
he thereby refreshes the Hotṛ with the vital breaths.
The vital breaths, indeed, are the [cups] for two deities;
cattle are the iḍā.
If, without consuming the cups for two deities, he were to invoke the iḍā, he would cut off the vital breaths by means of cattle.
Now when, having consumed the cups for two deities, he invokes the iḍā,
having placed the vital breaths in himself, he then invokes cattle.
Power (indriya), indeed, is Soma;
cattle are power.
When he consumes the Soma,
he places power in himself.
He consumes the Āśvina turning it round from all sides;
therefore one hears in all directions.

By means of the sacrifice, indeed, the gods went to the heavenly world.
They thought:
“By this [path], indeed, others will ascend after us to the world.”
They saw the metres;
they interlaced them,
so that the heavenly world would not be discerned.
In that he interlaces the metres,
the sacrificer thereby confounds the world and the metres of the rival,
so that the heavenly world would not be discerned.
Among the gods, indeed, were some metres, among the Asuras others.
Smaller ones were among the gods, larger ones among the Asuras.
Those gods with the smaller metres wrested away the larger metres of the Asuras.
In that he recites the larger metres differently with the smaller metres,
the sacrificer thereby wrests away the world and the metres of the rival;
he deprives him of world and metres.
He recites all the metres into the Anuṣṭubh.
The Anuṣṭubh, indeed, is speech.
Man is of the nature of the Anuṣṭubh;
therefore man speaks all speech.
The metres, indeed, are cattle;
speech is the Anuṣṭubh;
man is of the nature of the Anuṣṭubh.
In that, having recited all the metres, he recites the Anuṣṭubh last, therefore man above consumes the speechless cattle.
He recites all the metres together;
Prajāpati, indeed, is the metres;
he attains Prajāpati.
Six syllables are redundant from the stotra;
six, indeed, are the seasons;
he firmly establishes himself in the seasons.
From them four return back to the śastra;
by that he secures four-footed cattle.
Then two only are left over;
two, truth and falsehood, surpass the Virāj;
he attains the Virāj.

Prajāpati, indeed, distributed the sacrifices for the gods.
He thought:
“I have excluded myself.”
He tore out their powers and virtues again;
that became the Ṣoḍaśin.
Now at that time Indra was the lowest, the slackest among the gods.
To him he gave this Ṣoḍaśin.
By means of it Indra prospered;
thence the gods prospered, the Asuras were defeated.
Therefore one who, knowing thus, draws this Ṣoḍaśin cup prospers himself, [and] his rival is defeated.
Prajāpati, indeed, distributed bodies for the gods.
The one of them that had bay horses, that one he left for himself out of love.
When the puroruc of the cup contains [the word] ‘bay horses’,
that very dear body of his that has bay horses he attains.
They say: “There is no sacrifice named Ṣoḍaśin.”
Then why [is it called] Ṣoḍaśin?
Because it is made complete by both stotra and śastra, therefore [it is called] Ṣoḍaśin.
By means of the sacrifice, indeed, the gods went to the heavenly world.
It did not suffice for them.
Onto it, indeed, they fastened this Ṣoḍaśin;
by means of it they reached yonder world.
Therefore, indeed, the Ṣoḍaśin is drawn for reaching yonder world, for the attainment of yonder world.
At an Agniṣṭoma one should draw it for a nobleman;
unappeased, the thunderbolt kindles him for prosperity.
At an Atirātra for a Brahmin;
the Ṣoḍaśin is indeed a thunderbolt, capable of injuring the sacrificer when unappeased;
the night, indeed, appeases the thunderbolt.
It should be drawn at the morning pressing;
the morning pressing is brilliance;
he constructs the thunderbolt from brilliance itself.
It should be drawn at the midday pressing;
the midday pressing is force;
he constructs the thunderbolt from force itself.
It should be drawn at the third pressing;
the third pressing is cattle;
he constructs the thunderbolt from cattle themselves.
If he were to draw it at the morning pressing, the thunderbolt would be left over beyond the two later pressings.
If at the midday pressing, the thunderbolt would strike down in the middle.
It should be drawn at the third pressing;
thus he draws it for all the pressings, nor are the two earlier pressings brought to ruin.
It should be made fifteen-versed;
the fifteen-versed is indeed a thunderbolt;
he places the thunderbolt in him;
thereby he goes to conquest [and] prosperity.
It should be made sixteen-versed;
the sacrificer is fifteen-versed, the thunderbolt is sixteen-versed;
he places the thunderbolt in him;
it kindles him for prosperity, for supremacy.
It should be made seventeen-versed;
Prajāpati is seventeen-versed;
the Ṣoḍaśin belongs to Prajāpati;
he places him in his own womb.
It should be made twenty-one-versed;
the Anuṣṭubh has twenty-one domains;
the Ṣoḍaśin is of the nature of the Anuṣṭubh;
this is its closest abode;
therefore it should be made twenty-one-versed.
On the middle day of a three-day rite it should be drawn;
the middle day of a three-day rite is slack;
[it is drawn] for the firmness of the day, for unslackness.
On the fourth day of a four-day rite it should be drawn;
the fourth day of a four-day rite is slack;
[it is drawn] for the firmness of the day, for unslackness.
On every fourth day of an Ahīna rite it should be drawn;
every fourth day of an Ahīna rite is slack;
[it is drawn] for the firmness of the day, for unslackness.
It is not suitable in a two-day rite;
these two metres are indeed the Gāyatrī and the Triṣṭubh;
he would draw it without a metre, without an abode; the sacrificer would become destitute of an abode.
On the latter day of a two-day rite it should be drawn;
it extends beyond into the night as its abode.

A black-headed [victim] belongs to Agni;
a ewe to Sarasvatī;
a brown one to Soma;
a dark one to Pūṣan;
a white-backed one to Bṛhaspati;
a reddish-brown one to the Viśve Devas;
a ram to Indra;
a spotted one to the Maruts;
a piebald one to Indra and Agni;
a white-bellied one to Savitṛ;
a gelded ram to Varuṇa.
Prajāpati, having created creatures, felt emptied.
He saw this set of eleven [victims];
with it he replenished himself.
The sacrifice, indeed, is Prajāpati.
Therefore one who, knowing thus, ties up this set of eleven [victims] replenishes whatever of himself is deficient.
Prajāpati, having created creatures, felt emptied.
He saw these powers and virtues;
he bound them together in pairs onto himself.
The body belongs to Agni;
speech to Sarasvatī;
power to Soma.
Speech and power he bound together onto his body.
Nourishment belongs to Pūṣan;
the sacred lore to Bṛhaspati.
Nourishment and the sacred lore he bound together onto himself.
Strength belongs to the Viśve Devas;
virility to Indra.
Strength and virility he bound together onto himself.
Might belongs to the Maruts;
force to Indra and Agni.
Might and force he bound together onto himself.
The one for Savitṛ is for impulsion;
the one for Varuṇa is for freedom from Varuṇa.
All are tied after the one for Agni.
Agni, indeed, is all the deities;
they are tied, indeed, after all the deities.
The one for Sarasvatī is for pairing.
Now, as to why [there is] one for Soma:
Soma, indeed, is the placer of semen;
he places semen in him.
Now, as to why [there is] one for Pūṣan:
Pūṣan, indeed, is procreation;
[it is] for procreation.
The sacred lore is Bṛhaspati;
these creatures belong to the Viśve Devas.
He pushed the sacred lore ahead of these creatures;
moreover, he makes these creatures compliant with the sacred lore.
The Maruts are the peasantry;
he thereby harnesses the peasantry to the ruling power;
moreover, he makes the peasantry compliant with the ruling power;
by might he encompasses the peasantry for the ruling power.
The one for Agni is seized;
the one for Indra is seized;
then this one for Indra and Agni is seized:
he has made the sacred lore and the ruling power yoke-fellows.
Since Agni and Indra
are the most possessing of shares among the deities,
therefore the Brahmin and the king are the most possessing of shares among men.
The one for Savitṛ is for impulsion;
the one for Varuṇa is for freedom from Varuṇa.
Varuṇa, indeed, is the ocean;
the south is the ocean.
In that the one for Varuṇa is seized on the southern half,
[it is] for the sacrificer's freedom from Varuṇa.
Concerning this, indeed, this [verse] has been recited:
“You are a true god, O Varuṇa, for whom the seven rivers
flow down into your palate like a hollow tube.”
Thus [it says].

They slay Soma when they press [him];
they slay the sacrifice.
When sacrificial gifts are given,
they invigorate the sacrifice.
That is the nature of sacrificial gifts (dakṣiṇā), that they invigorate (dakṣayanti).
The midday pressing is indeed the heavenly world.
When sacrificial gifts are given at the midday pressing,
[it is] for the ascending of the heavenly world.
Much should be given;
he makes a bridge
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
There is gold in his hand, and then he leads [them];
gold is truth;
by means of truth indeed he leads them.
In front of the Gārhapatya,
behind the Sadas,
they let them loose northward, between the pit and the Āgnīdhra hut.
By this [path] indeed the Aṅgirases went to the heavenly world;
along that path he leads them.
With two [verses] he offers in the Gārhapatya;
with that he steps upon this [earth].
In the Āgnīdhra with a verse to Agni;
with that, the intermediate space.
Then, when sacrificial gifts are given,
with that, the heavenly world.
He gives first to the Āgnīdhra;
the Āgnīdhra is the mouth of the sacrifice;
he does not go away from the mouth of the sacrifice.
Moreover, Agni is all deities;
the deities become sought and pleased for him by it.
He gives to the Brahman;
the Brahman belongs to Prajāpati;
he pleases Prajāpati with it.
He gives to the ṛtvijs;
he pleases the offerings with it.
With that which he gives to the sadasyas, he buys back his Soma-drinking.
With that which he gives to one descended from a ṛṣi and learned, he pleases the metres;
for they do not deserve that Soma-drinking should be bought back from him.
With that which he gives to one descended from a ṛṣi and learned, he prospers in the world of the gods;
with that [which he gives] to one not descended from a ṛṣi [yet] learned, in the world of men;
with that which he gives to one who has not crept [into the Sadas], trees spread;
with that which he gives to one who begs, he enlivens the rival;
with that [which he gives] through fear, the Brahman consumes royal power.
That which he thrusts away is a tigress sacrificial gift;
if he should receive it again, having become a tigress it would crush him;
it must be received together with another [gift];
thus it does not crush him.
When he gives a goat,
the goat belongs to Agni;
by it he reaches Agni's dear domain.
When he gives a sheep,
by it he wins cattle-wealth.
When he gives a cow,
the cow belongs to the Viśve Devas;
by it he reaches the dear domain of the Viśve Devas.
When he gives a garment,
a garment belongs to all deities;
by it the deities become sought and pleased for him.
When he gives prepared food,
by it he redeems flesh.
When a cart or a chariot,
by it the body.
When he gives gold,
by it he makes his life-span longer.
When he gives a horse,
the horse belongs to Prajāpati;
by it he reaches Prajāpati's dear domain;
moreover, he conquers the world of yonder Āditya.
At the end [a gift] must be given to the Pratihartṛ;
the Pratihartṛ belongs to Rudra;
if he should give to the Pratihartṛ in the middle, he would lead Rudra into the middle;
therefore [a gift] must be given to the Pratihartṛ at the end.
Svarbhānu the Āsura pierced the sun with darkness;
Atri perceived it.
When he gives gold to a descendant of Atri,
he smites away darkness;
moreover, he places light above,
for the illumination of the heavenly world.

Viśvarūpa, son of Tvaṣṭṛ, thought that that part of the sacrifice was deficient
when the Ṛc and the Sāman are shifted apart.
He saw these [formulas],
for the repair of the sacrifice.
Whatever of the sacrifice is deficient here, these are offered for its repair.
There are six containing verses;
six, indeed, are the seasons;
he firmly stands in the seasons.
There are three yajus formulas;
three, indeed, are the pressings;
he obtains the pressings.
He offers these nine samiṣṭayajus formulas.
They praise with nine [verses] at the Bahiṣpavamāna;
that makes nine.
Nine are the life-breaths;
he places the life-breaths in himself.
Standing upright, he offers;
for standing upright, he is more vigorous.
For him whom he hates, he should offer standing silently;
he cuts off his life-breaths.
He offers continuously,
for the continuity of the life-breaths.
He offers without interruption,
so that the life-breaths are not interrupted.
If he were to interrupt, he would interrupt the life-breaths.
For him whom he hates, he should interrupt;
he interrupts his life-breaths.
With the same [breath] he offers dividing [the formulas];
for these life-breaths are equal.
These, indeed, are the linchpins of the sacrifice.
Viśvāmitra saw these two linchpins of the sacrifice;
therefore these are made the linchpins of the sacrifice.
'From where the sacrifice is taken, there it must be deposited again,' said Bharadvāja.
From where the sacrifice is taken, there indeed it is deposited again.

The sacrifice indeed has five pressings:
the three pressings,
the concluding bath, and the anūbandhyā [cow] is the fifth of the pressings.
The sacrifice is fivefold;
as great as the sacrifice is, he seizes it.
Whatever exceeds the sacrifice, Varuṇa seizes that.
Exceeding the sacrifice is the pressed-out Soma sediment;
the pressing-board and the [other] pressing-board.
Therefore they go down to the concluding bath with it.
They go down to water;
for water is Varuṇa.
They go down to standing [water];
for that is visibly Varuṇa.
If they were to go down to flowing [water], they would carry away the cattle;
and when they go down to standing [water],
[it is] for the non-carrying-away of the cattle.
Water indeed is Varuṇa;
wind is Varuṇa.
If they were to go down to the concluding bath northward, Varuṇa would seize the offspring facing him.
And when they go down to the concluding bath southward,
[it is] for the sacrificer's freedom from Varuṇa.
'King Varuṇa has made a broad [path],' they say as they proceed from the cātvāla.
This indeed is the unobstructed, safe path by which yonder sun goes;
'for the path of yonder sun,' [he means].
'A hundred healers are yours, O king, a thousand,' they say, looking away at the water.
They adorn the water;
purified, they walk with these purified [waters].
There are [cakes] on one potsherd;
man is not to be obtained by potsherds;
he obtains him in a single way.
Moreover, this [earth] is one;
he firmly stands on this [earth].
Having thrown a blade of grass, he offers;
he offers on what has fire, what has an abode.
The Adhvaryu would become blind if he were to offer on what lacks an abode.
He offers the fore-offerings without the sacrificial grass;
the sacrificial grass is offspring;
he releases the offspring from death.
He offers the two butter-portions,
for the nature of the sacrifice.
He offers to Varuṇa,
for freedom from Varuṇa.
He offers to Agni and Varuṇa;
he frees him from Varuṇa on both sides.
He does not offer the after-offerings, nor do they bring [it] to completion;
for they consider that to be superfluous.
'In the ocean is your heart, within the waters,' he stirs [it] together with the ladle;
whatever cruel act is done here, he appeases it.
'O concluding bath, splash down,' [he says];
this is his concluding bath.
Then what pressed-out Soma sediment floats up, they consume that;
for they consider that to be like the drinking of Soma.
The pressed-out Soma sediment is Varuṇa.
If he were to consume [it], he would smear Varuṇa onto himself.
Regarding that, they say:
'It must be consumed;'
the pressed-out Soma sediment is cattle;
'he smears cattle onto himself.'
'Loosened is Varuṇa's noose,' [he says];
he loosens Varuṇa's noose.
'Cast back is Varuṇa's noose,' [he says];
he casts back Varuṇa's noose.
'Homage to Varuṇa's noose,' [he says];
he has paid homage to Varuṇa's noose.
When the Aṅgirases were rising up, the demons rose up after [them].
Bṛhaspati followed after them with this;
with it, he drove off the demons for them.
When he follows after with this,
[it is] for driving off the demons.
Because it has an upright stobha,
it thereby belongs to Bṛhaspati.
And because it has three stobhas:
three, indeed, are these worlds;
from these worlds, from the sacrifice, he drives off the demons.
Because [it is] in an atichandas metre:
all the metres are indeed the atichandas;
with all the metres he drives off the demons from the sacrifice.
Because [it is] addressed to Agni:
Agni indeed is all the deities;
with all the deities he drives off the demons from the sacrifice.
They go down to the water;
waters indeed are demon-slayers;
the demons do not cross the waters;
[it is] for the driving off of the demons.
Without looking back, they come,
so that Varuṇa does not follow after.
They wipe themselves outside the cowpen;
outside the cowpen they apportion Varuṇa away.
'You are fuel, may we prosper,' [he says];
having become free from Varuṇa, they approach in order to prosper.
'You are a kindling stick, may we be kindled,' [he says];
for being kindled.

He offers with the Ṛc, he conducts with the Yajus,
he causes to go with the cup.
The Ṛc is for the stotra, the Yajus for the śastra.
Connected indeed are the stotra and the śastra;
connected are the Ṛc and the Yajus.
With the Ṛc it is performed loudly, with the Sāman loudly, with the Yajus in a low voice.
The gods thought:
'This part of the sacrifice is slack.'
They added the puroruc for non-slackness.
All deities expect, when a cup is being drawn:
'He is drawing for me, he is drawing for me.'
As for the puroruc,
[it is] for the distinction of the deities.
'Living are ye by name,
as such, make this one live;
Means of living are ye by name,
as such, make this one live;
Reviving are ye by name,
as such, revive this one.'
Having inserted twenty-one barley-grains and darbhapiñjūla stalks, he should address [them] thus.
Then he should consecrate [him with the formula]:
'May the Upāṃśu and the Antaryāma [cups] protect thine in-breath and out-breath;
may the Upāṃśusavana protect thy diffused breath;
may the Aindravāyava protect thy speech;
may the Maitrāvaruṇa protect thy ability and resolve;
may the Āśvina protect thy hearing;
may the Śukra and Manthin [cups] protect thine eyes;
may the Āgrāyaṇa protect thy self;
may the Ukthya protect thy limbs;
may the Dhruva protect thy life-span;
may the two season vessels protect thy breasts;
may the droṇakalaśa protect thy head;
may the two kalasa vessels protect thy flanks;
O Lord of nourishment, give back to him eye for eye, breath for breath, speech for speech, life-breath for life-breath; svāhā!'
Thus he should offer.
This indeed Vāsiṣṭha Sātyahavya used to say:
'I know that brāhmaṇa explanation regarding the vessels.
Just as a man is brought an offering once, only once should he do it, not before that.
If he should do it again, he would die.'
If he should practice sorcery, having uttered this very yajus, he should then consecrate [him]
again in the opposite way.

Some vessels are employed again, others are not.
Those that are employed again are for this world;
those that are not employed again are for yonder dwelling.
Now this ukthya vessel is employed again.
It corresponds indeed to wild animals;
by this means he holds the wild animals.
Because this is employed again,
therefore they kill these [wild animals] while facing them, having surrounded them.
Now this season vessel is employed again.
It corresponds indeed to the horse;
by this means he holds the horse.
Because this is employed again,
therefore the hoof of this horse is beneath [it], as it were.
Now this Āditya vessel is employed again;
it corresponds indeed to the cow;
by this means he holds the cow.
Because the most Soma is drawn with the most verses,
therefore the cow, though being slaughtered and eaten, is the most numerous of domestic animals.
Now this Śukra vessel is employed again;
it corresponds indeed to man;
by this means he holds man.
Yonder sun is Śukra;
the rays are the seasons;
inner is the season vessel.
The Śukra vessel is employed again,
for the rays are inside from yonder sun.
Now this Upāṃśu vessel is employed again;
it corresponds indeed to the sheep;
by this means he holds the sheep.
Now this Antaryāma vessel is employed again;
it corresponds indeed to the goat;
by this means he holds the goat.
The Āgrāyaṇa is Prajāpati;
because this is employed again,
therefore Prajāpati knows [his] creatures.
The droṇakalaśa is this vessel of Prajāpati;
because this is employed again,
therefore this dwelling exists.
Seven vessels indeed are worthy of repeated employment,
for they have connections.
Seven metres;
the metres are speech;
as great as speech is, that he attains.
Seven metres,
seven Hotrās,
seven domestic animals:
these he obtains.
Three vessels indeed have direct connections;
everything else has indirect connections.
The Aindravāyava is the Gāyatrī,
the Śukra is the Triṣṭubh,
the Āgrāyaṇa is the Jagatī.
He should cause it to stream without interruption;
his wish becomes fulfilled;
and Parjanya becomes one who rains without clouds.
He should not lead [the cows] toward the cup.
If he were to lead [them] toward the cup, the offspring would die in the womb.
They should glide back by the very way they glide forward at first.
If they were to glide by another way, the embryos would be liable to slip out.
By the sacrificial post indeed the oblations go to the heavenly world;
the dakṣiṇās [go] by the Adhvaryu.
The sacrificial post is erected upright; standing upright he responds,
for the attainment of the heavenly world.

Homage to Speech!
Homage to the Lord of Speech!
Homage to that Speech which is uttered, and which is not uttered!
Homage to the Ṛṣis, the mantra-makers, the mantra-knowers, the mantra-lords!
Let not the Ṛṣis, the mantra-makers, the mantra-knowers, forsake me!
May I utter divine Speech, pleasing to the gods, possessing svadhā for the Fathers, assented to by men;
let the gods aid that [speech] of mine for splendor;
let the Fathers applaud!
I enter upon the Gāyatrī meter;
I enter upon the Triṣṭubh meter;
I enter upon the Jagatī meter;
I enter upon the Anuṣṭubh meter;
I enter upon the Paṅkti meter;
I enter upon the meters as meter.
May they aid us;
may they protect us;
may she reach him who hates us and whom we hate.
O Brahman, we shall proceed with the Pravargya;
O Hotṛ, praise the Gharma;
O Āgnīdhra, set on the fire the two Rajanarohiṇa cakes;
O Pratiprasthātṛ, distribute [the fires];
O Prastotṛ, sing the Sāmans!
Joined with the Yajus, accompanied by the Sāmans, shaped by the Ṛc, brought together by all inspired thoughts, with sacrificial gifts,
extended, conveying across, let the stobhas, being well-disposed, carry [it].
Bhūr, Bhuvaḥ, Svar!
At the impulse of the god Savitṛ, impelled by Bṛhaspati.
Om! Possessed of Indra, proceed!

Ten [regions] to the east, ten to the south thou shinest;
ten to the west, ten to the north thou shinest;
ten upward thou shinest, being well-minded.
As such, protect our offspring and livestock, without malice.
Let Agni with the Vasus make thee shine from the east;
shining thus, make me shine.
Let the Fathers having Yama as king, with the Fathers, make thee shine from the south;
shining thus, make me shine.
Let Savitṛ with the Ādityas make thee shine from the west;
shining thus, make me shine.
Let Mitra and Varuṇa with the Maruts make thee shine from the north;
shining thus, make me shine.
Let Bṛhaspati with all the gods make thee shine from above;
shining thus, make me shine.
O divine Gharma, shining art thou among the gods;
make me shining well among gods and men;
place radiance in me.
Thou art radiance;
thou art the radiant one.
As thou shinest with radiance, so may I shine with radiance;
may the radiances of me as I shine be like thine.
Thou art speed;
thou art the speedy one.
As thou speedest with speed, so may I speed with speed;
may the speeds of me as I speed be like thine.
Thou art splendor;
thou art the splendid one.
As thou splendorest with splendor, so may I splendor with splendor;
may the splendors of me as I splendor be like thine.

Thou art the light of the cattle distributed among the gods;
thou art the light, the sheen.
The sap of the trees, of the waters, of the plants;
thee, who art spirited, I pour down into the spirited one;
upward is the mind, winning heaven.
Thou art milk;
may I be full of milk.
O Gharma, what heat of thine is in heaven, what is in heaven, what is in the high [region], what is in thunder, what is in the Jagatī meter—that [heat] of thine I avert with this [offering];
to that of thine, svāhā!
O Gharma, what heat of thine is in the intermediate space, what is in the intermediate space, what is in the wind, what is in the Vāmadevya [sāman], what is in the Triṣṭubh meter—that [heat] of thine I avert with this [offering];
to that of thine, svāhā!
O Gharma, what heat of thine is in the earth, what is in the earth, what is in fire, what is in the Rathantara [sāman], what is in the Gāyatrī meter—that [heat] of thine I avert with this [offering];
to that of thine, svāhā!
O Prastotṛ, sing the sāman!
"Along with us today"—this is [the verse].
To protect the priesthood from afar, protect the body of princely power!
By the ordinance of the clan, we stride around thee for newer welfare.
O Prastotṛ, sing the sāman!
"Along with us today"—this is [the verse].
To protect the breath from afar, protect the body of sight!
By the ordinance of hearing, we stride around thee for newer welfare.
O Prastotṛ, sing the sāman!
"Along with us today"—this is [the verse].
To protect heaven from afar, protect the body of the intermediate space!
By the ordinance of the earth, we stride around thee for newer welfare.
Four-cornered, the navel of truth, the seat, the Gharma, containing all life-spans, protection extensive;
we have kept off hatred, kept off the crookedness of him whose vow is alien.
Fair art thou, bestowing welfare and blessing;
a child suckling the folk.
Bring both welfare,
and carry [us] around.
O Gharma, this is thy food, this thy debris;
by that grow and swell;
and may we grow and may we swell!
Confounded be he who hates us and whom we hate!
